 {
  "title": "Evaluation of transformation products from chemical oxidation of micropollutants in wastewater by photoassisted generation of sulfate radicals",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chemosphere.2019.03.152",
  "source": "Chemosphere",
  "journal": "Chemosphere",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-07-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Evaluation of transformation products from chemical oxidation of micropollutants in wastewater by photoassisted generation of sulfate radicals",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "273018ca19867b58",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-09",
  "_ev": "ti:e63ecade67ad1fdb",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Chemosphere）",
  "content": "12 October 2026\n \nAperTO - Archivio Istituzionale Open Access dell'Università di Torino\nOriginal Citation:\nEvaluation of transformation products from chemical oxidation of micropollutants in wastewater\nby photoassisted generation of sulfate radicals\nPublished version:\nDOI:10.1016/j.chemosphere.2019.03.152\nTerms of use:\nOpen Access\n(Article begins on next page)\nAnyone can freely access the full text of works made available as \"Open Access\". Works made available\nunder a Creative Commons license can be used according to the terms and conditions of said license. Use\nof all other works requires consent of the right holder (author or publisher) if not exempted from copyright\nprotection by the applicable law.\nAvailability:\nThis is the author's manuscript\nThis version is available http://hdl.handle.net/2318/1709334 since 2021-03-11T12:19:44Z\n1 \n \nEvaluation of transformation products from chemical oxidation of 1 \nmicropollutants in wastewater by photoassisted generation of sulfate 2 \nradicals 3 \nJorge Rodríguez-Chuecaa, b, Carmen Garcia-Cañibanoa, Marco Sarroc, Ángel Encinase, 4 \nClaudio Medanad, Debora Fabbric, Paola Calzac, Javier Marugána,* 5 \na Department of Chemical and Environmental Technology (ESCET), Universidad Rey 6 \nJuan Carlos, C/ Tulipán s/n, 28933 Móstoles, Madrid, Spain. 7 \nb Department of Industrial Chemical & Environmental Engineering, Escuela Técnica 8 \nSuperior de Ingenieros Industriales, Universidad Politécnica de Madrid, C/ José Gutiérrez 9 \nAbascal 2, 28006, Madrid, Spain. 10 \nc Department of Chemistry, University of Torino, via P. Giuria 5, 10125, Torino, Italy 11 \nd Department of Molecular Biotechnology and Health Sciences, University of Torino, via 12 \nNizza 52, 10125, Torino, Italy 13 \ne Department of Innovation & Technology, FCC Aqualia, S.A., C/ Montesinos 28, 06002, 14 \nBadajoz, Spain. 15 \n 16 \n 17 \n 18 \n*Author to whom correspondence should be addressed: 19 \nTel: +34 916 64 7466 20 \njavier.marugan@urjc.es 21 \n2 \n \nABSTRACT 22 \nIn this research, the degradation of seven different micropollutants (MPs) and the 23 \nformation of their transformation products (TPs) have been assessed during the 24 \napplication of different advanced oxidation processes: photolytic and photocatalytic 25 \nactivation of peroxymonosulfate (PMS) and persulfate (PS). The results were compared 26 \nwith those obtained from the photolytic experiments using hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as 27 \noxidant. A significant abatement of almost all MPs was achieved , even with very low 28 \nUV-C contact time (9 and 28 s). The degradation of atenolol (ATN) and caffeine (CFN) 29 \nranged from 84 to 100% with a dose of 0.5 mM of any oxidant . The efficiencies for 30 \nbisphenol-A (BPA), carbamazepine (CBZ), diclofenac (DCF), ibuprofen (IBP) , and 31 \nsulfamethoxazole (SMX) varied depending on the oxidation system and operating 32 \nconditions (oxidant dose and UV -C contact time), leading to the photolysis of PMS to 33 \nhigher efficiencies than PS and H2O2. In all cases, the abatement of MPs ranged from 63 34 \nto 83%, even with the lowest PMS dosage. Moreover, the addition of Fe(II) as a catalyst 35 \nenhanced the removal efficiency, reaching almost total removal, especially over CBZ, 36 \nDCF, and IBP. The Dissolved Organic Carbon (DOC) removal ranged between 44 and 37 \n62%, suggesting the transformation of MPs in intermediate compounds. The 38 \nidentification of transformation products was carried out for each micropollutant and each 39 \noxidation treatment, being observed some transformation products specific of oxidation 40 \nby sulfate radicals. For example, m/z 165.0432 only appeared after PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C on 41 \nthe degradation of BFA, m/z 251.082 appeared after photolytic activation of PMS and PS 42 \non CBZ removal, and m/z 128.0452 was observed after any sulfate radical oxidation 43 \ntreatment, but not after photolysis of H2O2. 44 \nKeywords: sulfate radicals; micropollutants; transformation products; mechanisms; UV-45 \nC radiation. 46 \n3 \n \n1. Introduction 47 \nReports of micropollutants (MPs) occurrence in water bodies have increased in recent 48 \nyears. MPs pollution is the result of human activities , such as industrial, hospital, 49 \nveterinary, domestic, etc. The real effects that these MPs could exert on ecosystems and 50 \nhumans are currently undetermined , although some researchers assert that they could 51 \nnegatively affect aquatic ecosystems , biological processes of organisms , and overall 52 \nhuman health ( Boxall et al. , 2012; Schwarzenbach et al., 2006 ). Due to the chemical 53 \nstability and low biodegradability of most MPs, conventional wastewater treatment plants 54 \n(WWTP) are unable to achieve their complete removal (Collado et al., 2014; Casas & 55 \nBester, 2015; Gruchlik et al., 2018). For these reasons, although the vast majority of these 56 \ncompounds are not yet legislatively regulated, monitoring them is strongly recommended 57 \n(i.e., in the European Union through 2013/39/EU) . Therefore, wastewater reuse could 58 \nconstitute an important route for the release of MPs to the environment, and if MPs reach 59 \nwater bodies or water supplies (catchment of fresh water, or reuse of treated wastewater), 60 \nthey could present a serious concern due to their unknown consequences (Badia-Fabregat 61 \net al., 2015). 62 \nSulfate Radical-based A dvanced Oxidation Processes ( SR-AOPs) are becoming 63 \nemerging alternatives to the widely reported hydroxyl radicals-based AOPs (HR-AOPs). 64 \nAmong the main advantages of SR-AOPs are the low cost of persulfate salts, their lack 65 \nof toxicity, ease of handling and storage, and the low pH dependency of their activity 66 \n(Wacławek et al., 2017). Sulfate radicals could be generated from the activation of 67 \ndifferent persulfate salts, such as potassium peroxymonosulfate (HSO 5-; PMS) and 68 \nsodium or potassium persulfate (S2O82-; PS). Nevertheless, these persulfate anions are not 69 \nefficient by themselves, and they need to be activated by the following routes: i) heat; ii) 70 \nUV radiation; iii) elevated pH; or iv) addition of transition metals . Their high redox 71 \n4 \n \npotential (E0 = 2.5–3.1 V) and reactivity make sulfate radicals, as a strong oxidant species, 72 \nable to degrade any recalcitrant organic substance (Ling et al., 2017; Ahmed et al., 2014; 73 \nPang et al., 2016; Xia et al., 2017; Oncu et al., 2015), and they have been proven efficient 74 \nin the inactivation of microorganisms (Rodríguez-Chueca et al., 2017a, b). 75 \nMonitoring of the transformation products generated during the process is as important 76 \nas the r emoval of MPs present in wastewater. Some authors have investigated the 77 \ndegradation mechanisms of different MPs by sulfate radicals (Pang et al., 2016; Ling et 78 \nal., 2017; Mercado et al., 2018 ). H owever, to the best of our knowledge, no studies 79 \ncurrently exist about degradation mechanisms by SR-AOPs of MPs in real wastewater 80 \neffluents. 81 \nThis study is focused on the evaluation of the efficiency of different homogeneous AOPs 82 \nat the laboratory scale (H2O2/UV-C; PS/UV -C; PS/Fe(II)/UV -C; PMS/UV -C and 83 \nPMS/Fe(II)/UV-C) towards the degradation of detected MPs in real WWTP effluents, 84 \nincluding assessment of their transformation mechanisms and the extent of the 85 \nmineralization process. 86 \n2. Material and methods 87 \n2.1. Wastewater samples 88 \nTreatments were applied over real secondary effluents from Estiviel WWTP, located in 89 \nthe city of Toledo (Spain). Samples were collected in an intermediate bulk container 90 \n(IBC) from tertiary treatment of the WWTP, prior to the UV-C disinfection step but after 91 \na coagulation/flocculation/filtration process. The water was shipped to Universidad Rey 92 \nJuan Carlos facilities (Móstoles, Spain) to be characterized and to be submitted under 93 \nexperimental treatments . Main characteristics are sum marized in Table S1 94 \n(Supplementary material). 95 \n5 \n \n2.2. UV-C photoreactor 96 \nAll of the treatments were performed in continuous mode using flow rates of 1 and 3 97 \nL/min, and at the natural pH of samples without further adjustment (7.17 ± 0.08). These 98 \nflow rate values were selected to provide a UV-C contact time ranging from 9 to 28 s, in 99 \nthe order of the actual values in real wastewater treatment plant s (Rodríguez-Chueca et 100 \nal., 2018a). The feed system consists of a stirred tank with a volume of 1m 3 (enough to 101 \nfeed the reactor for few hours to allow the analysis of the system’s performance under 102 \nsteady state), a centrifugal pump, and a flow meter. The reactor is a 46 cm in length and 103 \n3.60 cm in inner -diameter quartz tube illuminated by a UV -C lamp (Philips TUV PL-L 104 \n95W/4P HO 1CT/25, λmax=254 nm) placed 10 cm above the tube in the axis of a parabolic 105 \nreflector. The total illuminated volume is ca. 470 mL . The UV-C dosages from 19 to 57 106 \nJ/L, calculated from the experimental value of 95.5 W/m 2 measured with a UV -C 107 \nradiometer (Delta OHM, Model HD 2102.2). The assay started when the UV-C radiation 108 \nsystem was switched on, also corresponding to the addition of oxidants (PMS, PS, or HP) 109 \nand the metal activator [ Fe(II)]. After reaching steady state o peration, three replicate 110 \nsamples were collected at 10 minutes of time interval to present results as average data. 111 \n2.3. Chemicals 112 \nDifferent oxidation treatments (SR-AOPs and HR-AOPs) were applied using two oxidant 113 \nconcentration levels (0.5 and 5 mM) based on previous studies (Rodríguez-Chueca et al., 114 \n2018a, 2018b). Photolytic and catalytic activation of peroxymonosulfate (HSO5-, PMS) 115 \nand p ersulfate (S 2O82-; PS) were performed . Potassium peroxymonosulphate 116 \n(2KHSO5·KHSO4·K2SO4) was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, while sodium persulfate 117 \nwas provided by Panreac. For catalytic activation of PMS, iron (II) sulfate heptahydrate 118 \n(FeSO4•7H2O) from Panreac was used, always in the same molar ratio as PMS and PS. 119 \nFinally, H2O2 (30% w/w; HP) was acquired from Panreac, for application of H2O2/UV-C 120 \n6 \n \ntreatments. All of the reagents were used in analytical grade. Deionized water was used 121 \nthroughout the study for analytics. 122 \n2.4. Analytical methods 123 \nPhysicochemical parameters (pH, soluble chemical oxygen demand (CODs), biological 124 \noxygen demand (BOD), total organic carbon (TOC) and nitrite (N -NO2)) were 125 \ndetermined according to Standard Methods (Eaton et al., 2005). Total, inorganic and 126 \norganic carbon (TC, IC and TOC) were determined by a Shimadzu analyzer (TOC -127 \nVSCH). Turbidity was measured employing a turbidimeter (HACH 2100N) and pH was 128 \nmonitored using a selective electrode pH meter HACH HQ30d. COD and NO 2- were 129 \ndetermined by reagents commercial kits specific for their determination, and using a 130 \nmultiparameter spectrophotometer HACH LANGE DR3900. 131 \n2.5. Micropollutants analysis 132 \nSeven target MPs , namely, ibuprofen (IBU), atenolol (ATN), carbamazepine (CBZ), 133 \ndiclofenac (DCF), bisphenol A (BPA), caffeine (CFN) and sulfamethoxazole (SMX), 134 \nwere detected in the studied wastewater samples by the employed analytical method. The 135 \nanalytical methodology developed for the quantification of MPs was based on 136 \nconventional solid phase extraction (SPE) , using as isotopic internal standards to assess 137 \nthe recovery : diclofenac-(acetophenyl ring-13C6) sodium salt hemi (nonahydrate) 138 \n(13C6C8H10Cl2NNaO2·4.5H2O; Sigma-Aldrich), caffeine-13C3 solution ( 13C3C5H10N4O2; 139 \nSigma-Aldrich), and carbamazepine -D10 (C15D10H2N2O; Sigma -Aldrich). The i nternal 140 \nstandard mixture was added to a filtered 1 ,000 mL water sample , and the sample was 141 \nextracted with a Waters Oasis HLB SPE cartridge that was first conditioned with 4 mL 142 \nof methanol and 2x4 mL of water. The cartridge was then washed with 2x4 mL of 5% 143 \nmethanol in water and dried with a vacuum. The extract was eluted first with 12 mL ethyl 144 \n7 \n \nacetate, and then with 12 mL of methanol . The two fraction s were mixed and finally 145 \nconcentrated to 200 L under a stream of nitrogen. Then, eluted samples were analyzed 146 \nby reverse phase liquid chromatography (UPLC) coupled with an LTQ Orbitrap hybrid 147 \nmass spectrometer. 148 \nChromatographic separation, followed by HRMS analysis, was run on a Phenomenex 149 \n(Castel Maggiore, BO, Italy) Luna C18 column, 150 × 2.0 mm, 3 µm particle size, using 150 \nan Ultimate 3000 HPLC instrument ( Thermo Scientific , Milan, Italy). The i njection 151 \nvolume was 20 μL , and the flow rate was 200 μL/min. The following g radient mobile 152 \nphase composition was adopted: 95/5 to 0/95 in 40 min aqueous eluent/acetonitrile. For 153 \npositive ion analysis , the aqueous eluent was 0.05% formic acid , and for negative ion 154 \nanalysis 5 mM ammonium acetate. 155 \nAn LTQ Orbitrap hybrid mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific, Bremen, Germany ) 156 \nequipped with an ESI ion source was used. The LC column effluent was delivered to the 157 \nion source, using nitrogen as both sheath and auxiliary gas. The source voltage was set to 158 \n4.5 kV in positive ion mode and 3.5 kV in negative ion mode . The heated capillary 159 \ntemperature was maintained at 27 0°C. The main tuning parameters adopted for the ESI 160 \nsource were: positive ion mode: 13.00 V for capillary voltage and 55 V for tube lens; and 161 \nnegative ion mode: 1.00 V for capillary voltage and 42 V for tube lens. Full scan spectra 162 \nwere acquired in the range 100-450 m/z. MSn spectra were acquired in the range between 163 \nion trap cut -off and precursor ion m/z values. Mass resolution was set to 30000. Mass 164 \naccuracy of recorded ions ( vs. calculated) was ± 0.001 u (without internal calibration). 165 \nQuantitative determinations were perf ormed on the basis of the validated method 166 \ndescribed in previous work (Medana et al., 2008). 167 \n 168 \n8 \n \n 169 \n3. Results and Discussion 170 \nMicropollutants detected in this research have been previously detected by other authors 171 \nin treated wastewater samples and water bodies (Collado et al., 2014; Luo et al., 2014; 172 \nBen et al., 2014). In general terms, the concentration of the studied MPs are in the same 173 \nrange than those reported in literature, with the exception of CBZ and SMX, with values 174 \nslightly higher than those reported by Collado et al. (2014) and Ben et al. (2018). 175 \nHowever this result may be not surprising taking into account the widespread use and 176 \nhigh refractoriness of these molecules to common wastewater treatments. 177 \n3.1. Photolysis of HP, PMS, and PS 178 \nThe photolysis efficiency of HP, PMS, and PS was investigated on the removal of seven 179 \ntarget MPs detected in real wastewater samples, and the results are shown in Table 1. In 180 \ngeneral, these treatments showed an efficiency significantly higher than UV-C radiation 181 \nalone (Rodríguez-Chueca et al., 2018a; Rodríguez-Chueca et al., 2018b), although some 182 \nexceptions exist, for instance, in the case of IBP for which no degradation was observed 183 \nunder some operating conditions (photolysis of H2O2, and with molar flux lower than 300 184 \nmmol/h of PMS and PS). In this case, just the PMS/UV-C system obtained notabl e 185 \nabatement when the highest dosage o f PMS (5 mM) was used (68 ± 3 % and 100% 186 \ndepending on the UV-C contact time). 187 \nCFN exhibited the highest abatement levels in all experimental conditions . Three 188 \noxidation systems reached total removal of CFN with 5 mM as oxidant dose, although 189 \nefficiency between 84 to 100% was obtained already using 10-fold less concentration of 190 \noxidants. ATN was also totally abated, but in this case , because of the very low initial 191 \nconcentration (220 ± 150 ng/L), it was easier to reach the limit of quantification (LOQ). 192 \n9 \n \nFor the remaining MPs (BPA, CBZ, DCF, and SMX) , efficiencies varied depending on 193 \napplied oxidation system and operating conditions (oxidant dose and UV-C contact time). 194 \nIn all of these cases, the c ombination PMS/UV -C reached higher efficiencies than 195 \nHP/UV-C and PS/UV-C. These differences were especially evident for DCF, CBZ, and 196 \nSMX. Moreover, as expected, the highest abatements were obtained by using the highest 197 \ndose of oxidants (5 mM). However, the use of lower PMS doses (0.5 mM) achieved 198 \nremoval values higher than 90% for DCF and SMX, and in the range from 63 to 83% of 199 \nSMX with the other two oxidants. Finally, IBP was not adequately removed under all of 200 \nthe applied operating conditions in the different photosystems. Indeed, only the PMS/UV-201 \nC system was able to achieve partial removal when a dose of 5 mM was used. 202 \nTwo different mechanisms might be involved in the activation of PS and PMS by UV 203 \nradiation (Wang & Wang, 2018). The first one is the cleavage of the O-O bond by energy 204 \ninput of UV, as shown in Eqs. (1) and (2): 205 \n𝑆2𝑂82−ℎ𝜐→ 2𝑆𝑂4•− (1) 206 \n𝐻𝑆𝑂5−ℎ𝜐→ 2𝑆𝑂4•−+𝐻𝑂• (2) 207 \nThe other PS and PMS activation hypothesis is the production of the electron by the 208 \ninteraction of UV radiation with water molecule, as described in Eqs. (3) to (5) (Wang & 209 \nWang, 2018): 210 \nH2O → H● + HO● (3) 211 \nS2O82− + H● → SO4●− + SO42− + H+ (4) 212 \nHSO5− + H● → SO4●− + H2O (5) 213 \nFor the second mechanism, the propagation of radicals was limited due to the extremely 214 \nshort penetration depth of UV-254 nm (Wang & Wang, 2018). 215 \n10 \n \nThe application of photolytic activation of PMS and PS to remove organic MPs is widely 216 \nreported in the literature (Gao et al., 2012; Tan et al., 2013; Mahdiahmed & Chiron, 2014; 217 \nAo & Liu, 2017; Yang et al., 2017). Nevertheless, to the best of our knowledge, no 218 \nreferences have yet applied sulfate radicals to different MPs at the same time, with just 219 \nseconds of UV-C contact time and with very low dosages of PMS and PS. For instance, 220 \nSharma et al. (2015) reported almost total abatement of BPA (96.7%) with an optimum 221 \ndose of PMS of 0.66 mM, which is very close to the dose (0.5 mM) reported here, but 222 \nwith a higher UV-C contact time (60 min) because of the different reactor configuration 223 \n(batch). On the other hand, Khan et al. (2017) reached total removal of lindane after 100 224 \nmin of treatment with 1 mM of PMS/UV-C. Dhaka et al. (2018) compared the efficiency 225 \nof three photolytic systems (HP/UV -C, PS/UV-C, and PMS/UV-C) on the removal of 226 \nethyl paraben. Dhaka et al. ’s (2018) results showed the highest efficiency of PS/UV-C, 227 \nfollowed by HP/UV -C and PMS/UV -C, with the following operating conditions : batch 228 \nreactor, clean water samples, and high UV-C contact time (90 min) . On the other hand, 229 \nTan et al. (2013) reported a higher efficiency of the removal of antipyrine from HP/UV-230 \nC than from the combination PS/UV-C, with an oxidant dose of 0.5 mM (equal to that 231 \nused in this research) and 1 h of reaction time. Furthermore, Ao and Liu (2017) found the 232 \ndegradation of SMX in Milli-Q water samples spiked wi th 23.69 µM of this antibiotic, 233 \nusing photolysis of HP, PS , and PMS. With an oxidant dosage of 1 mM and UV -C 234 \ndosages of 200 mJ/cm 2, the highest SMX removal rates are for the PMS/UV-C system 235 \nfollowed by PS/UV -C and HP/UV -C. Overall, all of these references point out the 236 \nimportance of the results of this study, demonstrating the efficiency of photolytic 237 \nactivation of PMS and PS on the removal, at the same time , of different MPs in a very 238 \ncomplex water matrix (real secondary effluent from WWTP) with low oxidan t dosages 239 \nand very low UV-C contact time (9 and 28 seconds) . However, it is also demonstrated 240 \n11 \n \nthat the concentration of PS or PMS required for the removal of organic compounds 241 \nchanged with the types and initial concentration of organic compounds . This constitutes 242 \na great challenge for the treatment of real wastewater, and the optimal concentration of 243 \nPS or PMS should be investigated when dealing with real wastewater (Wang & Wang, 244 \n2018). 245 \n3.2. Photocatalytic activation of PMS and PS with Fe(II) 246 \nTable 2 shows the results of MPs removal after the use of Fe(II) as an activator of PMS 247 \nand PS in c ombination with UV -C radiation. In general , it can be observed that the 248 \naddition of Fe(II) enhanced the MPs removal. However, because of the high abatement 249 \nobtained in photolytic treatments, improvement by Fe(II) was clearly observed over CBZ, 250 \nDCF, and IBP only. For instance, in the case of CBZ, the PS/Fe(II)/UV-C (96 – 100%) 251 \nsystem notably increased efficiency compared to the PS/UV-C system (47 – 79%). A 252 \nsignificant enhancement was also found by adding Fe(II) in the PS and PMS systems for 253 \nIBP removal. In this case, in the absence of Fe(II), photolytic treatments almost did not 254 \nreduce the concentration of IBP; in the presence of Fe(II), however, total abatement was 255 \nobserved under almost all of the operating conditions. 256 \nFor the other target MPs (ATN, BPA, and SMX), abatement results by combination of 257 \nFe(II) with PMS and PS were quite similar to those obtained in the absence of Fe(II). For 258 \nATN, CFN and SMX, total or almost total abatement was achieved. It is well known that 259 \nthe addition of a transition metal as an activator increases the generation rate of free 260 \nradicals (Wang & Wang, 2018). The activation of both persulfate salts, PS and PMS, by 261 \nmetal ions is as follows: 262 \nS2O82− + Mn →Mn + 1 + SO4●− + SO42− (6) 263 \nHSO5− + Mn →Mn + 1 + SO4●− + OH− (7) 264 \n12 \n \nwhere M = transition metal 265 \nWhile silver ion was proven to be the most efficient for activating PS (Anipsitakis & 266 \nDionysiou, 2004) , for PMS activation, cobalt ion (Co 2+) exhibited the best activated 267 \nperformance (Hu & Long, 2016) . Although Fe(II) is not the best activator, its use is 268 \nstrongly recommended because it is environmentally more friendly than Co(II) or Ni(II), 269 \nnot showing significant differences in comparison with the use of Co(II) (Rodríguez-270 \nChueca et al., 2017 c). Some authors have reported the activation of PMS and PS with 271 \nheterogeneous iron catalyst. For instance, Xia et al. (2017) achieved the successful 272 \nremoval of phenol in surface water and wastewater by PS activated by natural magnetic 273 \npyrrhotite, reaching a removal higher than in treatment without an iron activator, and 274 \nhigher than Fenton -like treatment (H 2O2/natural pyrrhotite) . In these treatments, the 275 \nreaction time was 30 min , and no UV radiation was applied. Different authors have 276 \nreported the activation mechanisms of PS and PMS with transition metal as an electron 277 \nacceptor (Liang et al., 2009; Teel et al., 2011). It is sugge sted that the HO bond in the 278 \nstructure of PMS breaks. Because of the different structure of PS with PMS, PS cannot 279 \nbe effectively activated by metal as an electron acceptor (Liang et al., 2009). 280 \nHowever, as shown in Tables 1 and 2, the combination of PMS and PS with Fe(II) is not 281 \nthe most suitable for treatments with a very low UV-C contact time (maximum 28 s), as 282 \nit is not enough time to observe the rate increase in the generation of sulfate radicals as a 283 \nconsequence of Fe(II) addition. 284 \nFigure 1 compares the efficiency of all of the treatments through the use of the inverse of 285 \nElectrical Energy per Order (1/EEO), defined as the number of kWh of electrical energy 286 \nrequired to reduce the concentration of a pollutant by one order of magnitude (90%) 287 \n(Bolton et al., 2001). The E EO (kWh/m3 order) in continuous mode reactions can be 288 \ncalculated as follows: 289 \n13 \n \n𝐸𝐸𝑂=\n𝑃\n𝐹·log⁡(\n𝐶𝑖\n𝐶𝑓)\n (8) 290 \nwhere P is the rated power (kW) of the AOP system; F is the flow rate (m3/h); and Ci and 291 \nCf are the initial and final pollutant concentrations, respectively (Bolton et al., 2001). 292 \nAs observed in Figure 1, a clear trend does not exist regarding which is the most efficient 293 \ntreatment for all MPs. In general, It seems that the most efficient operating conditions are 294 \nthe use of 5 mM dosages of oxidant and Fe(II) at 3 L/min (9 s UV -C contact time). 295 \nHowever, the best conditions and treatment strongly depend on each target compound , 296 \nand therefore the optimal process should be studied cases by case. 297 \n3.3. Degree of mineralization of organic matter 298 \nIn terms of mineralization, Table 3 shows DOC removal. The highest DOC abatement 299 \nwas shown for PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C, with values ranging from 49 to 62% in all of the tested 300 \noperating conditions, while DOC removal was slightly lower with the PS/Fe(II)/UV-C 301 \nsystem (44 – 54%). 302 \nMoreover, in contrast with that observed in terms of MPs removal, clear differences 303 \nexisted regarding whether or not F e(II) is added, especially in the case of PMS. In the 304 \nabsence of Fe(II), removal efficiency ranged from 17 to 27% using PMS, being slightly 305 \nlower than when PS was used (27 – 54%). 306 \nThese high mineralization rates constitute a promising result. Nevertheless, it is worth 307 \nnoting that treatments were applied over real treated effluents, with other substances 308 \ncompeting with free radicals, such as organic matter (COD = 27 ± 3 mg/L), suspended 309 \nsolids (5 ± 2 mg/L), or faecal microorganisms ( E. coli ≈ 10 4 – 105 CFU/mL). Indeed, 310 \nmineralization values could correspond only to the degradation of other organic 311 \n14 \n \nsubstances present in water, making it necessary to examine the oxidation by -products 312 \ngenerated after these UV -C-based AOPs . Besides, toxicity monitoring should be 313 \nconducted to assess the global effect of these SR-AOPs over the final treated effluent. 314 \n3.4. Transformation products analysis and proposed reaction mechanism 315 \nTPs formed along with MPs photoxidation systems were detected via HPLC–MS. Table 316 \n4 shows the occurrenc e of transformation products in each tested treatment (HP/UV-C; 317 \nPMS/UV-C; PS/UV-C; PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C and PS/Fe(II)/UV-C) and under all operating 318 \nconditions. 319 \nFigure 2 presents the different transformation products detected after the five proposed 320 \noxidation treatments for atenolol degradation (ATN) . The identification of TPs was 321 \nperformed on the basis of the MSn data and in agreement with a previous work (Medana 322 \net al., 2008). The three different TPs (m/z 315.1558, m/z 283.1658, and m/z 134.1173) do 323 \nnot appear in all of the proposed treatments. However, all of them were detected after 324 \nphotolysis of hydrogen peroxide and peroxymonosulfate . The lightest compound (m/z 325 \n134.1173) was detected after all of the treatments . This compound comes f rom the 326 \nmolecule cleavage by the ether bond. For the other treatments, two TPs were identified 327 \nfor each treatment. 328 \nSix different transformation products were detected (see Figure 3) in the degradation of 329 \nbisphenol A (BPA). According to Table 4, four of them (m/z 273.0748, m/z 257.0859 A, 330 \nm/z 149.0605, and m/z 135.0472) were detected in all of the treatments. These two lightest 331 \ncompounds are the consequence of molecule rupture at the level of quaternary carbon 332 \namong the two aromatic rings. It is important to note the occurrence of a transformation 333 \nproduct ( m/z 165.0432), exclusive of the PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C system, formed after the 334 \nbreakage of the BPA and the incorporation of a hydroxyl group . Sharma et al. (2015) 335 \n15 \n \nreported the different transformation products and the degradation mechanisms after the 336 \nUV-C photoactivation of PMS , being only the product with m/z 257 detected in the 337 \npresent study. In recent a recent work on the photocatalytic degradation of BPA with TiO2 338 \nand Ce-ZnO (Fabbri et al, 2019) two of the intermediates here reported ( m/z 273.0748 339 \nand 165.0432) were also identified. 340 \nEleven transformation products were detected after degradation treatment of 341 \ncarbamazepine (CBZ) , as shown in Figure 4. Structural attr ibution was performed in 342 \nagreement with the data present in the literature (Calza et al. , 2012). There were four 343 \nintermediates formed during all of the treatments, and two compounds were exclusively 344 \ndetected during PS/UV-C treatments. 345 \nAfter the application of treatments, caffeine (CFN) was transformed in four different 346 \nintermediate compounds (see Figure 5). One of them (m/z 144.0766) is common to all of 347 \nthe applied treatments and is formed as a consequence of the rupture of the imidazole 348 \nring. Also as a consequence of the rupture of the imidazole ring, but conserving both 349 \namine groups, an intermediate compound ( m/z 201.0985) appeared after the application 350 \nof the PMS/UV-C system. Intermediates at m/z 170.0560 are the only ones that preserved 351 \nthe imidazole ring and involved the rupture in the pyrimidine ring. This compound was 352 \ndetected after photolysis of hydrogen peroxide and peroxymonosulfate. Finally, the 353 \nlightest compound (m/z 128.0452) was specific to persulfate treatments, appearing after 354 \nthe photolysis of PMS and PS, and their activation with Fe(II). 355 \nFigure 6 shows two transformation products identified in the degradation of DCF. Both 356 \nTPs (m/z 232.0563 and m/z 166.0604) were detected after the performance of all of the 357 \ntested oxidation treatments. However, there were no specific intermediates found for any 358 \ntreatment. 359 \n16 \n \nIn the case of IBP, the MS d ata, in agreement with the information available in the 360 \nliterature, allowed us to detect and identify 14 different intermediates (see Figure 7) 361 \n(Michael et al., 2014; Rao et al., 2016; Xiang et al., 2016; Fabbri et al., 2019). As can be 362 \nseen in Table 4, nine out 14 of these intermediates are common to all of the applied 363 \ntreatments. Rao et al. (2016) reported the detected transformation products after the 364 \napplication of PMS activation by Fe(III) and irradiation with UV-C. 365 \nFinally, only one intermediate compound ( m/z 110.0597) was detected after 366 \nsulfamethoxazole (SMX) degradation. This intermediate compound is the cation of o -367 \naminophenol, and is common for all oxidation treatments, both hydroxyl and sulfate 368 \nradicals oxidation (Figure 8). 369 \n4. Conclusions 370 \nFrom the present study on the efficacy and transformation products of different hydroxyl 371 \nand sulfate radical advanced oxidation processes on the degradation of seven MPs 372 \ndetected in real wastewater samples, the following conclusions can be drawn: 373 \n- UV-C-driven SR -AOPs have been proven as a successful technology to reduce 374 \nmicropollutants concentration in treated wastewater effluents. This technology can be 375 \neasily applied in current UV-C reactors to improve the efficiency of the tertiary 376 \ntreatment for MPs removal for regeneration and water reuse purposes. 377 \n- The global efficiency of the MPs degradation is very variable. Some compounds, such 378 \nas ATN and CFN , were easily removed (84 – 100%) with low oxidant dosages (0.5 379 \nmM) and very low UV-C contact time (9 s). In contrast, other MPs such as BPA, CBZ, 380 \nDCF, IBP , and SMX presented a removal efficiency strongly dependant on the 381 \noxidation system and operating conditions (dose and reaction time). Therefore, system 382 \n17 \n \noperation in real process should be optimized depending on the MPs chosen to monitor 383 \nthe process efficiency. 384 \n- As a general trend, the relative order of efficiency of the studied processes is : 385 \nPMS/UV-C > PS/UV -C > H 2O2/UV-C. The addition of Fe(II) as a catalyst clearly 386 \nenhanced the removal of those compounds that show low removal efficiencies by the 387 \ndirect photolysis of oxidants, allowing the almost total degradation of compounds such 388 \nas CBZ, DCF, IBP , and SMX. Only in the case of BPA, similar or even lower 389 \nefficiencies were obtained in the presence of Fe(II). 390 \n- Finally, different transformation products were identified during the degradation of 391 \neach MP depending on the oxidation treatment. The use of sulfate radicals leds to the 392 \ngeneration of specific intermediates that suggest different degradation pathways in 393 \ncomparison with hydroxyl radicals based processes. This could have an impact on the 394 \nevolution of the toxicity during the process that should be carefully analysed in future 395 \nstudies. 396 \nAcknowledgements 397 \nThe authors gratefully acknowledge the financial support of the Spanish State Research 398 \nAgency (AEI), the Spanish Ministry of Economy and Competitiveness (MINECO), the 399 \nSpanish Centre for the Development of Industrial Technology (CDTI) and the Italian 400 \nMIUR in the frame of the collaborative international consortium WATERJPI2013 – 401 \nMOTREM of the Water Challenges for a Changing World Joint Programming Initiative 402 \n(Water JPI) Pilot Call and Comunidad de Madrid through the program REMTAVARES 403 \n(S2013/MAE-2716). Jorge Rodríguez-Chueca also acknowledges the funding provided 404 \nby the Spanish Ministry of Economy and Competitiveness (MINECO) through the Juan 405 \nde la Cierva-formación grant (No. FJCI-2014-20195). 406 \n 407 \n18 \n \nReferences 408 \nAhmed, M.M., Brienza , M., Goetz , V., Chiron , S., 2014. Solar photo -Fenton using 409 \nperoxymonosulfate for organic micropollutants removal from domestic wastewater: 410 \nComparison with