idant] = 0.5 mM; 562 \n[Fe(II)] = 0.5 mM; UV-C contact time 9 s. 563 \nFigure 7. Scheme of TPs formed from ibuprofen (IBP) in the presence of HP/UV-C; 564 \nPMS/UV-C; PS/UV -C; PMS/Fe(II)/UV -C and PS/Fe(II)/UV -C. [Oxidant] = 0.5 mM; 565 \n[Fe(II)] = 0.5 mM; UV-C contact time 9 s. 566 \n26 \n \nFigure 8 . Scheme of TPs formed from sulfamethoxazole (SMX) in the presence of 567 \nHP/UV-C; PMS/UV-C; PS/UV-C; PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C and PS/Fe(II)/UV-C. [Oxidant] = 568 \n0.5 mM; [Fe(II)] = 0.5 mM; UV-C contact time 9 s. 569 \n 570 \n27 \n \nTable Captions 571 \nTable 1. Micropollutants abatement by photolysis of HP, PMS , and PS. Operating 572 \nconditions: [Oxidant] = 0.5 – 5 mM; UV-C contact time = 9 – 28 s. 573 \nTable 2. Micropollutants abatement by Fe(II) activation of PMS and PS. Operating 574 \nconditions: [Oxidant] = [Fe(II)] = 0.5 – 5 mM; UV-C contact time = 9 – 28 s. 575 \nTable 3. DOC removal percentage (mineralization degree) after all applied oxidation 576 \ntreatments. 577 \nTable 4 . Transformation products occurrence (striped boxes) after the application of 578 \ndifferent oxidation treatments. Operating conditions: A) flow rate = 1 L/min; UV -C 579 \ncontact time = 28 s; [Oxidant] = 0.5 mM; [Fe(II)] = 0.5 mM; B) flow rate = 3 L/min; UV-580 \nC contact time = 9 s; [Oxidant] = 0.5 mM; [Fe(II)] = 0.5 mM ; C) flow rate = 1 L/min; 581 \nUV-C contact time = 28 s; [Oxidant] = 5 mM; [Fe(II)] = 5 mM; B) flow rate = 3 L/min; 582 \nUV-C contact time = 9 s; [Oxidant] = 5 mM; [Fe(II)] = 5 mM. 583 \n 584 \n 585 \n28 \n \nTable 1 586 \nMP \nOxidant \nConcentration \n(mM) \nUV-C \ncontact \ntime (s) \nmmolOxidant/h [MP]0 µg/L \nHP/UV-C PMS/UV-C PS/UV-C \n[MP]t µg/L % Removal [MP]t µg/L % Removal [MP]t µg/L % Removal \nATN \n0.5 28 30 \n(22 ± 15)·10-2 \n< LOQ 100 (15 ± 12)·10-2 34 ± 53 < LOQ 100 \n0.5 9 90 (52 ± 59)·10-2 < 0 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \n5 28 300 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 (41 ± 51)·10-2 < 0 \n5 9 900 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \nBPA \n0.5 28 30 \n(30 ± 20)·10-2 \n(33 ± 1)