37 ± 90 (19 ± 27)·10-2 37 ± 90 \n5 9 900 (17 ± 16)·10-2 44 ± 52 (7 ± 3)·10-2 78 ± 9 \nCBZ \n0.5 28 30 \n43 ± 37 \n5 ± 5 88 ± 11 2 ± 3 96 ± 6 \n0.5 9 90 1 ± 1 98 ± 3 1 ± 1 98 ± 2 \n5 28 300 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \n5 9 900 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \nCFN \n0.5 28 30 \n21 ± 28 \n16 ± 3 26 ± 15 94 ± 113 < 0 \n0.5 9 90 < LOQ 100 7 ± 10 67 ± 47 \n5 28 300 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \n5 9 900 13 ± 11 40 ± 50 4 ± 6 80 ± 28 \nDCF \n0.5 28 30 \n(16 ± 14)·10-1 \n< LOQ 100 (2 ± 3)·10-2 99 ± 2 \n0.5 9 90 < LOQ 100 (7 ± 11)·10-1 95 ± 7 \n5 28 300 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \n5 9 900 (4 ± 0)·10-2 97 ± 0 (5 ± 7)·10-2 97 ± 4 \nIBP \n0.5 28 30 \n(10 ± 4)·10-1 \n(5 ± 6)·10-1 54 ± 64 (2 ± 3)·10-2 100 \n0.5 9 90 (1 ± 1)·10-2 99 ± 1 (30 ± 43)·10-2 100 \n5 28 300 < LOQ 100 (19 ± 27)·10-2 99 ± 1 \n5 9 900 < LOQ 100 (7 ± 3)·10-2 98 ± 3 \nSMX \n0.5 28 30 \n91 ± 88 \n14 ± 18 85 ± 20 24 ± 33 74 ± 37 \n0.5 9 90 1 ± 0 99 ± 0 8 ± 2 91 ± 2 \n5 28 300 < LOQ 100 < LOQ 100 \n5 9 900 0 ± 1 100 ± 1 1 ± 0 99 ± 0 \n 589 \n30 \n \nTable 3 590 \nTreatment H2O2/UV-C PMS/UV-C PS/UV-C PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C PS/Fe(II)/UV-C \nDosage UV-C contact time = 28 s \n0.5 mM 37 ± 9 23 ± 2 27 ± 11 49 ± 2 54 ± 5 \n5 mM 45 ± 3 17 ± 1 54 ± 8 62 ± 3 44 ± 2 \n UV-C contact time = 9 s \n0.5 mM 9 ± 3 20 ± 8 47 ± 8 62 ± 7 51 ± 8 \n5 mM 38 ± 5 27 ± 1 51 ± 8 58 ± 1 3 \n 591 \n 592 \n31 \n \nTable 4 593 \nMPs TPs (m/z) HP/UV-C PMS/UV-C PS/UV-C PMS/Fe(II)/UV-C PS/Fe(II)/UV-C A B C D A B C D A B C D A B C D A B C D \nATN \n283.1658 \n315.1558 \n134.1173 \nBFA \n273.0748 \n257.0859 A \n257.0859 B \n165.0432 \n149.0605 \n135.0472 \nCBZ \n271.1031 A \n271.1031 B \n271.1031 C \n269.0926 A \n269.0926 B \n269.0926 C \n267.0770 A \n267.0770 B \n253.0976 A \n253.0976 B \n251.082 \n224.071 \n210.0918 \nCFN \n201.0985 \n170.0560 \n144.0766 \n128.0452 \nDCF 232.0563 166.0504 \nIBF \n253.1060 A \n253.1060 B \n251.0943 A \n251.0943 B \n251.0943 C \n237.1161 A \n237.1161 B \n237.1161 C \n221.1209 A \n221.1209 B \n219.098 \n207.1009 \n191.1060 \n181.0744 \nSMX 110.0597 \n 594 \n 595 \n32 \n \nSupplementary Material 596 \nEvaluation of transformation products from chemical oxidation of 597 \nmicropollutants in wastewater by photoassisted generation of sulfate radicals 598 \nJorge Rodríguez-Chuecaa, b, Carmen Garcia-Cañibanoa, Marco Sarroc, Ángel Encinase, Claudio 599 \nMedanad, Debora Fabbric, Paola Calzac, Javier Marugána,* 600 \na Department of Chemical and Environmental Technology (ESCET), Universidad Rey Juan 601 \nCarlos, C/ Tulipán s/n, 28933 Móstoles, Madrid, Spain. 602 \nb Department of Industrial Chemical & Environmental Engineering, Escuela Técnica Superior 603 \nde Ingenieros Industriales, Universidad Politécnica de Madrid, C/ José Gutiérrez Abascal 2, 604 \n28006, Madrid, Spain. 605 \nc Department of Chemistry, University of Torino, via P. Giuria 5, 10125, Torino, Italy 606 \nd Department of Molecular Biotechnology and Health Sciences, University of Torino, via Nizza 607 \n52, 10125, Torino, Italy 608 \ne Department of Innovation & Technology, FCC Aqualia, S.A., C/ Montesinos 28, 06002, 609 \nBadajoz, Spain. 610 \n 611 \n 612 \n*Author to whom correspondence should be addressed: 613 \nTel: +34 916 64 7466 614 \njavier.marugan@urjc.es 615 \n33 \n \nTable S1. Physicochemical characteristics of the wastewater. 616 \n 617 \nParameter Value \npH 7.17 ± 0.08 \nConductivity (μS/cm) 1,040 ± 83 \nTurbidity (NTU) 4 ± 1 \nSuspended solids (mg/L) 5 ± 2 \nBOD (mg/L) 7 ± 2 \nCOD (mg/L) 27 ± 3 \nDOC (mg/L) 20 ± 8 \n 618",
