  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Science of the Total Environment）",
  "content": "This may be the author’s version of a work that was submitted/accepted\nfor publication in the following source:\nLv, Shaojun, Gong, Daocheng, Ding, Y aozhou, Lin, Y oujing, Wang, Hao,\nDing, Hang, Wu, Gengchen, He, Chunqian, Zhou, Lei, Liu, Shaw Chen,\nRistovski, Zoran, Chen, Duohong, Shao, Min, Zhang, Yuanhang, & Wang,\nBoguang\n(2019)\nElevated levels of glyoxal and methylglyoxal at a remote mountain site in\nsouthern China: Prompt in-situ formation combined with strong regional\ntransport.\nScience of the Total Environment, 672, pp. 869-882.\nThis ﬁle was downloaded from: https://eprints.qut.edu.au/128403/\n© Consult author(s) regarding copyright matters\nThis work is covered by copyright. Unless the document is being made available under a\nCreative Commons Licence, you must assume that re-use is limited to personal use and\nthat permission from the copyright owner must be obtained for all other uses. If the docu-\nment is available under a Creative Commons License (or other speciﬁed license) then refer\nto the Licence for details of permitted re-use. It is a condition of access that users recog-\nnise and abide by the legal requirements associated with these rights. If you believe that\nthis work infringes copyright please provide details by email to qut.copyright@qut.edu.au\nLicense: Creative Commons: Attribution-Noncommercial-No Derivative\nWorks 4.0\nNotice: Please note that this document may not be the Version of Record\n(i.e. published version) of the work. Author manuscript versions (as Sub-\nmitted for peer review or as Accepted for publication after peer review) can\nbe identiﬁed by an absence of publisher branding and/or typeset appear-\nance. If there is any doubt, please refer to the published source.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.04.020\nAccepted Manuscript\nElevated levels of glyoxal and methylglyoxal at a remote\nmountain site in southern China: Prompt in-situ formation\ncombined with strong regional transport\nShaojun Lv, Daocheng Gong, Yaozhou Ding, Youjing Lin, Hao\nWang, Hang Ding, Gengchen Wu, Chunqian He, Lei Zhou,\nShawchen Liu, Zoran Ristovski, Duohong Chen, Min Shao,\nYuanhang Zhang, Boguang Wang\nPII: S0048-9697(19)31532-3\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.04.020\nReference: STOTEN 31727\nTo appear in: Science of the Total Environment\nReceived date: 4 December 2018\nRevised date: 5 March 2019\nAccepted date: 2 April 2019\nPlease cite this article as: S. Lv, D. Gong, Y. Ding, et al., Elevated levels of glyoxal\nand methylglyoxal at a remote mountain site in southern China: Prompt in-situ formation\ncombined with strong regional transport, Science of the Total Environment, https://doi.org/\n10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.04.020\nThis is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As\na service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The\nmanuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before\nit is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may\nbe discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the\njournal pertain.\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n1 \nElevated levels of glyoxal and methylglyoxal at a remote mountain site in southern \nChina: prompt in-situ formation combined with strong regional transport \nShaojun Lv1#, Daocheng Gong1#, Yaozhou Ding1, Youjing Lin1, Hao Wang1,2*, Hang Ding1, Gengchen Wu1, \nChunqian He 1, Lei Zhou 1, Shawchen Liu 1, Zoran Ristovski 2,3, Duohong Chen 4, Min Shao 1, Yuanhang \nZhang5, Boguang Wang1,2* \n1Institute for Environmental and Climate Research, Jinan University, Guangzhou 511443, China \n2JNU–QUT Joint Laboratory for Air Quality Science and Management, Jinan University, Guangzhou 511443, \nChina \n3International Laboratory for Air Quality and Health, Queensland University of Technology, Brisbane, QLD 4001, \nAustralia \n4State Environmental Protection Key Laboratory of Regional Air Quality Monitoring, Guangdong Environmental \nMonitoring Center, Guangzhou 510308, China \n5State Key Joint Laboratory of Environmental Simulation and Pollution Control, College of Enviro nmental \nSciences and Engineering, Peking University, Beijing 100871, China \n \n \n \n# These authors contributed equally to this work \n*Correspondence: Boguang Wang (tbongue@jnu.edu.cn) and Hao Wang (wanghao@jnu.edu.cn) \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n2 \n \nAbstract \nThe dicarbonyls glyoxal (Gly) and methylglyoxal (Mgly) are key tracers for the oxidation of volatile organic \ncompounds (VOCs) in the atmosphere , but their atmospheric chemistry in remote forest environment s are \nnot well understood. A study was carried out during Jul. 31- Nov. 5 of 2016 at the summit of Mt. Tianjing \n(1,690m.a.s.l.), a remote mountaintop site in southern China , to measure the levels of Gly and Mgly and \nexplore their sources and fate. During the study period, the average mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly were \n509±31 pptv and 340±32 pptv, respectively, with the Gly/Mgly ratios averaging 1.8±0.2. Both the \ndycarbonyl concentrations and the Gly/Mgly ratios were significantly higher than those observed in other \nbackground sites. Production yield calculations and meteorological data analysis indicate that high levels of \nGly and Mgly observed at the study site were largely a combined result of rapid in -situ formation and \nregional transport by prevailing winds. On average, in-situ formation from precursors is estimated to account \nfor 67% of the observed Mgly and about 9% of the observed Gly. There were significant changes in Gly and \nMgly mixing ratios among different time periods when air masses from different source regions dominated, \nindicating contribution of regional transport to the observed dicarbo nyl mixing ratios at the study site . \nBiogenic emissions in eastern China and a nthropogenic emissions in the Pearl River Delta region were the \ntwo main sources responsible for the dicarbonyls observed at the site during most of the sampling period, \nbut large-scale biomass burning in central China was also important in the late autumn, as supported by a \nbackward trajectory analysi s of fire spot data and the identification of biomass burning tracers . This study \nprovides insights into the background atmospheric chemistry and the impact of biogenic and anthropogenic \nsources on the dicarbonyls speciation. \nKeywords: glyoxal, methylglyoxal, high-elevation mountain, dicarbonyls, regional transport \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n3 \n1 Introduction \nGlyoxal (CHOCHO, Gly) and methylglyoxal ( CH3COCHO, Mgly), the two smallest bi -functional carbonyl \norganic compounds, are ubiquitous intermediates in the troposphere and p lay an important role in \natmospheric chemistry (Fu et al., 2008 ). They are key tracers for the photochemical oxidations of many \nnatural and anthropogenic volatile organic compounds (VOCs) , thus acting as novel indicators for active \nphotochemistry on global scales (Spaulding et al., 2003 ). Gly and Mgly , being highly reactiv e in the \natmosphere, contribute greatly to the formation of free radicals, ozone (O3), and peroxyacylnitrates (PANs) \nprimarily through photolysis (62~71%) and photo-oxidation with OH and NO3 radicals (11~14%), affecting \nthe oxidation capacity of the atmosphere (Fu et al., 2 008; Munger et al., 1995 ). In addition, several studies \nhave suggested that gas/particle partitioning of dicarbonyls and their oxidation or oligomerization in \naqueous aerosols and clouds could be a significant source of secondary organic aerosols ( SOA) due to their \nhigh water-solubility (Liggio et al., 2005; Lim et al., 2013; Ortiz-Montalvo et al., 2016; Schwier et al., 2010). \nThe dicarbonyls are also important precursors for the formation of oxalic acid , the most abundant species \nfound in organic aerosols (Kawamura and Bikkina, 2016 ; Mkoma and Kawamura, 2013 ). Therefore, \nknowledge about Gly and Mgly in the atmosphere is important for understanding atmospheric characteristics \nand processes such as VOC degradation, oxidative capacity, and SOA formation. \nThe global inventories of Gly and Mgly are estimated to be ~ 45 Tg/yr and 140 Tg/yr, respectively (Fu et al., \n2008). Both Gly and Mgly are emitted directly from fossil fuel combustion (Hays et al., 2002 ), vehicle \nexhausts (Zhang et al., 2016 ), and biomass burning (Zarzana et al., 2017 ; Zarzana et al., 2018 ), but direct \nemissions are considered to be a minor source of Gly and Mgly compared to secondary formations \n(Myriokefalitakis et al., 2008 ). Although in many polluted regions anthropogenic acetylene, ethylene, and \naromatics are considered as major precursors of dicarbonyls (Li et al., 2014 ; Liu et al., 2012 ; Miller et al., \n2016), on a global scale biogenic isoprene is the most important precursor of Gly and Mgly (Fu et al., 2008). \nIsoprene is the most abundant non-methane VOC in the atmosphere, contributing more than 40% of the total \natmospheric VOC flux (Guenther et al., 2012 ). Acetone, which mainly comes from primary emissions of \nvegetation and secondary formation from oxidation of monoterpenes and methylbutenol, ranks as the second \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n4 \nmost important precursor of Mgly (Jacob et al., 2002 ). However, recent satellite observations and modeling \nstudies have shown that, in air pollution hot-spot regions such as southern and eastern China, there appear to \nbe some missing sources for tropospheric Gly (Liu et al., 2012; Miller et al., 2016). This suggests a need for \nmore ground-based measurements of the dicarbonyls and their precursors in order to establish a dicarbonyl \nprecursor relationship in highly polluted regions. \nMost g round-based studies of ambient Gly and Mgly in recent years have been conducted in urban , \nsuburban and rural atmospheres (Dai et al., 2012; Ho et al., 2014; Li et al., 2013b; Li et al., 2014; Lui et al., \n2017; Spaulding et al., 2003 ), and there is only limited knowledge about their concentrations and \ncharacteristics in the atmospheres of background areas such as high -altitude mountains (Kawamura et al., \n2013; Munger et al., 1995 ) and remote forests (Ieda et al., 2006 ; MacDonald et al., 2012 ; Matsunaga et al., \n2004). Recent studies have shown that in remote forests the atmospheric oxidative capacity and the \ndegradation of isoprene would be amplified under the influence of anthropogenic emissions (Liu et al., 2018; \nSantos et al., 2018 ). As forests are the largest source of biogenic isoprene and high mountains frequently \nreceive polluted airs through regional transport, the forested mountaintop sites offer a desirable locale for \nobserving the interaction of anthropogenic and natural emission sources (Okamoto and Tanimoto, 2016) and \nobtaining valuable data and information for better understanding regional atmospheric oxidizing capacity \nand the formation of photochemical pollution (Li et al., 2013b; MacDonald et al., 2012). \nThe Nanling Mountains in southern China is a major mountain range , which serves as the dividing line \nbetween subtropical and temperate zones (Siu et al., 2005 ). Influenced by the East Asian monsoons, the \nforest-covered mountain range is the key pathway for the long-range transport of air pollutants between \nsouthern and central -eastern China, making it a good location for background air quality monitoring in the \nregion. To the south of the Nanling Mountains is the Pearl River Delta (PRD) , one of the most urbanize d \nregions in China. Over the last several decades, with rapid urbanization and industrialization the region has \nexperienced serious air pollution problems (Chan and Yao, 2008 ). A number of studies have reported \nworsening photochemical pollution with strong atmospheric oxidative capacity (Hofzumahaus et al., 2009 ) \nand frequent occurrence of surface O3 pollution (Wang et al., 2009 ) throughout the region . In particular, \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n5 \nrecent satellite observations have suggested that the PRD is one of the Gly “hot-spots” (Miller et al., 2016 ) \nin the world, likely due to the large photochemical turnover and aromatics emission in the region. Although \na few ground-based studies have detected high levels of dicarbonyls in the PRD (Li et al., 2013b; Lui et al., \n2017), there have been no surface observations of these compounds in this photochemically active region. \nDirect measurement of dicarbonyls in representative areas, such as the Nanling Mountains is important for \nunderstanding the complex photochemical processes and reducing the severe air pollution in the PRD \nregion. \nTo improve our understanding of dicarbonyls in the background atmosphere of southern China and their \nsources and fate, we carried out an observational study for the first time at a forested mountaintop site in the \nNangling Mountains. In this paper, we first discuss the meteorological conditions of the Nanling area and \npresent the characteristics of measured mixing rat ios of Gly and Mgly . We then show the results of \ncalculations of in-situ Gly and Mgly formation from the observed VOC precursors . Finally, we discuss the \nimpact of regional transport of air pollutants from biogenic and anthropogenic source s on the \nphotochemistry of the Nanling atmosphere. \n2 Experiments and methods \n2.1 Sampling site and sampling periods \nThe sampling site (24°41′56″N, 112°53′56″E, 1,690 m a.s.l.) is located on the top of Mt . Tianjing of the \nNanling Mountains in southern China and is about 200 km north of the PRD . It is one of the sixteen \nNational Ambient Air Background Stations in China (Zhang et al., 2018) . This mountaintop site, usually in \nthe free troposphere or upper planetary boundary layer , is surrounded by pristine subtropical forests with a \nvegetation coverage > 95%. Influenced by the East Asian monsoon, th e site receives warm humid air flows \nfrom the South China Sea during the summer and cold dry air parcels from central-eastern China during the \nwinter. A detailed description of the site can be found in Gong et al. (2018). \nAs the East Asian monsoon is divided into the warm and wet summer monsoon (Apr. to Sept.) and cold and \ndry winter monsoon (Oct. to Mar.), the sampling of the study was conducted during both the wet season (Jul. \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n6 \n31  Aug. 5) and dry season (Oct. 9  Oct. 15, Oct. 25  Nov. 5), for a total of 22 days , in 2016. During \neach of the sampling days samples were collected six times with each lasting for 3 h ours (05:0008:00, \n09:0012:00, 12:00 15:00, 15:00 18:00, 18:00 21:00, 22:00 01:00 LT). The sunrise and sunset were \naround 06:00 and 19:00 in the wet season and 06:30 and 18:00 in the dry season, respectively. \n2.2 Sampling and analytical methods \n2.2.1 Dicarbonyls \nDicarbonyls were collected with pentafluorophenyl hydrazine (PFPH) cartridges and analyzed by Gas \nChromatography-Mass Spectrometry (GC-MS) (Ho and Yu, 2004 ; Pang et al., 2011 ). The sampler was \nplaced inside a two-story building, with the sampling tube inlet located 1.5 m above the rooftop. Ambient air \nwas drawn at a flow rate of 100 m l/min through a 2 -m perfluoroalkoxy tube (OD 1/4 inch). An air sample \nwas collected with a sampling pump ( FCG-5, China) into a sampling tube that had been filled with Tenax \nTA (60–80 mesh, ANPEL Corporation, China ) and impregnated with PFPH ( GC grade >98.0%, ANPEL \nCorporation, China). The C=O groups of the target carbonyls reacted with PFPH to form therm ally stable \nderivatives. In order to avoid the interference of O 3, a 1 -m long helical copper tube (ID=0.25 cm) coated \nwith saturated potassium iodide (KI) was placed in front of each sampling tube. A total of 209 samples were \ncollected during the field campaign. The sampled tubes were stored at the ambient temperatures (~25 °C) for \n3 days and returned to the laboratory for analysis in one week. \nThe sampled tubes were eluted with 4 .0-mL of hexane , and the hexane extracts were then concentrated to \n1.0 mL with a Termovap Sample Concentrator (DC-12, ANPEL Corporation, China ). Analysis of PFPH \nderivatives was performed on a gas chromatography -mass spectrometry detection system (7820A GC / \n5977E MSD, Agilent Technologies Inc., USA ) equipped with an HP-5MSI column (30 m × 250 μm × 0.25 \nμm, Agilent Technologies Inc., USA). The initial temperature of the GC oven was set at 72 °C and \nmaintained for 1 min ; the temperature was again increased to 110 °C at a rate of 8 °C min -1and then to \n175 °C at a rate of 4.5 °C min-1; after the temperature was kept at 175 °C for 2 min, it was then increased to \n200 °C at a rate of 2.5 °C min-1 and held for 8 min. The inlet temperature was 275 °C and the GC/MS \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n7 \ntransfer line temperature was 290 °C. The memory effect of the dicarbonyl compounds and their \ndecomposition in the injector were negligible. Solvent delay was set at 5 min for MS detector to avoid \ndamage to MS detector due to the high level of PFPH in solvent. The mass spectrometer was operated in a \nscan mode with a mass range of 50450 Da. \n2.2.2 VOC Precursors \nVOC precursors were collected with a double-channel cryogenic pre -concentrator (TH-PKU 300B, Wuhan \nTianhong Instrument Co. Ltd., China) and analyzed using a gas chromatography-mass spectrometry/flame \nionization detection system (7820A GC, 5977E MSD, Agilent Technologies Inc., USA) . A PLOT \n(Al2O3/KCl) column (20 m × 320 μm × 3.0 μm, Dikma Technologies Inc. , USA) connected to an FID was \nused to quantify C 2–C5 hydrocarbons. A DB–624 column (60 m × 250 μm × 1.4 μm, Agilent Technologies \nInc., USA ) connected to an MS was used to analyze other VOC species (e.g. C 5–C12 hydrocarbons, \noxygenated VOCs, and halocarbons ). The carrier gas es for the two columns were ultra-pure helium \n(>99.999%). The initial temperature of the GC oven was set at 40 °C and maintained for 5 min, and the \ntemperature was then increased to 180 °C at a rate of 6 °C min-1 and held at the final temperature of 180 °C \nfor 4 min. The system was calibrated at 7 concentration levels in the range of 1002000 pptv, and R2 values \nfor calibration curves were >0.99 for all species. The method detection limits (MDL) for target VOCs \nranged from 2 to 50 pptv with a precision of <5% for all compounds. \n2.2.3 Measurement of other relevant data \nO3 was monitored with a commercial UV photometric instrument ( Model 49i, Thermo Scientific, Inc.) . \nPM2.5 was measured by tapered element oscillating microbalance (TEOM 1405, Thermo Fisher Scientific \nInc., MA, US). Nitrogen dioxide (NO2) was monitored with a chemiluminescence analyzer equipped with an \ninternal MoO catalytic converter (Model 42i-TL, Thermo Scientific, Inc. ). It should be noted that the NO 2 \nmeasured with MoO conversion may significantly overestimate NO2 and the observed NO 2 actually \nrepresented a major fraction of NO y (Xu et al., 2013 ). Therefore, the observed NO 2 in this study was \nexpressed as NO2*, which includes NO2 and other oxidized nitrogen compounds. Meteorological parameters, \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n8 \nincluding temperature, relative humidity (RH), wind speed, wind direction and precipitation, were measured \nusing an integrated sensor suite (WXT520, Vaisala, Inc., Finland). Solar radiation s (e.g. UVa and UVb) \nwere monitored during the second half of the field campaign with Kipp & Zonen UVS series. In addition, \nthree-hourly and one-degree cloud cover data were obtained from the Real-time Environmental Applications \nand Display sYstem (https://ready.arl.noaa.gov/READYamet.php, last access: 8 Aug 2018). \n2.2.4 Quality assurance and quality control (QA/QC) \nBefore use, sampling tubes were pre-baked at 450 °C for 4 h, and Tenax TA was placed in a muffle furnace \nat 275 °C for 8 h to remove impurities. To avoid the interference from the ubiquitous carbo nyls in the \nlaboratory air, the sample pretreatment was carried out in a glove box filled with ultra -pure nitrogen \n(>99.999%). To avoid the contamination during transport, the sampling tubes were stored in a clean \nrefrigerator at < 4 °C. The flow rates of air samplers were calibrated before and after each sample collection \nusing a flow meter. During the field campaign, breakthrough tests were conducted by connecting two tubes \nin series to collect ambient samples. At a flow rate of 100 mL min -1, collection efficiencies >82% and >92% \nwere achieved for Gly and Mgly, respectively. Two parallel samples were collected daily, and the relative \nstandard deviation (RSD) of the parallel samples are within 10%. One field blank and one laboratory blank \nwere collected every two days during the field sampling , and t he concentrations of Gly and Mgly in the \nblank samples were either not detected or below the MDL. Recovery tests by using standard addition of the \ntarget compounds were also conducted, and the recovery percentages were in the range of 80%-92%. \nCalibration curves were established by analyzing the PFPH -dicarbonyl derivatives prepared in hexane \nsolutions with known concentrations. The mixtures of the standard dicarbonyls (40 wt. % solution in H 2O, \nSigma- Aldrich) and PFPH in hexane were allowed to stand at the room temperatures for at least 2 h to \nensure complete derivatization. Concentrations of individual standard dicarbonyl ranged from 0.01 to 5.0 μg \nmL-1. Calibration curves were obtained by first running the calibration standards with the GC-MS system the \nsame way as the hexane extracts and then linearly correlating responses (peak areas) with the amounts of \ndicarbonyls (R2 > 0.995). MDLs of dicarbonyls, which were determined by using seven replicate analyses of \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n9 \nthe lowest concentrations, were < 0.1 ppbv . To check the response of the system, a medium concentration \nstandard was injected to the system, and the RSD was within 10%. \n2.3 Calculation of dicarbonyl formations from observed precursors \n2.3.1 Production rate of dicarbonyls \nGly and Mgly are secondarily produced in the atmosphere through the oxidation of various VOCs by \noxidants such as OH, NO3 and O3. With the known dicarbonyl yields of an oxidation process and the mixing \nratios of precursor and oxidant, the chemical production rate (PR) of dicarbonyl can be calculated according \nto the following equation (Choi et al., 2010): \n (Eq.1) \nwhere k and y represent the rate coefficients and dicarbonyl yields for the reaction between oxidant and \nprecursor, respectively ; [precursor] and [ oxidant] denote the mixing ratio s of precursor and oxidant, \nrespectively. The dicarbonyl production rate was calculated at 1 -hour intervals corresponding to the \nsampling intervals for VOC precursors. \n2.3.2 Expected mixing ratios of dicarbonyls \nThe expected steady-state mixing ratios of a dicarbonyl ([dicarbonyl]E) through the oxidation of their \nprecursors can be calculated using the following equation (Lawson et al., 2015): \n \n \n \n (Eq.2) \nwhere y represents dicarbonyl yields for the reaction between oxidant and precursor; [precursor] is the \nobserved mixing ratio of precursor; τdicarbonyl and τprecursor represent the in-situ lifetime of dicarbonyl and \nprecursor, respectively. \nIn the troposphere, VOCs including dicarbonyls and their precursors are removed mainly by the processes of \nphotolysis as well as the oxidation by hydroxyl (OH) radicals, nitrate (NO 3) radicals and O 3 (Atkinson and \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n10 \nArey, 2003 ; Myriokefalitakis et al., 2008 ). For dicarbonyls, irreversible uptake by aqueous particles and \ncloud droplets is another important sink (Ervens, 2015). Physical processes of wet and dry dep osition are \nconsidered as only a minor sink for dicarbonyls and their precursors (Fu et al., 2008 ). Therefore, the in-situ \nlifetimes of dicarbonyls and precursors (τ) can be calculated using the following equation (Spaulding et al., \n2003; Volkamer et al., 2007): \n \n \n (Eq.3) \nwhere kOH, kO3 and kNO3 are the rate coefficients for the reaction with OH, O3 and NO3, respectively (Table \nS1); [OH], [O3] and [NO3] represent the concentrations of OH, O 3 and NO3, respectively; kphotolysis and kuptake \nrepresent the rate constants of photolysis and aqueous uptake, respectively. The average diurnal patterns of \nthe calculated in-situ lifetimes are presented in Figure S1. The steady state approximation requires that a \nreaction must have proceeded for a period several times longer than the loss lifetime (Washenfelder et al., \n2011). In this study, the estimated average daytime lifetime of Gly and Mgly were 0.7 h and 1.9 h, \nrespectively. Therefore, the steady state approximation was conducted at 3–h intervals corresponding to the \nsampling intervals of dicarbonyls. \nA few things should be noted about our calculations of dicarbonyl formation from the precursors . First, the \nreactions of O 3 with some important short -lived precursors ( e.g. glycolaldehyde, methylbutenol, \nhydroxyacetone, monoterpenes other than α-pinene, sesquiterpenes, and some higher carbon number olefins) \nalso produce substantial Gly and Mgly (Calvert, 2000 ; Fu et al., 2008 ; Mackenzie-Rae et al., 2017). \nUnfortunately, these compounds were not measured in this study due to the limitation of our instruments . \nSecond, since the concentrations of OH and NO 3 radical were not measured, the values estimated using a \nphotochemical box model incorporating Master Chemical Mechanism (PBM -MCM) (Gong et al., 2018 ) \nwere used in the calculation. Third, the dicarbonyl yields for most other precursors used in this study (Table \n2) were derived from previous studies (Calvert, 2000; Fick et al., 2003; Fu et al., 2008; Nishino et al., 2010; \nVolkamer R et al., 2006). \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n11 \n3 Results and discussion \n3.1 Back trajectories and meteorological conditions \nTo characterize the transport of air masses that arrived at the Nanling site during the field campaign, 72-hr \nback trajectories were calculated using the Hybrid Single Particle Lagrangian Integrated Trajectory \n(HYSPLIT) model (Stein et al., 2015) for each of the days in the sampling period (Figure 1). An elevation of \n1,800 m a.s.l. was chosen as the endpoint in the model (Lin et al., 2017). The end time was set to 05:00 LT, \nwhich was the start ti me of dicarbonyl sampling. Overall, the Nanling site was mainly affected by three \nsources of air masses during the sampling period : easterly, southerly and northerly . The easterly, which \noccurred between Jul 31 – Aug 2 and Oct 9–14, originated from the ocean, traversed through eastern China \nat a lower height, and arrived at the site from the northeast. The southerly, which occurred between Aug 3–5, \nOct 15, and Oct 25–31, originated from the South China Sea, traversed through the Pearl River Delta region, \nand arrived at the site from the south. The northerly, which occurred during Nov 1–5, originated from central \nChina, traversed at a higher height, and arrived at the site from the northwest. During the field campaign, the \nmean temperature, relative humidity , wind speed , and cloud cover were 14.9 ± 0.3 °C (mean ± 95% \nconfidence interval, the same below), 89.0 ± 1.3 %, 4.2 ± 0.2 m s–1 and 58.0 ± 4.8%, respectively (Figure 2). \nThe average mixing ratios of O 3, NO2* and CO were 56.2 ± 1.2, 3.2 ± 0.1 and 407.0 ± 16.1 ppbv during the \nsampling period. \n3.2 Data overview and comparison with other studies \n3.2.1 Concentrations and variations of dicarbonyls and related compounds \nFigure 2 presents the time series for Gly and Mgly and the key precursors (i.e. isoprene, acetone, acetylene, \nethene, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes), trace gases (O 3, NO 2*, CO , CH 3Cl, ethane ), and \nmeteorological parameters (temperature, relative humidity, UVa, cloud cover, wind speed) for the sampling \nperiod. The mixing ratio of Gly ranged from 215 to 992 pptv with an average of 509±31 pptv , and the \nmixing ratio of Mgly ranged from 121 to 891 pptv with an average of 340±32 pptv. \nThe mixing ratios of dicarbonyls and related gases were markedly different in the air masses of different \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n12 \norigins. To better understand the characteristics of the different air masses, in Figure 3 the average diurnal \nvariations of dicarbonyls, key precursors, selected trace gases, and meteorological parameters , were shown \nfor the three sources of identified air masses. On average, the mixing ratios of dicarbonyls in the air masses \nof the easterly and southerly origins (547±35 pptv Gly and 362±37 pptv Mgly) were higher than those of the \nnortherly origin (390±49 pptv Gly and 251±31 pptv Mgly). During the periods of prevailing easterly winds, \nthe air masses had relatively high mixing ratio s of isoprene and acetone , likely indicating the dominant \nimpact of biogenic sources on the observed dicarbonyls . During the periods of prevailing southerly winds, \nthe air masses had passed over the highly industrialized PRD region before arriving at the site and thus had \nhigh levels of BTEX (benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene and xylenes). Of note is the peak value of 992 pptv in \nGly mixing ratio recorded on Oct. 27 during an ozone episode with hourly O3 mixing ratios up to 110 ppb). \nThe O 3 episodes were associated with high levels of toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes, which are often \nused as solvents in various industries in the PRD (Guo et al., 2011 ). These results suggest that regional \ntransport of anthropogenic emissions from the PRD brought significant amounts of oxidants and dicarbonyl \nprecursors to the Nanling site . Finally, w hen the northerly winds were prevailing in November, the air \nmasses traveled over important biomass burning regions in central China and carried to the site abundant \nbiofuel combustion tracers ( e.g. CH 3Cl, acetylene, and CO). Overall, these observations reveal different \ncharacteristics of pollutants in the air masses transported to the site and indicate impact of both biogenic and \nanthropogenic sources on the concentrations and characteristics of dicarbonyls observed in this study. \n3.2.2 Comparison with previous background observations \nTable 1 compares the average mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly measured at the Nanling site and the mixing \nratios found in other studies. The overall levels of dicarbonyl mixing ratios in this study were higher than the \nbackground levels previously observed at either near-ground sites or mountaintop sites . Of note is that the \nmixing ratios of dicarbonyls in Mt. Tai, where dicarbonyls were derived from biomass burning in the North \nChina Plain (Kawamura et al., 2013 ), were considerably higher than those found in similar forest \natmospheres such as the Blodgett forest in USA and Hokkaido in Japan (Matsunaga et al., 2004 ; Spaulding \net al., 2003 ). However, the mixing ratios of dicarbonyls observed at the Nanling site were still higher than \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n13 \nthose found at Mt. Tai although they were approximately 0.5~2 times lower than those in urban areas \ndominated by vehicle exhaust (Ho et al., 2002 ; Liu et al., 2006 ). In addition, the Gly mixing ratios at the \nNanling site were even higher than those in the rural PRD region (Li et al., 2013b), one of the “hot-spots” of \nGly in the world (Miller et al., 2016) as well as several urban regions surrounded by residential area (Dai et \nal., 2012; Okuzawa et al., 2007). \nHigh mixing ratio s of dicarbonyls found at forested mountain s ites may suggest strong accumulation \nprocesses caused by either more rapid production from the oxidation of local biogenic precursors or strong \nregional transport from anthropogenic influenced source regions or both . Many recent studies have \nattributed high levels of Gly mixing ratios observed in forested or urban regions to active photochemistry (Li \net al., 2013b ; MacDonald et al., 2012 ; Miller et al., 2016 ; Miller et al., 2017 ). Kawamura et al. (2013 ) \nconcluded that regional transport of biomass burning emissions significantly contributed to the observed \nhigh level dicarbonyls at Mt. Tai during intensive field burning of agricultural wastes in the North Chin a \nPlain. Although a number of studies have reported that the polymerization process es of dicarbonyls in \nparticulate matter (PM) play an important role in the formation of SOA (Liggio et al., 2005; Ortiz-Montalvo \net al., 2 016; Schwier et al., 2010 ), the relatively low PM2.5 concentrations in Nanling site (averaging 11.1 \nμg/m3) suggest that its dicarbonyl concentrations may not be significantly affected by their heterogeneous \nreaction in PM. Based on the above , we hypothesized that rapid in-situ formation and strong regional \ntransport could be the two main contributors for the observed high mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly at the \nNanling site. Further discussion will be presented in Sections 3.3 and 3.4. \n3.3 In-situ formations of Gly and Mgly \nTo understand the contribution of in-situ formation to the observed high levels of dicarbonyl mixing ratios, \nthe Gly and Mgly production from the oxidation of measured precursors were estimated using a \nparameterized approach. \n3.3.1 Dicarbonyl production rate from precursors \nTable 2 shows the average production rates of Gly and Mgly from the precursors observed during the \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n14 \nsampling periods. The total production rate of Mgly (201 ± 46 pptv h−1) was significantly higher than that of \nGly (74 ± 13 pptv h −1), with Mgly showing a stronger association with biogenic precursors when compared \nto Gly. The reactions of isoprene and OH dominated the dicarbonyl production, accounting for 58% of Gly \nand 86% of Mgly production, respectively . The OH-initiated oxidation of aromatics was the second \nimportant pathway for the Gly and Mgly production , accounting for 18% of Gly and 9% of Mgly , \nrespectively. The oxidations of isoprene by NO 3, which mainly occurred at night, also contributed \nsignificantly to the dicarbonyl production (11% of Gly and 4% of Mgly). Some longer-lived precursors such \nas benzene, acetone and >C3 alkanes, which can travel a long distance before being oxidized, had only minor \ncontributions to the in-situ dicarbonyl formation. \nBoth Gly and Mgly production rates display a clear diurnal cycle with rapid rising at 09:00 LT and peaking \nat 12:00 LT (Figure 4), similar to the patterns of solar radiation and OH radical concentrations (Figure S1). \nThe reaction of NO3 with isoprene dominated the dicarbonyl production at night. \nThe dicarbonyl producti on also varied with the different air masses arriving at the site from different \ndirections (Figure 4). The production of Gly and Mgly was the highest in the air masses with the easterly \norigin, followed by the southerly and northerly air masses . For the Mgly production, the oxidation of \nisoprene was the dominant contributor in all the air masses, with aromatics contributed slightly during the \ntimes dominated by the southerly winds. Unlike Mgly, the oxidation of isoprene was the most important in \nthe Gly production during the times of the easterly winds, but the oxidation of aromatics and acetylene \ncontributed substantially to the Gly production during the times when the southerly and northerly winds \ndominated, implying enhanced regional transport of anthropogenic precursors. \nOverall, the calculated dicarbonyl production indicates the important role of biogenic (e.g. isoprene) and \nanthropogenic (e.g. aromatics and acetylene) precursors in the secondary formation of Gly and Mgly, an \nobservation consistent with previous studies in southern China by Li et al. (2014 and Miller et al. (2016). \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n15 \n3.3.2 Linking expected and observed dicarbonyls \nThe expected mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly were calculated using the mixing ratios of precursors measured \nin the same samples for Gly and Mgly measurements (Figure 5). Overall, the average mixing ratios of the \nGly and Mgly formed in-situ are 49 ± 18 and 222 ± 77 pptv, respectively, and account for 9 ± 3% and 67 ± \n21% of the observed Gly and Mgly, respecti vely. The expected mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly calculated \nusing the production rates in Table 2 are higher for the times of easterly winds than those for the times of the \nsoutherly and northerly winds. The observed Mgly mixing ratio s can be adequately accounted for by the \nin-situ oxidation of observed precursors, among which isoprene was the dominant species. For Gly, however, \na large difference exists between the observed and the expected mixing ratios, suggesting there might be an \nunaccounted-for sources for this dicarbonyl species. \nOur calculations show that on average only 9% of the observed Gly can be explained by the in-situ oxidation \nof the precursors. The large discrepancy between expected and observed Gly mixing ratios at the site could \nbe due to (1) other unaccounted -for mechanisms for Gly production ; (2) formation of Gly from other \nbiogenic VOCs (BVOCs) that were not measured ; or (3) long-range transport from additional primary or \nsecondary Gly sources. First, one likely Gly production mechani sm is the oxidation of organic aerosol \n(Kwan et al., 2006 ), but this is not expected to be significant because of the much greater Gly production \nfrom gas phase oxidation of ubiquitous precursors in the lower troposphere (Fu et al., 2008 ). Second, \npotential contributions to the Gly production from the other unmeasured BVOCs including several \nmonoterpenes (e.g. α-pinene, △3-carene, geraniol, and citral) and methylbutenol (MBO) are expected to be \nminor, as our calculations include all major BVOC precursors. Fu et al. (2008 ) reported that the mean \noverall molar yield of Gly and Mgly from oxidation of monoterpenes is 2.8% and 4.2%, respectively, which \nwere 15~18 times lower than the contribution of isoprene. These authors also reporte the contribution of \nmethylbutenol to the Gly production to be small (yielding ~ 0.3% of Gly) . Thus, a large portion of the \nobserved Gly was likely transported to the Nanling site from either primary anthropogenic emissions or \nsecondary sources. \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n16 \nOn a regional scale, the lifetimes of Gly (2.9 h) in the atmosphere is generally longer than that of Mgly (1.6 \nh) because of the more rapid photolysis and oxidation of Mgly (Fu et al., 2008). Thus it is expected that Gly \nwould be more slowly removed out of the air and more easily transported to the Nanling sit e with aged air \nmasses from the upwind areas. However, the equilibrium of dicarbonyls between production and loss is \nsensitive to relative humidity due to their high water solubility (Fick et al., 2003; Liggio et al., 2005) . It has \nalso been found that wet deposition and uptake on clouds and aerosols are likely to be significant loss \nprocesses at night (Fu et al., 2008) . Therefore, in a local environment with high humidity such as th e \nforested mountains, the in -situ lifetime of Gly may be shorter than that of Mgly due to the much higher \nwater solubility of Gly (Kroll et al., 2005). For example, our calculation shows that Gly has a shorter in -situ \nlifetime than Mgly at the Nanling site (Figure S1). Therefore, it is possible that compared to Mgly, Gly was \nmore readily to be transported from the distant sources but was more quickly removed from the air when air \nmasses passing through the humid forests surrounding the site. \nAlthough globally Mgly has a shorter atmospheric lifetime, regionally its ambient mixing ratio s can be \nslightly higher than that of Gly due to its much higher production yield from biogenic precursors (e.g. \nisoprene and monoterpenes) and ar omatics as well as precursors from urban regions (e.g., >C 2 alkenes \nand >C 3 alkanes) (Fu et al., 2008 ). This was observed in several studies conducted in remote or rural \natmospheres, where the G ly/Mgly ratios were found to be less than 1 (Table 1). In this study, with the \nexpected mixing ratio of Mgly significantly higher than that of Gly , the estimated ratio of the expected \nGly/Mgly ratio is about 0.5 (Figure S2). However, the observed Gly/Mgly ratio (1.8 ± 0.2) was significantly \nhigher than the expected ratio, suggesting that there were additional sources supplying Gly to the Nanling \nsite. Thus, primary or secondary Gly transported from distant sources cannot be ruled out as a significant \nfactor affecting the Gly mixing ratios in the areas around the Nanling site. \n3.4 Impact of regional transport \nThe earlier discussions indicate that Gly could be transported over long ranges to the Nanling site from \npotential “hot-spot” regions such as forests, metr opolitan areas, and biomass burning areas. To assess the \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n17 \nimpact of long -range transport of dicarbonyl precursors, polarplots of Gly and Mgly mixing ratio s were \nconstructed and are shown in Figure 6. The plots indicate that high mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly were \nassociated with winds from the south and, to a less extend, winds from east with speed >6 m s-1. There were \nactive biogenic and anthropogenic emission sources to the east (Li et al., 2013a) and south (Fang et al., 2016) \nof our site, and the oxidized plumes from these sources could have contributed to the high mixing ratio \nlevels of dicarbonyls. The observed mixing ratio s of dicarbonyls were mostly at high levels (>400 pptv) \nwhen the southly and eastly winds were strong (>4 m/s), suggesting that distance transport of dicarbonyls by \nwinds might be a primary source of dicarbonyls during those times. \n3.4.1 Biogenic sources from eastern China in the easterly air masses \nHigh mixing ratios of secondary Gly have been observed over areas with high biogenic VOCs emissions \n(MacDonald et al., 2012 ; Vrekoussis et al., 2009 ). The easterly air masses passing through the areas with \nabundant vegetation (Li et al., 2013a) had the highest mixing ratios of BVOCs (including isoprene, α-pinene, \nand acetone). That a large fraction of Gly cannot be explained by the in -situ formation from the observed \nprecursors in the easterly air masses strongly suggests the influence of biogenic sources from eastern of the \nNanling site. After emitted from the sources and/or during the transport of air masses from the upwind areas, \nbiogenic precursors with higher reactivity (e.g . isoprene and pinenes ) will decay more rapidly, with the \nsurvival of less reactive species (e.g. acetone) and the enrichment of oxidation products (i.e., Gly and Mgly) \nin the aged air masses. Therefore, these air masses can bring considerable amount of Gly to the site via \nlong-distance transport under the favorable wind conditions. \n3.4.2 Anthropogenic source in southerly air masses from PRD region \nDuring the periods of the southerly winds, our calculation shows that only 2–16% of the observed Gly can \nbe attributed to in -situ formation from precursors (Figure 5), and thus there is a large discrepancy between \nthe observed and the expected mixing ratios of Gly (Figure 7). In the south erly air masses that had passed \nover the PRD, a region with high levels of Gly and its precursor aromatics based on both satellite \nobservations (Miller et al., 2016; Vrekoussis et al., 2009) and ground-based measurements (Li et al., 2013b), \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n18 \nthe mixing ratio s of the aromatics were very high. Due to their relatively long lifetimes, aromatics can \ngreatly enhance the dicarbonyl mixing ratio levels in the outflow of anthropogenic emission regions (Fu et \nal., 2008). At the Nanling site diurnal variations of decreased daytime mixing ratios and increased nighttime \nmixing ratios were observed for aromatics (Figure 3), which was opposite to the diurnal variations of Gly \nmixing ratios with higher daytime mixing ratios (541 ± 175 pptv) and slightly lower nighttime mixing ratios \n(502 ± 170 pptv). The two different diurnal variation patterns indicate that unlike aromatics, Gly was not \ndirectly transported from the PRD but had more secondary production from the anthropogenic precursors \nduring the regional transport. These results suggest the transport of air masses from the PRD region to the \nNanling site as an additional source for secondary formation of Gly , especially under prevailing southerly \nwind conditions. \n3.4.3 Biomass burning emissions in northerly air masses from central China \nAlthough in the late autumn when the northerly winds were prevailing the dicarbonyl mixing ratios at the \nNanling site showed a decline likely due to the lower temperature and the weaker UV radiation (Figure 2), \nthey were still higher than the background levels of many other sites (Table 1). High concentrations of Gly \nand Mgly in biomass burning seasons are often attributed to the biomass burning or biofuel combustion \n(Kawamura et al., 2013 ; Zarzana et al., 2017 ; Zarzana et al., 2018 ). Direct emissions from biomass burning \nand biofule use have been estimated to contribute 38% of the Gly budget and 8% of the Mgly budget, lower \nthan formation from biogenic precursors but higher than other anthropogenic emissions (Fu et al., 2008 ). \nThus, during the late autumn, the dicarbonyls at the Nanling site were likely affected by the active biomass \nburning. CH3Cl, CO, and acetylene , because of their high emissions from biomass burning, have been \nwidely used as representative tracers for air masses perturbed by biomass burning emissions (De Gouw et al., \n2004; Liu et al., 2008 ; Yurganov et al., 2008 ). Indeed, in our study the mixing ratios of CH 3Cl, acetylene, \nand benzene showed good to excellent correlations with the CO mixing ratio during November 1 -5, as \nshown in Figure 8. To further validate the potential influence of biomass burning on the dicarbonyl \nchemistry of the Nanling site, backward trajectories were analyzed along with the fire spot data provided by \nMODIS (Moderate-resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer). Backward trajectory patterns calculated using \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n19 \nthe HYSPLIT model reveal the route of air masses passing through the burning areas. A highly active fire \nepisode occurred near the site during this period, and at the same time the CH3Cl mixing ratio reached the \nhighest level at the site. Also, the ratio of CH 3Cl to CO was 0.31 (r = 0.60), which is within the suggested \nrange of 0.2–0.83 for biomass burning or biofuel combustion (Andreae and Merlet, 2001 ).Based on the \nabove, it can be concluded that the dicarbonyl mixing ratios at the Nanling site were significantly influenced \nby biomass burning emissions during the late autumn. \n4 Conclusions \nA field campaign to study ambient Gly and Mgly were conducted during Jul 31-Nov 5 of 2016 at a remote \nmountaintop site in southern China. During the sampling period, the average mixing ratios of Gly and Mgly \nwere 509±31 and 340±32 pptv, respectively, with the average Gly/Mgly ratio equal to 1.8± 0.2. Both the \nconcentrations and the ratio are significantly higher than those found in a number of background sites \naround the world . We calculated the production of Gly and Mgly using observed precursors and analyzed \nthe influences of air masses with different origins t o examine the hypothesis that rapid in -situ formation \ncombined with strong regional transport were the two main factors causing the high mixing ratio s of \ndicarbonyls observed at the site. On average, 67% of the observed Mgly can be explained by the in -situ \nformation from the precursors, but only 9% of the Gly can be accounted for by this pathway, suggesting that \nMgly was mainly derived from the in -situ formation but Gly might still have unidentified sources. There \nwere large variations in dicarbonyl mixing ratio s in the air masses originated from different regions , \nindicating the important role of regional transport of pollutants in the atmospheric chemistry of dicarbonyls \nat the Nanling site and the surrounding areas. Correlation analysis and model calculations indicate that the \nbiogenic sources in eastern China and anthropoge nic sources in the PRD region were the two primary \ncontributors to the dicarbonyls in the Nanling area. Furthermore, the backward trajectory analysis coupled \nwith fire spot data shows that in the late autumn time of the sampling period, biomass burning als o had a \nsignificant impact on the dicarbonyls at the site. This study shows that atmospheric concetrations of Gly and \nMgly in southern China are dominated by biogenic and anthropogenic sources and highlight s the role of \nbiomass burning emission in late aut umn. Our results indicate that regional transport of anthropogenic \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n20 \npollution from the urban and suburban areas has signifcantly perturbed the atmospheric chemistry in the \nbackground forests in southern China. \n \nData availability. The data set is available t o the community and can be accessed by request from Boguang \nWang (tbongue@jnu.edu.cn) and Hao Wang (wanghao@jnu.edu.cn) \nAuthor contributions. BW and HW planned and organized the study , and were deeply involved in writing \nthe manuscript. SL and DG performed the atmospheric measurement s and data analysis and wrote the \nmanuscript. YL, YD, and HD assisted heavily with the atmospheric measurements and data analysis. GW \nand CH conducted the model development and data analysis. Other coauthors provided useful insi ghts in \ndata analysis and contributed to the writing of the manuscript. \nSL and DG contributed equally to this work. \n \n5 Acknowledgments \nThis work was supported by the Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC) Major Research Program \n(91544215), the National Key R&D Program of China (2016YFC0202200), the NSFC Projects (41877370 \nand 41373116), and the Science and Technology Planning Project of Guangdong Province of China \n(2014B090901058). We thank Jie Ou and Yu Zheng of Shaoguan Environmental Monitoring Central Station \nfor the ir help during the sampling campaign. We also thank Huagui Peng and his group of Guangdong \nTianjingshan Forest Farm for their help during the field sampling. We acknowledge Dr. David Carslaw for \nthe provision of the R package “open air” (http://www.openair -project.org) used in this publication. Very \nspecial thanks also go to Zhengquan Zhu’s family for their warm help during the field campaign. \n \n6 References \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n21 \nAndreae MO, Merlet P. Emission of trace gases and aerosols from biomass burning. Global biogeochemical cycles 2001; 15: \n955-966. \nAtkinson R, Arey J. Atmospheric degradation of volatile organic compounds. Chem Rev 2003; 103: 4605 -38. \nCalvert JG. The Mechanisms of Atmospheric Oxidation of the Alkenes: Oxford Univers ity Press, 2000. \nChan CK, Yao X. Air pollution in mega cities in China. Atmospheric Environment 2008; 42: 1 -42. \nChoi W, Faloona IC, Bouvier-Brown NC, McKay M, Goldstein AH, Mao J, et al. Observations of elevated formaldehyde over a \nforest canopy suggest mi ssing sources from rapid oxidation of arboreal hydrocarbons. Atmospheric Chemistry and \nPhysics 2010; 10: 8761-8781. \nDai WT, Ho SSH, Ho KF, Liu WD, Cao JJ, Lee SC. Seasonal and diurnal variations of mono - and di-carbonyls in Xi'an, China. \nAtmospheric Research 2012; 113: 102-112. \nDe Gouw J, Cooper O, Warneke C, Hudson P, Fehsenfeld F, Holloway J, et al. Chemical composition of air masses transported \nfrom Asia to the US West Coast during ITCT 2K2: Fossil fuel combustion versus biomass ‐burning signatures. \nJournal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres 2004; 109. \nErvens B. Modeling the processing of aerosol and trace gases in clouds and fogs. Chem Rev 2015; 115: 4157 -98. \nFang X, Shao M, Stohl A, Zhang Q, Zheng J, Guo H, et al. Top -down estimates of benzene and tolu ene emissions in the Pearl \nRiver Delta and Hong Kong, China. Atmos. Chem. Phys. 2016; 16: 3369 -3382. \nFick J, Pommer L, Nilsson C, Andersson B. Effect of OH radicals, relative humidity, and time on the composition of the \nproducts formed in the ozonolysis of α -pinene. Atmospheric Environment 2003; 37: 4087-4096. \nFu TM, Jacob DJ, Wittrock F, Burrows JP, Vrekoussis M, Henze DK. Global budgets of atmospheric glyoxal and \nmethylglyoxal, and implications for formation of secondary organic aerosols. Journal of Geop hysical \nResearch-Atmospheres 2008; 113: 596-598. \nGong D, Wang H, Zhang S, Wang Y, Liu SC, Guo H, et al. Low -level summertime isoprene observed at a forested mountaintop \nsite in southern China: implications for strong regional atmospheric oxidative capacity . Atmos. Chem. Phys. 2018; 18: \n14417-14432. \nGuenther AB, Jiang X, Heald CL, Sakulyanontvittaya T, Duhl T, Emmons LK, et al. The Model of Emissions of Gases and \nAerosols from Nature version 2.1 (MEGAN2.1): an extended and updated framework for modeling biog enic emissions. \nGeoscientific Model Development 2012; 5: 1471-1492. \nGuo H, Cheng HR, Ling ZH, Louie PKK, Ayoko GA. Which emission sources are responsible for the volatile organic \ncompounds in the atmosphere of Pearl River Delta? Journal of Hazardous Materi als 2011; 188: 116-124. \nHays MD, Geron CD, Linna KJ, Smith ND, Schauer JJ. Speciation of gas -phase and fine particle emissions from burning of \nfoliar fuels. Environmental Science & Technology 2002; 36: 2281-2295. \nHo KF, Ho SS, Dai WT, Cao JJ, Huang RJ, Tia n L, et al. Seasonal variations of monocarbonyl and dicarbonyl in urban and \nsub-urban sites of Xi'an, China. Environ Monit Assess 2014; 186: 2835 -49. \nHo SS, Yu JZ. Determination of airborne carbonyls: comparison of a thermal desorption/GC method with the s tandard \nDNPH/HPLC method. Environ Sci Technol 2004; 38: 862-70. \nHo SSH, Yu JZ, Chem A. Feasibility of collection and analysis of airborne carbonyls by on -sorbent derivatization and thermal \ndesorption. Analytical Chemistry 2002; 74: 1232. \nHofzumahaus A, Rohrer F, Lu K, Bohn B, Brauers T, Chang CC, et al. Amplified trace gas removal in the troposphere. Science \n2009; 324: 1702-4. \nHoque HMS, Irie H, Damiani A. First MAX -DOAS Observations of Formaldehyde and Glyoxal in Phimai, Thailand. Journal of \nGeophysical Research: Atmospheres 2018; 123: 9957-9975. \nIeda T, Kitamori Y, Mochida M, Hirata R, Hirano T, Inukai K, et al. Diurnal variations and vertical gradients of biogenic \nvolatile and semi-volatile organic compounds at the Tomakomai larch forest station in Japan. Tellus Series B-Chemical \nand Physical Meteorology 2006; 58: 177-186. \nJacob DJ, Field BD, Jin EM, Bey I, Li QB, Logan JA, et al. Atmospheric budget of acetone. Journal of Geophysical \nResearch-Atmospheres 2002; 107: ACH-1-ACH 5-17. \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n22 \nKawamura K, Bikkina S. A review of dicarboxylic acids and related compounds in atmospheric aerosols: Molecular \ndistributions, sources and transformation. Atmospheric Research 2016; 170: 140 -160. \nKawamura K, Okuzawa K, Aggarwal SG, Irie H, Kanaya Y, Wang Z. Determination of gaseous and particulate carbonyls \n(glycolaldehyde, hydroxyacetone, glyoxal, methylglyoxal, nonanal and decanal) in the atmosphere at Mt. Tai. \nAtmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2013; 13: 5369-5380. \nKroll JH, Ng NL, Murphy SM, Varutbangkul V, Flagan RC, Seinfeld JH . Chamber studies of secondary organic aerosol growth \nby reactive uptake of simple carbonyl compounds. Journal of Geophysical Research -Atmospheres 2005; 110. \nKwan AJ, Crounse JD, Clarke AD, Shinozuka Y, Anderson BE, Crawford JH, et al. On the flux of oxygenated volatile organic \ncompounds from organic aerosol oxidation. Geophysical Research Letters 2006; 33. \nLawson SJ, Selleck PW, Galbally IE, Keywood MD, Harvey MJ, Lerot C, et al. Seasonal in situ observations of glyoxal and \nmethylglyoxal over the temp erate oceans of the Southern Hemisphere. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2015; 15: \n223-240. \nLi LY, Chen Y, Xie SD. Spatio -temporal variation of biogenic volatile organic compounds emissions in China. Environmental \nPollution 2013a; 182: 157-168. \nLi X, Brauers T, Hofzumahaus A, Lu K, Li YP, Shao M, et al. MAX -DOAS measurements of NO2, HCHO and CHOCHO at a \nrural site in Southern China. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2013b; 13: 2133 -2151. \nLi X, Rohrer F, Brauers T, Hofzumahaus A, Lu K, Shao M, et al. Model ing of HCHO and CHOCHO at a semi -rural site in \nsouthern China during the PRIDE-PRD2006 campaign. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2014; 14: 12291 -12305. \nLiggio J, Li SM, McLaren R. Reactive uptake of glyoxal by particulate matter. Journal of Geophysical R esearch-Atmospheres \n2005; 110. \nLim YB, Tan Y, Turpin BJ. Chemical insights, explicit chemistry and yields of secondary organic aerosol from methylglyoxal \nand glyoxal. Atmospheric Chemistry & Physics 2013; 13: 4687-4725. \nLin Q, Zhang G, Peng L, Bi X, Wang X, Brechtel FJ, et al. In situ chemical measurement of individual cloud residue particles at \na mountain site, South China. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics Discussions 2017; 2017: 1 -39. \nLiu W, Zhang J, Kwon J, Weisel C, Turpin B, Zhang L, et al. Concentrat ions and Source Characteristics of Airborne Carbonyl \nCompounds Measured Outside Urban Residences. Journal of the Air & Waste Management Association 2006; 56: \n1196. \nLiu Y, Seco R, Kim S, Guenther AB, Goldstein AH, Keutsch FN, et al. Isoprene photo -oxidation products quantify the effect of \npollution on hydroxyl radicals over Amazonia. Science Advances 2018; 4. \nLiu Y, Shao M, Fu L, Lu S, Zeng L, Tang D. Source profiles of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) measured in China: Part I. \nAtmospheric Environment 2008; 42: 6247-6260. \nLiu Z, Wang YH, Vrekoussis M, Richter A, Wittrock F, Burrows JP, et al. Exploring the missing source of glyoxal (CHOCHO) \nover China. Geophysical Research Letters 2012; 39. \nLui KH, Dai WT, Chan CS, Ho SSH, Cao JJ, Lee SC, et al. Spatial dis tributions of airborne di -carbonyls in urban and rural \nareas in China. Atmospheric Research 2017; 186: 1-8. \nMacDonald SM, Oetjen H, Mahajan AS, Whalley LK, Edwards PM, Heard DE, et al. DOAS measurements of formaldehyde \nand glyoxal above a south-east Asian tropical rainforest. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2012; 12: 5949 -5962. \nMackenzie-Rae FA, Liu T, Deng W, Saunders SM, Fang Z, Zhang Y, et al. Ozonolysis of α -phellandrene – Part 1: Gas- and \nparticle-phase characterisation. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2017; 17: 6583-6609. \nMatsunaga S, Mochida M, Kawamura K. Variation on the atmospheric concentrations of biogenic carbonyl compounds and \ntheir removal processes in the northern forest at Moshiri, Hokkaido Island in Japan. Journal of Geophysical \nResearch-Atmospheres 2004; 109: 207-225. \nMiller CC, Jacob DJ, Abad GG, Chance K. Hotspot of glyoxal over the Pearl River delta seen from the OMI satellite instrument: \nimplications for emissions of aromatic hydrocarbons. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2016; 16: 4631-4639. \nMiller CC, Jacob DJ, Marais EA, Yu KR, Travis KR, Kim PS, et al. Glyoxal yield from isoprene oxidation and relation to \nformaldehyde: chemical mechanism, constraints from SENEX aircraft observations, and interpretation of OMI satellite \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n23 \ndata. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2017; 17: 8725-8738. \nMkoma SL, Kawamura K. Molecular composition of dicarboxylic acids, ketocarboxylic acids, α -dicarbonyls and fatty acids in \natmospheric aerosols from Tanzania, East Africa during wet and dry seasons. Atmosp heric Chemistry and Physics \n2013; 13: 2235-2251. \nMunger JW, Jacob DJ, Daube BC, Horowitz LW, Keene WC, Heikes BG. Formaldehyde, glyoxal, and methylglyoxal in air and \ncloudwater at a rural mountain site in central Virginia. Journal of Geophysical Research A tmospheres 1995; 100: \n9325–9333. \nMyriokefalitakis S, Vrekoussis M, Tsigaridis K, Wittrock F, Richter A, Bruhl C, et al. The influence of natural and \nanthropogenic secondary sources on the glyoxal global distribution. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2008; 8: \n4965-4981. \nNishino N, Arey J, Atkinson R. Formation Yields of Glyoxal and Methylglyoxal from the Gas -Phase OH Radical -Initiated \nReactions of Toluene, Xylenes, and Trimethylbenzenes as a Function of NO2 Concentration. Vol 114, 2010. \nOkamoto S, Tanimoto H. A review of atmospheric chemistry observations at mountain sites. Progress in Earth and Planetary \nScience 2016; 3. \nOkuzawa K, Mochida M, Bendle J, Wang H, Kawamu ra K. Diurnal variation of semi -volatile dicarbonyls and \nhydroxycarbonyls in the urban atmosphere. Geochemistry 2007; 41: 125-134. \nOrtiz-Montalvo DL, Schwier AN, Yong BL, Mcneill VF, Turpin BJ. Volatility of methylglyoxal cloud SOA formed through \nOH radical oxidation and droplet evaporation. Atmospheric Environment 2016; 130: 145 -152. \nOrtiz R, Ha gino H, Sekiguchi K, Wang QY, Sakamoto K. Ambient air measurements of six bifunctional carbonyls in a \nsuburban area. Atmospheric Research 2006; 82: 709-718. \nPang X, Lewis AC, Hamilton JF. Determination of airborne carbonyls via pentafluorophenylhydrazine d erivatisation by \nGC-MS and its comparison with HPLC method. Talanta 2011; 85: 406 -14. \nPossanzini M, Tagliacozzo G, Cecinato A. Ambient levels and sources of lower carbonyls at Montelibretti, Rome (Italy). Water \nAir and Soil Pollution 2007; 183: 447-454. \nSantos F, Longo K, Guenther A, Kim S, Gu D, Oram D, et al. Biomass burning emission disturbances of isoprene oxidation in a \ntropical forest. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2018; 18: 12715-12734. \nSchwier AN, Sareen N, Mitroo D, Shapiro EL, McNeill VF. Gl yoxal-methylglyoxal cross -reactions in secondary organic \naerosol formation. Environ Sci Technol 2010; 44: 6174-82. \nSiu CL, Lee L, Li XD, Zhang G, Peng XZ, Zhang L. Biomonitoring of trace metals in the atmosphere using moss (Hypnum \nplumaeforme) in the Nanli ng Mountains and the Pearl River Delta, Southern China. Atmospheric Environment 2005; \n39: 397-407. \nSpaulding RS, Schade GW, Goldstein AH, Charles MJ. Characterization of secondary atmospheric photooxidation products: \nEvidence for biogenic and anthropogenic sources. AGU Fall Meeting, 2003, pp. 103-112. \nStein AF, Draxler RR, Rolph GD, Stunder BJB, Cohen MD, Ngan F. NOAA’s HYSPLIT Atmospheric Transport and \nDispersion Modeling System. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society 2015; 96: 2059 -2077. \nVolkamer R, Barnes I, Platt U, Molina L T, J MM. Remote Sensing of Glyoxal by Differential Optical Absorption Spectroscopy \n(DOAS): Advancements in Simulation Chamber and Field Experiments. Environmental Simulation Chambers: \nApplication to Atmospheric Chemical Processes, 2006. \nVolkamer R, Martini FS, Molina LT, Salcedo D, Jimenez JL, Molina MJ. A missing sink for gas -phase glyoxal in Mexico City: \nFormation of secondary organic aerosol. Geophysical Research Letters 2007; 34. \nVrekoussis M, Wittrock F, Richter A, Burro ws JP. Temporal and spatial variability of glyoxal as observed from space. \nAtmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2009; 9: 4485-4504. \nWang T, Wei XL, Ding AJ, Poon CN, Lam KS, Li YS, et al. Increasing surface ozone concentrations in the background \natmosphere of Southern China, 1994-2007. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics 2009; 9: 6217-6227. \nWashenfelder RA, Young CJ, Brown SS, Angevine WM, Atlas EL, Blake DR, et al. The glyoxal budget and its contribution to \norganic aerosol for Los Angeles, California, during Ca lNex 2010. Journal of Geophysical Research -Atmospheres 2011; \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n24 \n116. \nXu Z, Wang T, Xue LK, Louie PKK, Luk CWY, Gao J, et al. Evaluating the uncertainties of thermal catalytic conversion in \nmeasuring atmospheric nitrogen dioxide at four differently polluted si tes in China. Atmospheric Environment 2013; 76: \n221-226. \nYurganov LN, McMillan WW, Dzhola AV, Grechko EI, Jones NB, van der Werf GR. Global AIRS and MOPITT CO \nmeasurements: Validation, comparison, and links to biomass burning variations and carbon cycle. J ournal of \nGeophysical Research: Atmospheres 2008; 113. \nZarzana KJ, Min KE, Washenfelder RA, Kaiser J, Krawiec -Thayer M, Peischl J, et al. Emissions of Glyoxal and Other \nCarbonyl Compounds from Agricultural Biomass Burning Plumes Sampled by Aircraft. Enviro nmental Science & \nTechnology 2017; 51: 11761-11770. \nZarzana KJ, Selimovic V, Koss AR, Sekimoto K, Coggon MM, Yuan B, et al. Primary emissions of glyoxal and methylglyoxal \nfrom laboratory measurements of open biomass burning. Atmospheric Chemistry and Physics Discussions 2018: 1-35. \nZhang X, Yu Y, Xie S, Meng X, Qi W, Wang S, et al. The Characteristic of Black Carbon Concentration at National Ambient \nAir Background Stations. Environmental Monitoring in China 2018; 34: 32 -40. \nZhang YL, Wang XM, Wen S, Herrman n H, Yang WQ, Huang XY, et al. On -road vehicle emissions of glyoxal and \nmethylglyoxal from tunnel tests in urban Guangzhou, China. Atmospheric Environment 2016; 127: 55 -60. \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n25 \nList of Tables \nTable 1. Comparison of average concentrations of Gly and Mgly at the Nanling site with previous \nground-based observations at remote, rural and urban sites \nTable 2. Average molar yield and production rate of Gly and Mgly for the reaction of oxidants ( OH, NO3 \nand O3) and measured precursors during sampling periods (31 Jul. – 5 Nov. 2016) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n26 \n \nTable 1. Comparison of average concentrations of Gly and Mgly at the Nanling site with previous ground -based \nobservations at remote, rural and urban sites. \nSite and location Altitude \n(m a .s.l.) \nSampling \ntime \nGly \n(pptv) \nMgly \n(pptv) \nG/M d Reference \nRemote \nNanling Mountains, \nChina \n1690 Jul.–Nov. 509±31 340±32 1.8 This study \nEasterly a 564±53 362±64 1.9 \nSoutherly b 533±47 \n390±49 \n362±48 \n251±31 \n1.8 \n1.7 Northerly c \nMount Tai, China 1533 Jun. 204 327 0.6 Kawamura et al. (2013) \nHokkaido, Japan 250 Aug. 18 28 0.6 Matsunaga et al. (2004) \nDanum Valley, Malaysia 503 Jun., Jul. 335±41 − − MacDonald et al. (2012) \nBlodgett forest, USA 1315 Aug., Sep. 27±15 130±60 0.2 Spaulding et al. (2003) \nRural \nPRD region, China 53 Jul. 400 − e − Li et al. (2013b) \nHeihe, China 460 Jul. 60±40 130±80 − Ho et al. (2014) \nTomakomai, Japan 140 Sep. 27 58 0.5 Ieda et al. (2006) \nSaitama, Japan 11 Aug. 6 6.5 0.9 Ortiz et al. (2006) \nPhimai, Thailand 204 Jun.–Sep. 50 − − Hoque et al. (2018) \nMontelibretti, Italy 26 Aug. 409±209 848±340 0.5 Possanzini et al. (2007) \nSoutheast Nexus, US ~1000 Jun., Jul. 85 − − Miller et al. (2017) \nPinnacles, US 1037 Sep. 40 − − Munger et al. (1995) \nUrban \nXi’an, China 397 Jan. 190±90 180±70 1.0 Dai et al. (2012 ) \n June 130±90 210±130 0.6 \nHong Kong, China 104 − 1517 867 1.7 Ho and Yu (2002 ) \nElizabeth, NJ, USA − Jun.–Aug. 768±449 737±331 1.0 Liu et al. (2006 ) \nTokyo, Japan 57 Jul.–Aug. 170 270 0.6 Okuzawa et al. (2007 ) \na, b, c The sampling periods with easterly (31 Jul. – 1 Aug., 9 – 14 Oct. 2016), southerly (2 – 5 Aug., 15 Aug., 25 – 31 Oct. \n2016) and northerly (1 – 5 Nov. 2016) air masses, respectively. \nd ratios of Gly to Mgly (G/M, in pptv/pptv). \ne “−” data not reported or not applicable. \n \n \n \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n27 \nTable 2. Average molar yield and production rate of Gly and Mgly for the reaction of oxidants ( OH, NO3 and O3) \nand measured precursors during sampling periods (31 Jul – 5 Nov 2016). \n \nPrecursor Oxidant Molar yield (%) a Production rate (pptv h−1) \nGly Mgly Gly Mgly \nAlkenes 54.8 182.2 \nIsoprene OH \nNO3 \nO3 \n6.2 \n43.7 \n4 \n25 \n37.8 \n− d \n42.8 ± 13.7 \n8.5 ± 3.0 \n0.5 ± 0.1 \n172.8 ± 55.4 \n7.3 ± 2.6 \n− \nα-Pinene O3 5.1 4.2 0.9 ± 0.1 0.7 ± 0.1 \nEthene OH \nO3 \n5.7 \n0.44 \n− \n− \n1.6 ± 0.4 \n0.1 \n− \n− \n>C2 alkenes b OH − 7.7 − 1.4 ± 0.5 \nPropene O3 8.3 − 0.2 ± 0.1 − \n1-Pentene O3 2 − 0.1 − \nIsobutene O3 0.5 − 0.1 − \nAlkanes 0.1 1.2 \nPropane OH − 11 − 0.8 ± 0.2 \n>C3 alkanes b OH 1 3.2 0.1 ± 0.1 0.4 ± 0.1 \nAromatics 13.7 17.1 \nBenzene OH 36 − 0.4 ± 0.1 − \nToluene OH 26 21.5 2.3 ± 1.1 1.9 ± 0.9 \nEB c OH 55 − 1.9 ± 0.7 − \nm,p-Xylene OH 25.2 35.1 1.3 ± 0.4 1.8 ± 0.5 \no-Xylene OH 12.7 33.1 0.5 ± 0.2 1.4 ± 0.5 \n1,2,3-TMB c OH 4.7 15.1 0.7 ± 0.2 2.3 ± 0.8 \n1,2,4-TMB OH 8.7 27.2 0.9 ± 0.3 2.8 ± 1.0 \n1,3,5-TMB OH − 58.1 − 6.9 ± 2.5 \nn-PB c OH 55 − 1.4 ± 0.4 − \ni-PB OH 57 − 0.7 ± 0.2 − \no-ET c OH 16 − 0.5 ± 0.1 − \nm-ET OH 3.6 − 0.3 ± 0.1 − \np-ET OH 40.5 − 2.8 ± 0.9 − \nOthers 5.7 0.5 \nAcetylene OH 63.5 − 5.6 ± 1.5 − \nAcetone OH 2.8 16.2 0.1 ± 0.1 0.5 ± 0.1 \nSum 74 201 \na Molar yields were taken from Fu et al. (2008 ), Volkamer R et al. (2006 ), Nishino et al. (2010 ), Fick et al. (2003 ) and \nCalvert (2000). \nb >C2 alkenes (include 3 alkenes) and >C3 alkanes (include 17 alkanes) are represented in this study as a single lumped \nspecies with the reactivity of propene and n-butane, respectively. \nc EB, TMB, PB, ET represents ethylbenzene, trimethylbenzene, propylbenzene and ethyltoluene, respectively. \nd “−” not applicable. \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n28 \nList of Figures \nFigure 1: (a) HYSPLIT 72-hr back trajectories for air masses at 1,800 m a.s.l. during the field campaign (Jul \n31 – Nov 5, 2016). The black circle and white borders refer to the sampling site (1,690 m a.s.l.) and the PRD \nregion in southern China, respectively. The red, green and magenta lines denote the southerly, easterly and \nnortherly air masses passing through the PRD region, eastern China , and centra l China, respectively. (b) \nHeights of air masses as a function of elapsed time. \nFigure 2: Time series (3 -hr averages) of dicarbonyls (Gly and Mgly, in ppbv), main precursors (isoprene, \nacetone, acetylene, ethene, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes, in ppbv), trace gases (CO, O 3, NO 2*, \nCH3Cl, unit: ppbv) , and other parameters (temperature, relative humidity, UVa, wind speed, cloud cover) \nduring 31 Jul – 5 Nov 2016. Sampling were not conducted on Aug 2, Oct 28 and Oct 31. The green, red and \nmagenta denot e periods of different prevailing winds (Figure 1). The cloud cover data are available at \nhttps://ready.arl.noaa.gov/READYamet.php, last access: 19 Oct 2018). \nFigure 3: Diurnal variations in average concentrations of Gly, Mgly, precursors (isoprene, α-pinene, acetone, \nacetylene, ethene, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes, trimethylbenzenes), and trace gases (CH3Cl, O3, \nNO2*, CO), meteorological parameters (temperature, relative humidity), ratios of Gly to Mgly (G/M) at the \nNanling site during Jul 31Nov 5 2016. Plots were grouped into three periods according to the three sources \nof air masses arriving at the site (Figure 1). The concentrations of α-pinene and trimethylbenzenes in the \nnortherly air masses are not shown in the figure as they were lower than the detection limit. \nFigure 4: Mean diurnal variations of (a) Gly and (b) Mgly production rates (pptv h −1) from the OH−, O3− and \nNO3−initiated oxidation of measured VOCs during the periods with three sources of air masses. \nFigure 5: Mean diurnal variations of expected mixing ratios of (a) Gly and (b) Mgly from the oxidation of \nmeasured VOCs during the sampling period in air masses carried by the three different prevailing winds. \nFigure 6: Comparison of observed and expected dicarbonyl mixing ratios. Green dashed lines denote slopes \nfor different ratios of observed to expected values. \nFigure 7: Polarplots of the (1) observed , (2) expected, and (3) transported (a) Gly and (b) Mgly during the \nfield campaign period (Jul 31–Nov 5, 2016). Each data point represents a 3 -hour average. Concentrations \nvaried by wind speed (m s‒1) and wind direction. \nFigure 8: (a) HYSPLIT 72-hr back trajectories for air masses at 1,800 m a.s.l. during Nov. 1 – Nov. 5, 2016. \nThe red dots denote the MODIS fire spots during the corresponding periods (fire spot data are available at \nhttps://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/, last access: Oct 20 2018). (b) Correlation plots between CH 3Cl, \nacetylene, and CO mixing ratios observed during Nov 1– Nov 5, 2016 (1-hr data). \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n29 \n \nFigure 1. (a) HYSPLIT 72-hr back trajectories for air masses at 1800 m a.s.l. during the field campaign ( Jul 31 – \nNov 5, 2016). The black circle and white borders refer to the the sampling site (1,690 m a.s.l.) and the PRD region in \nsouthern China, respectively. The red, green, and magenta lines denote the southerly, easterly , and northerly air \nmasses passing through the PRD region, eastern China, and central China, respectively. (b) Heights of air masses as \na function of elapsed time. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n30 \n \nFigure 2. Time series (3-hr averages) of dicarbonyls (Gly and Mgly, in ppbv), key precursors (isoprene, acetone, \nacetylene, ethene, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes, in ppbv), trace gases (CO, O3, NO2*, CH3Cl, unit: ppbv) \nand other parameters (temperature, relative humidity, UVa, wind speed, cloud cover) during 31 Jul – 5 Nov 2016. \nSampling were not conducted on Aug 2, Oct 28 and Oct 31. The green, red and magenta denote periods of different \nprevailing winds (Figure 1). The cloud cover data are available at https://ready.arl.noaa.gov/READYamet.php, last \naccess: 19 Oct 2018). Data discontinuities in the figure mean that during those time periods, either no data were \navailable due to the instrumental calibration and maintenance or the removal of values below the MDL. \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n31 \n \n \n \nFigure 3. Diurnal variations in average concentrations of Gly, Mgly, precursors (isoprene, α-pinene, acetone, \nacetylene, ethene, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylenes, trimethylbenzenes), and trace gases (CH 3Cl, O3, NO2*, \nCO), meteorological parameters (temperature, relative humidity), ratios of Gly to Mgly (G/M) at the Nanling site \nduring Jul 31 Nov 5 2016. Plots were grouped into three periods according to the three sources of air masses \narriving at the site (Figure 1). The concentrations of α-pinene and trimethylbenzenes in the northerly air masses \nare not shown in the figure as they were lower than the detection limit. \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n32 \n \n \nFigure 4. Mean diurnal variations of (a) Gly and (b) Mgly production rates (pptv h −1) from the OH −, O3− and \nNO3−initiated oxidation of measured VOCs during the periods with three sources of air masses (see Figure 1). \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n33 \n \nFigure 5. Mean diurnal variations of expected mixing ratios of (a) Gly and (b) Mgly from the oxidation of \nmeasured VOCs during the sampling period in air masse s carried by the three different prevailing winds ( see \nFigure 1). \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n34 \n \nFigure 6. Polarplots of the (1) observed , (2) expected, and (3) transported (a) Gly and (b) Mgly during the filed \ncampaign (Jul 31–Nov 5, 2016). Each data point represents a 3-hour average. Concentrations varied by wind speed \n(m s‒1) and wind direction. \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n35 \n \nFigure 7. Comparison of observed and expected dicarbonyl mixing ratios. Green dashed lines denote slopes for \ndifferent ratios of observed to expected values. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n36 \n \nFigure 8. (a) HYSPLIT 72-hr back trajectories for air masses at 1,800 m a.s.l. during Nov1 – Nov 5, 2016. The red \ndots denote the MODIS fire spots during the corresponding periods (fire spot data are available at \nhttps://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/, last access: Oct 20 2018). (b) Correlation plots between CH3Cl, acetylene, and \nCO mixing ratios observed during Nov 1– Nov 5, 2016 (1-hr data). \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n37 \n \n \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT\n \n38 \nHighlights \n Extremely high levels of Gly, Mgly and Gly/Mgly were observed at a remote mountaintop site in \nsouthern China; \n Strong atmospheric oxidation capacity in this region can explain about 67% of the measured MGly but \nonly 9% for Gly; \n Regional transport of biogenic emissions from eastern China and anthropogenic emissions from the PRD \nregion heavily influenced the Gly concentration and the Gly/MGly ratio at the national background \nstation. \nACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT",
