  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Agricultural Water Management）",
  "content": "Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia\n - Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Contact & Help \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Press/Media \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia \n Ammar Adham * , Jan G. Wesseling , Rasha Abed , Michel Riksen , Mohamed Ouessar , Coen J. Ritsema * Corresponding author for this work \n Soil Physics and Land Management Soil, Water and Land Use PE&RC WIMEK \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › Academic › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 51 \n \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n Citations\n(Scopus) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n Climate change is believed to have a large impact on water resources system both globally and regionally. It has become a major global issue, especially in developing countries because these are most affected by its impacts. Rainwater harvesting techniques offer an alternative source of water and represent specific adaptive strategies to cope with water scarcity within future climate change. Studying the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting techniques, however, is difficult, because the general circulation models (GCMs) which are widely used to simulate scenarios of future climate change operate on a coarse scale. We estimated the impact of climate change on water availability at the watershed level by downscaling precipitation and temperature from the GCMs using a statistical downscaling model. A water harvesting model then assessed the performance of the rainwater harvesting techniques for the Oum Zessar watershed in southeastern Tunisia under current climatic conditions and scenarios of future climate change. Annual temperature tended to increase and precipitation tended to decrease. These changes of climatic variables were used in the water harvesting model to simulate future water availability. Changing the directions of water flow between sub-catchments in combination with changing the spillway heights strongly affected the performance of rainwater harvesting under the scenarios of future climate, resulting in a sufficient water supply for 92% of all sub-catchments, compared to 72% without these changes. \n Original language English Pages (from-to) 131-140 Number of pages 10 Journal Agricultural Water Management Volume 221 DOIs https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 Publication status Published - 20 Jul 2019 \n \n \n \n \n UN SDGs \n This output contributes to the following UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n SDG 6 \n Clean Water and Sanitation \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n SDG 13 \n Climate Action \n \n \n \n \n \n Keywords Climate change GCMs SDSM Tunisia Water harvesting model \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n 10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Climate Change \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Tunisia \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Rainwater Harvesting \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n General Circulation Model \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 28% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Downscaling \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 28% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Water Availability \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 28% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Water Scarcity \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 14% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Water Supply \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 14% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Adham, A., Wesseling, J. G., Abed, R. , Riksen, M. , Ouessar, M. , & Ritsema, C. J. (2019). Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia . Agricultural Water Management , 221 , 131-140. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 \n \n \n \n Adham, Ammar ; Wesseling, Jan G. ; Abed, Rasha et al. / Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia . In: Agricultural Water Management . 2019 ; Vol. 221. pp. 131-140. \n \n \n \n @article{f7624f1101764e6eb466a78857b676b8, title = \"Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia\", abstract = \"Climate change is believed to have a large impact on water resources system both globally and regionally. It has become a major global issue, especially in developing countries because these are most affected by its impacts. Rainwater harvesting techniques offer an alternative source of water and represent specific adaptive strategies to cope with water scarcity within future climate change. Studying the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting techniques, however, is difficult, because the general circulation models (GCMs) which are widely used to simulate scenarios of future climate change operate on a coarse scale. We estimated the impact of climate change on water availability at the watershed level by downscaling precipitation and temperature from the GCMs using a statistical downscaling model. A water harvesting model then assessed the performance of the rainwater harvesting techniques for the Oum Zessar watershed in southeastern Tunisia under current climatic conditions and scenarios of future climate change. Annual temperature tended to increase and precipitation tended to decrease. These changes of climatic variables were used in the water harvesting model to simulate future water availability. Changing the directions of water flow between sub-catchments in combination with changing the spillway heights strongly affected the performance of rainwater harvesting under the scenarios of future climate, resulting in a sufficient water supply for 92\\% of all sub-catchments, compared to 72\\% without these changes.\", keywords = \"Climate change, GCMs, SDSM, Tunisia, Water harvesting model\", author = \"Ammar Adham and Wesseling, \\{Jan G.\\} and Rasha Abed and Michel Riksen and Mohamed Ouessar and Ritsema, \\{Coen J.\\}\", year = \"2019\", month = jul, day = \"20\", doi = \"10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006\", language = \"English\", volume = \"221\", pages = \"131--140\", journal = \"Agricultural Water Management\", issn = \"0378-3774\", publisher = \"Elsevier\", } \n \n \n \n Adham, A, Wesseling, JG, Abed, R , Riksen, M , Ouessar, M & Ritsema, CJ 2019, ' Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia ', Agricultural Water Management , vol. 221, pp. 131-140. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 \n \n \n \n Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia. / Adham, Ammar; Wesseling, Jan G.; Abed, Rasha et al. In: Agricultural Water Management , Vol. 221, 20.07.2019, p. 131-140. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › Academic › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia AU - Adham, Ammar AU - Wesseling, Jan G. AU - Abed, Rasha AU - Riksen, Michel AU - Ouessar, Mohamed AU - Ritsema, Coen J. PY - 2019/7/20 Y1 - 2019/7/20 N2 - Climate change is believed to have a large impact on water resources system both globally and regionally. It has become a major global issue, especially in developing countries because these are most affected by its impacts. Rainwater harvesting techniques offer an alternative source of water and represent specific adaptive strategies to cope with water scarcity within future climate change. Studying the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting techniques, however, is difficult, because the general circulation models (GCMs) which are widely used to simulate scenarios of future climate change operate on a coarse scale. We estimated the impact of climate change on water availability at the watershed level by downscaling precipitation and temperature from the GCMs using a statistical downscaling model. A water harvesting model then assessed the performance of the rainwater harvesting techniques for the Oum Zessar watershed in southeastern Tunisia under current climatic conditions and scenarios of future climate change. Annual temperature tended to increase and precipitation tended to decrease. These changes of climatic variables were used in the water harvesting model to simulate future water availability. Changing the directions of water flow between sub-catchments in combination with changing the spillway heights strongly affected the performance of rainwater harvesting under the scenarios of future climate, resulting in a sufficient water supply for 92% of all sub-catchments, compared to 72% without these changes. AB - Climate change is believed to have a large impact on water resources system both globally and regionally. It has become a major global issue, especially in developing countries because these are most affected by its impacts. Rainwater harvesting techniques offer an alternative source of water and represent specific adaptive strategies to cope with water scarcity within future climate change. Studying the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting techniques, however, is difficult, because the general circulation models (GCMs) which are widely used to simulate scenarios of future climate change operate on a coarse scale. We estimated the impact of climate change on water availability at the watershed level by downscaling precipitation and temperature from the GCMs using a statistical downscaling model. A water harvesting model then assessed the performance of the rainwater harvesting techniques for the Oum Zessar watershed in southeastern Tunisia under current climatic conditions and scenarios of future climate change. Annual temperature tended to increase and precipitation tended to decrease. These changes of climatic variables were used in the water harvesting model to simulate future water availability. Changing the directions of water flow between sub-catchments in combination with changing the spillway heights strongly affected the performance of rainwater harvesting under the scenarios of future climate, resulting in a sufficient water supply for 92% of all sub-catchments, compared to 72% without these changes. KW - Climate change KW - GCMs KW - SDSM KW - Tunisia KW - Water harvesting model U2 - 10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 DO - 10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 M3 - Article AN - SCOPUS:85064996216 SN - 0378-3774 VL - 221 SP - 131 EP - 140 JO - Agricultural Water Management JF - Agricultural Water Management ER - \n \n \n \n Adham A, Wesseling JG, Abed R , Riksen M , Ouessar M , Ritsema CJ . Assessing the impact of climate change on rainwater harvesting in the Oum Zessar watershed in Southeastern Tunisia . Agricultural Water Management . 2019 Jul 20;221:131-140. doi: 10.1016/j.agwat.2019.05.006 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new tab , Scopus Link opens in a new tab & Elsevier Fingerprint Engine&trade; Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n All content on this site: Copyright &copy; 2026 Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\n \n We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the use of cookies \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research data protection policy Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n About web accessibility Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Report vulnerability Link opens in a new tab",
