 {
  "title": "Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016",
  "source": "Journal of Environmental Management",
  "journal": "Journal of Environmental Management",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-04-01",
  "category": "tech",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "b39f51854e13f751",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-10",
  "_ev": "ti:92162385eff46150",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Journal of Environmental Management）",
  "content": "Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential\n - Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Contact & Help \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Press/Media \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential \n Misery Mulele Nabuyanda * , Johan van Bruggen , Peter Kelderman , Kenneth Irvine * Corresponding author for this work \n Aquatic Ecology and Water Quality Management \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › Academic › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 23 \n \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n Citations\n(Scopus) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n There is critical concern over heavy metals because they are biotoxins. The best management option is elimination or at least minimization of effluence into the environment, but in several regions, mining wastewater or acid mine drainage (AMD) effluence into natural wetlands has continued. The ability of wetlands to attenuate heavy metals in mining wastewater and AMD has led to natural wetlands being used as recipients of these effluents in many parts of the world. Ten greenhouse-based laboratory-scale constructed wetlands (GLCW) were set up at IHE-Delft Institute for Water Education to understand the mechanisms and fate of heavy metals in three Zambian wetlands in attenuation of Co, Cu, and Pb. These were operated as Free Water Surface Constructed Wetlands (FWS-CWs). The principal investigations compared how vegetated and unvegetated microcosm artificial wetlands retained controlled additions of heavy metals and the effect of drying and rewetting on that. The potential for phytoremediation using Typha angustifolia was also investigated. Typha angustifolia was planted in three vegetated and compared with one unvegetated treatment. Treatments A, B, and, the investigated, Treatment D received synthetic wastewater containing Co, Cu, and Pb, while a control, Treatment C, received tap water. Water samples were taken throughout the experiment, and sediment samples collected after the first flushing and before drying. Samples of T. angustifolia were taken before drying the wetlands. Analyses for Co, Cu, and Pb were made in the water and sediment, and in roots, stems and leaves of plant samples. The unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs removed more Co from wastewater (52%) than the vegetated Dutch and Zambian sediments GLCWs (13% and −4%, respectively). There was a similar removal of Cu among the GLCWs receiving wastewater (81%–87%). The removal of Pb was significantly higher in the vegetated Dutch sediment GLCWs than the unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs, (89% and 72%, respectively). It was concluded that a hectare of the vegetated Zambian sediments with similar design parameters of 50 mg/m 2 .day for Co, Cu, and Pb used in the experiment would on average retain 83 g/day of Co, and 417 g/day of both Cu and Pb. After drying, Co, Cu, and Pb washed out on the first day of rewetting. The washout after that took only a few days. How long the metals washed out of the GLCWs was in order Co > Cu > Pb. T. angustifolia could neither be classified as an accumulator nor an excluder species because the concentrations of Co, Cu, and Pb in the sediments and T. angustifolia were below phytotoxic levels mainly due to a short running period of the experiment. \n Original language English Pages (from-to) 510-518 Journal Journal of Environmental Management Volume 236 DOIs https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 Publication status Published - 15 Apr 2019 \n \n \n \n Keywords Co Constructed wetlands Cu Greenhouse-based Pb Phytoremediation Removal Rewetting Washout \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Rewetting \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Laboratory Scale \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Heavy Metal \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Remobilization \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Phytoremediation \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Typha angustifolia \n Agricultural and Biological Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Constructed Wetland \n Agricultural and Biological Sciences \n 75% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Acid Mine Drainage \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 50% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Nabuyanda, M. M., van Bruggen, J., Kelderman, P. , & Irvine, K. (2019). Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential . Journal of Environmental Management , 236 , 510-518. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 \n \n \n \n Nabuyanda, Misery Mulele ; van Bruggen, Johan ; Kelderman, Peter et al. / Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential . In: Journal of Environmental Management . 2019 ; Vol. 236. pp. 510-518. \n \n \n \n @article{f9fb3f7623b342ebaf3d9ea26dc72137, title = \"Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential\", abstract = \" There is critical concern over heavy metals because they are biotoxins. The best management option is elimination or at least minimization of effluence into the environment, but in several regions, mining wastewater or acid mine drainage (AMD) effluence into natural wetlands has continued. The ability of wetlands to attenuate heavy metals in mining wastewater and AMD has led to natural wetlands being used as recipients of these effluents in many parts of the world. Ten greenhouse-based laboratory-scale constructed wetlands (GLCW) were set up at IHE-Delft Institute for Water Education to understand the mechanisms and fate of heavy metals in three Zambian wetlands in attenuation of Co, Cu, and Pb. These were operated as Free Water Surface Constructed Wetlands (FWS-CWs). The principal investigations compared how vegetated and unvegetated microcosm artificial wetlands retained controlled additions of heavy metals and the effect of drying and rewetting on that. The potential for phytoremediation using Typha angustifolia was also investigated. Typha angustifolia was planted in three vegetated and compared with one unvegetated treatment. Treatments A, B, and, the investigated, Treatment D received synthetic wastewater containing Co, Cu, and Pb, while a control, Treatment C, received tap water. Water samples were taken throughout the experiment, and sediment samples collected after the first flushing and before drying. Samples of T. angustifolia were taken before drying the wetlands. Analyses for Co, Cu, and Pb were made in the water and sediment, and in roots, stems and leaves of plant samples. The unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs removed more Co from wastewater (52\\%) than the vegetated Dutch and Zambian sediments GLCWs (13\\% and −4\\%, respectively). There was a similar removal of Cu among the GLCWs receiving wastewater (81\\%–87\\%). The removal of Pb was significantly higher in the vegetated Dutch sediment GLCWs than the unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs, (89\\% and 72\\%, respectively). It was concluded that a hectare of the vegetated Zambian sediments with similar design parameters of 50 mg/m 2 .day for Co, Cu, and Pb used in the experiment would on average retain 83 g/day of Co, and 417 g/day of both Cu and Pb. After drying, Co, Cu, and Pb washed out on the first day of rewetting. The washout after that took only a few days. How long the metals washed out of the GLCWs was in order Co > Cu > Pb. T. angustifolia could neither be classified as an accumulator nor an excluder species because the concentrations of Co, Cu, and Pb in the sediments and T. angustifolia were below phytotoxic levels mainly due to a short running period of the experiment. \", keywords = \"Co, Constructed wetlands, Cu, Greenhouse-based, Pb, Phytoremediation, Removal, Rewetting, Washout\", author = \"Nabuyanda, \\{Misery Mulele\\} and \\{van Bruggen\\}, Johan and Peter Kelderman and Kenneth Irvine\", year = \"2019\", month = apr, day = \"15\", doi = \"10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016\", language = \"English\", volume = \"236\", pages = \"510--518\", journal = \"Journal of Environmental Management\", issn = \"0301-4797\", publisher = \"Elsevier\", } \n \n \n \n Nabuyanda, MM, van Bruggen, J, Kelderman, P & Irvine, K 2019, ' Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential ', Journal of Environmental Management , vol. 236, pp. 510-518. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 \n \n \n \n Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential. / Nabuyanda, Misery Mulele; van Bruggen, Johan; Kelderman, Peter et al. In: Journal of Environmental Management , Vol. 236, 15.04.2019, p. 510-518. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › Academic › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential AU - Nabuyanda, Misery Mulele AU - van Bruggen, Johan AU - Kelderman, Peter AU - Irvine, Kenneth PY - 2019/4/15 Y1 - 2019/4/15 N2 - There is critical concern over heavy metals because they are biotoxins. The best management option is elimination or at least minimization of effluence into the environment, but in several regions, mining wastewater or acid mine drainage (AMD) effluence into natural wetlands has continued. The ability of wetlands to attenuate heavy metals in mining wastewater and AMD has led to natural wetlands being used as recipients of these effluents in many parts of the world. Ten greenhouse-based laboratory-scale constructed wetlands (GLCW) were set up at IHE-Delft Institute for Water Education to understand the mechanisms and fate of heavy metals in three Zambian wetlands in attenuation of Co, Cu, and Pb. These were operated as Free Water Surface Constructed Wetlands (FWS-CWs). The principal investigations compared how vegetated and unvegetated microcosm artificial wetlands retained controlled additions of heavy metals and the effect of drying and rewetting on that. The potential for phytoremediation using Typha angustifolia was also investigated. Typha angustifolia was planted in three vegetated and compared with one unvegetated treatment. Treatments A, B, and, the investigated, Treatment D received synthetic wastewater containing Co, Cu, and Pb, while a control, Treatment C, received tap water. Water samples were taken throughout the experiment, and sediment samples collected after the first flushing and before drying. Samples of T. angustifolia were taken before drying the wetlands. Analyses for Co, Cu, and Pb were made in the water and sediment, and in roots, stems and leaves of plant samples. The unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs removed more Co from wastewater (52%) than the vegetated Dutch and Zambian sediments GLCWs (13% and −4%, respectively). There was a similar removal of Cu among the GLCWs receiving wastewater (81%–87%). The removal of Pb was significantly higher in the vegetated Dutch sediment GLCWs than the unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs, (89% and 72%, respectively). It was concluded that a hectare of the vegetated Zambian sediments with similar design parameters of 50 mg/m 2 .day for Co, Cu, and Pb used in the experiment would on average retain 83 g/day of Co, and 417 g/day of both Cu and Pb. After drying, Co, Cu, and Pb washed out on the first day of rewetting. The washout after that took only a few days. How long the metals washed out of the GLCWs was in order Co > Cu > Pb. T. angustifolia could neither be classified as an accumulator nor an excluder species because the concentrations of Co, Cu, and Pb in the sediments and T. angustifolia were below phytotoxic levels mainly due to a short running period of the experiment. AB - There is critical concern over heavy metals because they are biotoxins. The best management option is elimination or at least minimization of effluence into the environment, but in several regions, mining wastewater or acid mine drainage (AMD) effluence into natural wetlands has continued. The ability of wetlands to attenuate heavy metals in mining wastewater and AMD has led to natural wetlands being used as recipients of these effluents in many parts of the world. Ten greenhouse-based laboratory-scale constructed wetlands (GLCW) were set up at IHE-Delft Institute for Water Education to understand the mechanisms and fate of heavy metals in three Zambian wetlands in attenuation of Co, Cu, and Pb. These were operated as Free Water Surface Constructed Wetlands (FWS-CWs). The principal investigations compared how vegetated and unvegetated microcosm artificial wetlands retained controlled additions of heavy metals and the effect of drying and rewetting on that. The potential for phytoremediation using Typha angustifolia was also investigated. Typha angustifolia was planted in three vegetated and compared with one unvegetated treatment. Treatments A, B, and, the investigated, Treatment D received synthetic wastewater containing Co, Cu, and Pb, while a control, Treatment C, received tap water. Water samples were taken throughout the experiment, and sediment samples collected after the first flushing and before drying. Samples of T. angustifolia were taken before drying the wetlands. Analyses for Co, Cu, and Pb were made in the water and sediment, and in roots, stems and leaves of plant samples. The unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs removed more Co from wastewater (52%) than the vegetated Dutch and Zambian sediments GLCWs (13% and −4%, respectively). There was a similar removal of Cu among the GLCWs receiving wastewater (81%–87%). The removal of Pb was significantly higher in the vegetated Dutch sediment GLCWs than the unvegetated Dutch sediments GLCWs, (89% and 72%, respectively). It was concluded that a hectare of the vegetated Zambian sediments with similar design parameters of 50 mg/m 2 .day for Co, Cu, and Pb used in the experiment would on average retain 83 g/day of Co, and 417 g/day of both Cu and Pb. After drying, Co, Cu, and Pb washed out on the first day of rewetting. The washout after that took only a few days. How long the metals washed out of the GLCWs was in order Co > Cu > Pb. T. angustifolia could neither be classified as an accumulator nor an excluder species because the concentrations of Co, Cu, and Pb in the sediments and T. angustifolia were below phytotoxic levels mainly due to a short running period of the experiment. KW - Co KW - Constructed wetlands KW - Cu KW - Greenhouse-based KW - Pb KW - Phytoremediation KW - Removal KW - Rewetting KW - Washout U2 - 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 DO - 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 M3 - Article C2 - 30771671 AN - SCOPUS:85061671111 SN - 0301-4797 VL - 236 SP - 510 EP - 518 JO - Journal of Environmental Management JF - Journal of Environmental Management ER - \n \n \n \n Nabuyanda MM, van Bruggen J, Kelderman P , Irvine K . Investigating Co, Cu, and Pb retention and remobilization after drying and rewetting treatments in greenhouse laboratory-scale constructed treatments with and without Typha angustifolia, and connected phytoremediation potential . Journal of Environmental Management . 2019 Apr 15;236:510-518. doi: 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.02.016 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new tab , Scopus Link opens in a new tab & Elsevier Fingerprint Engine&trade; Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n All content on this site: Copyright &copy; 2026 Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\n \n We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the use of cookies \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research data protection policy Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n About web accessibility Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Report vulnerability Link opens in a new tab",
