  "content": "1 \n \nFlow Alteration-Ecology Relationships in Ozark Highland Streams: Consequences for 1 \nFish, Crayfish and Macroinvertebrate Assemblages 2 \nDustin T. Lyncha, Douglas R. Leasureb, and Daniel D. Magoulickc 3 \naArkansas Natural Heritage Commission, Department of Arkansas Heritage, 1100 North Street, 4 \nLittle Rock, Arkansas, USA, 72201 5 \nbRiver Basin Center, Odum School of Ecology, University of Georgia, 203 D.W. Brooks Drive, 6 \nAthens, Georgia, USA, 30602 7 \ncU.S. Geological Survey, Arkansas Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit, Department of 8 \nBiological Sciences, University of Arkansas, Science and Engineering, Room 601, Fayetteville, 9 \nArkansas, USA, 72701 10 \n 11 \n 12 \nCorresponding Author: Dustin Lynch – dustin.lynch@okstate.edu 13 \n 14 \nThis draft manuscript is distributed solely for the purposes of scientific peer review. Its content is 15 \ndeliberative and predecisional, so it must not be disclosed or released by reviewers. Because the 16 \nmanuscript has not yet been approved for publication by the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS), it 17 \ndoes not represent any official USGS findings or policy. 18 \nAbstract 19 \n 20 \n We examined flow alteration-ecology relationships in benthic macroinvertebrate, fish, 21 \nand crayfish assemblages in Ozark Highland streams, USA, over two years with contrasting 22 \nenvironmental conditions, a drought year (2012) and a flood year (2013). We hypothesized that: 23 \n \n2 \n \n1) there would be temporal variation in flow alteration-ecology relationships between the two 24 \nyears, 2) flow alteration-ecology relationships would be stronger during the drought year vs the 25 \nflood year, and 3) fish assemblages would show the strongest relationships with flow alteration. 26 \nWe used a quantitative richest-targeted habitat (RTH) method and a qualitative multi-habitat 27 \n(QMH) method to collect macroinvertebrates at 16 USGS gaged sites during both years. We 28 \nused backpack electrofishing to sample fish and crayfish at 17 sites in 2012 and 11 sites in 2013. 29 \nWe used redundancy analysis to relate biological response metrics, including richness, diversity, 30 \ndensity, and community-based metrics, to flow alteration. We found temporal variation in flow 31 \nalteration-ecology relationships for all taxa, and that relationships differed greatly between 32 \nassemblages. We found that relationships were stronger for macroinvertebrates during the 33 \ndrought year but not for other assemblages, and that fish assemblage relationships were not 34 \nstronger than the invertebrate taxa. Magnitude of average flow, frequency of high flow, 35 \nmagnitude of high flow, and duration of high flow were the most important categories of flow 36 \nalteration metrics across taxa. Alteration of high and average flows was more important than 37 \nalteration of low flows. Of 32 important flow alteration metrics across years and assemblages, 38 \n19 were significantly altered relative to expected values. Ecological responses differed 39 \nsubstantially between drought and flood years, and this is likely to be exacerbated with predicted 40 \nclimate change scenarios. Differences in flow alteration-ecology relationships among taxonomic 41 \ngroups and temporal variation in relationships illustrate that a complex suite of variables should 42 \nbe considered for effective conservation of stream communities related to flow alteration. 43 \n 44 \n \n3 \n \nIntroduction 45 \n The natural flow regime paradigm posits that the ecological integrity of rivers depends on 46 \ntheir natural dynamic character (Poff et al., 1997), and that traditional approaches to managing 47 \nstreams by simply focusing on minimum low flows may be inadequate to protect these 48 \necosystems and their biota (Bunn and Arthington, 2002; Poff et al., 2010). A concept related to 49 \nthe natural flow regime is the environmental flow regime; the key difference is that 50 \nenvironmental flow regimes allow for some degree of hydrologic alteration in an attempt to 51 \nbalance the needs of humans as well as stream ecosystems, while still resulting in the same 52 \npatterns and ecological outcomes as the natural flow regime (Poff et al. 1997, Bunn and 53 \nArthington 2002, Poff et al. 2010). One of the great challenges in the implementation of the 54 \nenvironmental flows (e-flows) approach to management and restoration is accounting for natural 55 \nvariability and complexity among different types of streams, even those within the same 56 \ngeographic region (Arthington et al., 2006; Kennard et al. 2010; Poff et al. 2010). 57 \nNatural streamflow regimes are threatened worldwide by a host of anthropogenic factors, 58 \nincluding construction of dams and diversion structures, groundwater withdrawals from aquifers, 59 \nand other hydromorphological alterations (Sondergaard and Jeppesen 2007, Carlisle et al., 2010). 60 \nAdditionally, extreme climate events are expected to increase as a result of global climatic 61 \nchange, including many events that directly impact lotic ecosystems, such as increases in drought 62 \nfrequency, duration, and intensity in many regions of the world (Beniston et al., 2007; Beche et 63 \nal., 2009), including in the focal region of this study, where these phenomena have already had 64 \nconsequences for rare and imperiled aquatic species (Magoulick and Lynch 2015). The potential 65 \ninteractive effects of natural and anthropogenic stressors such as drought, climate change, and 66 \nhuman water use on ecosystems highlight the need for increased understanding of each stressor 67 \n \n4 \n \n(Christensen et al., 2006; Beche et al., 2009). For example, water withdrawals during dry years 68 \ncan reduce habitat connectivity and result in critical flow reductions (Beche et al., 2009). The 69 \nmaintenance of natural hydrologic regimes can also provide resistance to species invasion (Closs 70 \nand Lake, 1996; Caiola et al., 2014), another pervasive world-wide phenomenon in freshwater 71 \nhabitats, often facilitated by anthropogenic alteration of flow regimes (Bunn and Arthington, 72 \n2002). For example, naturally flashy streams or rivers typified by frequent or rapid onset of high 73 \nflows can prevent the establishment of non-native fish species that lack behavioral adaptations to 74 \nrapid onset of flows (Meffe, 1984; Poff et al., 2010) or have a vulnerable juvenile stage present 75 \nduring periods of peak flows (Fausch et al., 2001; Poff et al., 2010). 76 \nQuantifying flow alteration, the degree of variation away from the natural flow regime, is 77 \na crucial step in environmental-flows based management approaches such as the ELOHA 78 \nframework (Poff et al., 2010; Kendy et al., 2012; Gillespie et al., 2014, McManamay & 79 \nFrimpong, 2015; King et al., 2015, Sengupta et al., 2018). While there is strong evidence that 80 \nflow alteration generally negatively affects biodiversity as well as ecosystem function (Bunn and 81 \nArthington, 2002; Harris and Heathwaite, 2011; Warfe et al., 2014), there are challenges to 82 \nestablishing transferable relationships between flow alteration and ecological response (Poff and 83 \nZimmerman, 2010). Crucial steps in the ELOHA process include regional flow regime 84 \nclassification and the quantification of flow alteration; these steps are often made difficult by 85 \nlack of hydrological data due to the somewhat sparse nature of stream gages, which are often 86 \nplaced only on larger order stream segments and may not represent all stream types in an area 87 \n(Zimmerman et al., 2018). Determining quantifiable relationships between hydrologic alteration 88 \nand biological data is not only of great interest in informing management decisions relating to 89 \nissues of water conservation and restoration (McManamay et al., 2014), but could potentially 90 \n \n5 \n \nalso be a critical tool in the assessment of the possible impacts of climate change on stream 91 \necosystems and organisms (Xenopolous et al., 2005; Farjad et al., 2015). 92 \n The objective of this study was to examine flow alteration-biological response 93 \nrelationships for fish, crayfish, and benthic macroinvertebrate assemblages in the Ozark 94 \nHighlands. We hypothesized that: 1) there would be temporal variation in flow alteration-95 \necology relationships between the two years (drought year versus flood year), 2) flow alteration-96 \necology relationships would be stronger during the drought year vs the flood year, and 3) fish 97 \nassemblages would show the strongest relationships with flow alteration. 98 \nWe hypothesized that the potential interactive effects between the dual stressors of 99 \ndrought and flow alteration would lead to stronger relationships during the drought year (Acuna 100 \net al. 2005, Beche et al., 2009, Bunn and Arthington 2002, Dodds et al. 2004, Poff and Allan 101 \n1995, Lynch et al. 2018). We hypothesized that fish would be more strongly impacted by 102 \ndrought than the other groups due to the ability of benthic macroinvertebrates and crayfish to 103 \nutilize the hyporheic zone as a refuge (DiStefano et al., 2009; Wood, et al. 2010; Stubbington 104 \n2012) or to utilize drought-coping life history strategies ranging from aestivation (Wickson et al., 105 \n2012) and desiccation-resistant eggs (Pallares et al., 2016) to overland escape (Chester et al., 106 \n2014) or shifts in timing of emergence (Stenroth et al., 2010). To address our objectives, we 107 \nconducted aquatic community sampling at 18 sites in Groundwater Flashy streams in the Ozark 108 \nHighlands over two years and used redundancy analysis (RDA) to relate biological response 109 \nvariables to metrics of flow alteration, including magnitude, frequency, duration, timing, and rate 110 \nof change. 111 \nStudy Area 112 \n \n6 \n \nThe Ozark Highlands ecoregion of southern Missouri, northern Arkansas, and northeast 113 \nOklahoma, USA (Omernik and Griffith, 2014), is heavily affected by a suite of anthropogenic 114 \nimpacts, including rapid development of urban areas and agricultural practices that affect water 115 \nquality (Petersen et al., 2005; Haggard 2010; Scott et al., 2011), expansion of natural gas 116 \nextraction (Johnson et al., 2015), displacement of native fauna due to the spread of invasive 117 \nspecies (Magoulick and DiStefano, 2007; Larson et al., 2009), and direct hydrologic alteration of 118 \nstreams via construction of reservoirs and dams (TNC-OEAT, 2003). This region is home to a 119 \ndiverse assortment of freshwater habitats and aquatic species, including endemic fish, crayfish, 120 \nmussels, macroinvertebrates, and herpetofauna (TNC-OEAT, 2003). Understanding the impacts 121 \nof hydrologic alteration could be a crucial step in the formulation of guidelines for protection and 122 \nrestoration of stream ecosystems in the region. 123 \nMethods 124 \nSite Selection 125 \n Sampling was conducted at 18 sites with USGS stream gages over two summer field 126 \nseasons (May-July) during 2012 and 2013 in northwest Arkansas, southwest Missouri, and 127 \nnortheast Oklahoma (Figure 1). Precipitation and flow conditions contrasted strongly between 128 \nthe two years. In summer 2012 the study area experienced an extreme drought, as measured on 129 \nthe Palmer Drought Severity Index (Palmer, 1965), while sustained higher than normal 130 \nprecipitation resulted in summer flooding at most sites during 2013 (NOAA, 2015). To facilitate 131 \nbiological comparisons, site selection was limited to a single ecoregion, the Ozark Highlands, a 132 \nsingle physiographic region, the Springfield Plateau, and a single flow regime, Groundwater 133 \nFlashy streams, based on a classification of Ozark-Ouachita Interior Highland streams into seven 134 \n \n7 \n \ndifferent hydrologic flow regimes (Leasure et al., 2016). Streams selected ranged in drainage 135 \narea from 16 to 542 km2. 136 \n Macroinvertebrate collections were taken at 16 sites that were the same in both years 137 \n(Figure 1). Due to extreme differences in sampling conditions between the two years (drought in 138 \n2012 versus extensive flooding in 2013), we were unable to resample seven of the largest sites 139 \nfrom the first field season for fish and crayfish during the second season, but did add one 140 \nadditional site. Seventeen sites were sampled for fish and crayfish in 2012 and 11 in 2013, with 141 \n10 overlapping sites between the two years (Figure 1). 142 \nHydrologic variable and flow alteration estimation 143 \nWe identified 64 USGS gaged streams in our study area in least-disturbed reference 144 \ncondition based on a composite hydrologic disturbance index (Falcone et al. 2010) using water 145 \nwithdrawals, density of major dams, change in dam storage between 1950 and 2009), percent 146 \ncanals in the watershed, water discharge locations, road density, and land cover fragmentation 147 \n(Leasure et al. 2016). All streams selected in least-disturbed condition had an index less than the 148 \nmedian of all gaged streams in the study area (Leasure et al. 2016). Using these streams, we 149 \ndeveloped a set of random forest models to predict 171 flow metrics (Olden and Poff 2003). Full 150 \nmodels were built initially that included 282 predictor variables describing climate, geology, 151 \nsoils, topography, groundwater and landscape characteristics within reference watersheds 152 \n(Appendix A). Importance of each variable was assessed using the default method of the 153 \nrandomForest R package (Liaw and Wiener 2002) that is based on how much prediction error 154 \nincreases when each variable is permuted while others are left the same. A benefit of using 155 \nrandom forest models for this approach is that they are not sensitive to the number of variables at 156 \n \n8 \n \neach node or the number of trees (Liaw and Wiener 2002). A reduced model was built for each 157 \nflow metric that included only the 30 most important predictor variables. 158 \nData were collected at all 208 USGS gages in the Interior Highlands for any predictor 159 \nvariable selected for at least one of the reduced random forest models. The reduced random 160 \nforest models were used to predict values of each flow metric expected under natural conditions, 161 \nas well as the distribution of expected values. The expected value for each flow metric under 162 \nnatural conditions was taken as the median of the predicted distribution. 163 \nFlow metrics were calculated for every complete 15 year period within the daily flow 164 \nrecords of 18 gages used in this study. Flow alteration was calculated as: 165 \n𝑓𝑙𝑜𝑤 𝑎𝑙𝑡𝑒𝑟𝑎𝑡𝑖𝑜𝑛 = 𝑜𝑏𝑠𝑒𝑟𝑣𝑒𝑑 − 𝑚𝑒𝑑𝑖𝑎𝑛(𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑑𝑖𝑐𝑡𝑒𝑑)𝑠𝑡𝑑. 𝑑𝑒𝑣(𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑑𝑖𝑐𝑡𝑒𝑑) 166 \nwhere observed is the value of the flow metric from a specific period with a gage’s record, and 167 \npredicted is the distribution of values expected under natural conditions predicted by the random 168 \nforest models. The standard deviation (std. dev) of predicted natural values was used for 169 \nstandardization rather than the interquartile range because the interquartile range may be zero for 170 \nrandom forest models with high accuracy. We decided not to assess flow alteration as observed / 171 \nexpected as recommended by Carlisle et al. (2010) because of issues arising when expected 172 \nvalues are zero. We dropped flow metrics that were outside our threshold criteria for bias, 173 \nprecision and accuracy, reducing our initial set of 171 metrics to 154 (Appendix B). 174 \nAquatic community assessment 175 \n \n9 \n \n Benthic macroinvertebrates, fish, and crayfish were sampled at each site. Reaches were 176 \ndefined by the presence of at least three discrete units of riffles, runs, and pools, and a qualitative 177 \nattempt was made to ensure that sampling reaches were as comparable as possible between sites. 178 \nSampling was stratified by habitat to include three units each of riffles, runs and pools for a total 179 \nof nine units per reach. Total area of reaches ranged from 140 – 957 m2 and units averaged 8.3 180 \nm (SD = 3.2) in length across all habitat types. Habitat units were located at least 100 m from 181 \nroad crossings to avoid the hydrologic influence of man-made structures that could affect stream 182 \nhabitat (Barbour et al., 1999). 183 \nBenthic macroinvertebrate samples were typically collected at sites a few hours prior to 184 \nfish and crayfish sampling. Macroinvertebrates were sampled using two different, 185 \ncomplementary methods at each site, a semi-quantitative richest-targeted habitat (RTH) method 186 \nand a qualitative multiple habitat (QMH) method, both developed for the U.S. Geological Survey 187 \nNational Water Quality Assessment (NAWQA) program (Moulton et al., 2002). Quantitative 188 \nRTH collections were taken in riffles using a 500-µm mesh Slack sampler (50 cm × 30 cm) 189 \nequipped with a 0.25 m2 area PVC frame attached to the upstream end of the sampler (Moulton 190 \net al., 2002). The Slack sampler was positioned immediately downstream of the chosen quadrat 191 \nperpendicular to the direction of flow. Large cobble and woody debris were lightly brushed, 192 \ninspected for any remaining invertebrates, and then removed from the sampling area. The 193 \nsampling area was then agitated by disturbing the substrate upstream of the mouth of the 194 \nsampler, allowing the dislodged invertebrates to flow into the trailing net. The nine discrete 195 \nsubsamples were composited into a 19-L plastic bucket for processing, which consisted of 196 \nrinsing and removing large debris from the samples, followed by elutriation and sieving (with a 197 \n500 µm sieve) in order to separate invertebrates and organic debris from inorganic material. 198 \n \n10 \n \n The QMH method was used to document invertebrate taxa present in all habitat types 199 \nthroughout the reach (Moulton et al., 2002). Crew members assessed the entire reach to 200 \ndetermine the number of different instream habitat types present and to estimate the proportion 201 \nof each type. QMH samples were collected using a D-frame kicknet with 500-µm mesh. Each 202 \nhabitat type was sampled in proportion to total habitat area for a standardized time of one hour 203 \nper reach. Samples were processed in the field as described for the RTH method. 204 \n In the laboratory, RTH and QMH samples were sorted on a square gridded subsampling 205 \nframe of 25 5 × 5 cm squares using a fixed-count approach targeting a minimum of 300 206 \norganisms (Barbour et al., 1999; Moulton et al., 2000). After pouring the sample into the frame 207 \nand allowing it to settle evenly, an initial inspection was performed to remove large and rare 208 \norganisms likely to be missed during subsampling. A grid square was randomly selected and all 209 \norganisms present were removed from the grid and processed. Subsampling proceeded in this 210 \nfashion until a minimum of 300 organisms were counted, with the square in which the 300th 211 \norganism was counted also fully processed. Macroinvertebrates were identified to the lowest 212 \npractical taxonomic level, generally family or genus. To estimate total numbers of organisms, a 213 \nlaboratory subsampling correction factor was used (Moulton et al., 2000) in which the total 214 \nnumber of grids was divided by the number of grids sorted during subsampling and multiplied by 215 \nthe number of organisms subsampled. Large and rare organisms taken from the sample as a 216 \nwhole were added to these numbers without a correction factor. These numbers were then used 217 \nto calculate invertebrate community response metrics. 218 \nFish and crayfish were collected using a Smith-Root Model LR-24 backpack- 219 \nelectrofishing unit which has been shown effective for fish and crayfish sampling in Ozark 220 \n \n11 \n \nstreams (Rabenin et al., 1997; Dauwalter and Pert, 2003). Standard LR-24 settings for power 221 \noutput based on ambient stream conductivity were used. Prior to sampling, 1.6 cm2 mesh block-222 \nnets were placed at the end of each habitat unit to prevent animals from escaping or biasing 223 \nsampling data by moving from one unit to another. A four-person team conducted three 224 \nupstream passes per habitat unit. Collections from all passes were kept in separate buckets until 225 \nall passes were completed. Each pass was processed separately and all specimens were 226 \nidentified to species and released live back into the stream. 227 \nBiological Response Metric Selection 228 \nBiological response metrics were calculated for macroinvertebrate, fish, and crayfish 229 \nassemblages (Table 1). A subset of metrics considered most ecologically relevant was chosen 230 \nbased on published relationships and best professional judgment. The five macroinvertebrate 231 \nresponse metrics chosen were: abundance; taxa richness; Simpson's diversity; percent 232 \ncontribution of individuals belonging to Orders Ephemeroptera, Plecoptera, and Trichoptera 233 \n(EPT), taxa that are associated with undisturbed habitat and high water quality (Karr, 1991); and 234 \npercent contribution of the family Chironomidae, considered a generally tolerant taxon predicted 235 \nto increase in abundance with increasing levels of perturbation (Barbour et al., 1999). Response 236 \nmetrics calculated from RTH and QMH samples were analyzed separately. 237 \n For crayfish, the three response metrics chosen were: Simpson's diversity, total crayfish 238 \ndensity (per volume sampled), and percent contribution of species designated as habitat 239 \ngeneralists in an assessment of invasion risk of crayfish in the eastern United States (Larson and 240 \nOlden 2010). These are represented in our dataset by two species, Faxonius neglectus neglectus 241 \n \n12 \n \nand Faxonius virilis. Species richness was not used as a community response metric for crayfish 242 \ndue to the generally low and relatively uniform richness across sites. 243 \n For fish, the five biological response metrics chosen were: species richness, Simpson's 244 \ndiversity, total fish density (per volume sampled), percent of total individuals belonging to 245 \nFamily Centrarchidae, and percent of total individuals belonging to species categorized as 246 \nintolerant, i.e. sensitive to various environmental perturbation, in an Index of Biotic Integrity 247 \nspecifically developed for fish assemblages of the Ozark Highlands (Dauwalter et al., 2003). 248 \nPercent Centrarchidae was chosen as a response metric because most Ozark centrarchids are 249 \necologically tolerant habitat generalists (Dauwalter et al., 2003; Robison & Buchanan, 1988). 250 \n Data Analysis 251 \n Redundancy analysis (RDA) was used to evaluate flow alteration-ecology relationships 252 \nseparately for assemblages and sampling years. RDA is a canonical ordination procedure that 253 \nexamines relationships among response variables and predictor variables in multivariate space 254 \n(ter Braak, 1995). Linear model RDA’s were appropriate because preliminary Detrended 255 \nCorrespondence Analyses (DCA) indicated that species gradient lengths were less than 1 256 \nstandard deviation (ter Braak, 1995). We used forward selection in CANOCO 4.5 (ter Braak and 257 \nSmilauer, 2002) to select flow alteration variables that were related to response metrics. We 258 \nlimited the flow alteration variables to those with lambda ≥ 0.7 after entry into the model. 259 \nPrior to RDA analysis, response variables were centered and standardized. Scaling of 260 \nordination scores was focused on inter-response variable correlations rather than inter-sample 261 \ndistances, and the response variable scores were standardized to prevent variables with large 262 \n \n13 \n \nvariances from disproportionately influencing ordination diagrams (ter Braak and Smilauer, 263 \n2002). Monte Carlo permutations were performed for each RDA to test the significance of the 264 \ncanonical axes together and were then performed for each RDA to determine the overall 265 \nimportance of remaining environmental variables in influencing response variables. Analyses of 266 \nresponse variable-flow alteration relationships were performed separately for each year, 267 \ntaxonomic assemblage and sampling type (for macroinvertebrates). All significant hydrologic 268 \nalteration metrics are listed and defined in Table 2. Percent variance explained in assemblage-269 \nenvironment relationships was examined by comparing eigenvalues from RDA analysis in order 270 \nto test our second and third hypotheses. 271 \nResults 272 \nRTH Macroinvertebrates 273 \n In 2012, RTH macroinvertebrate response metrics were significantly related to alteration 274 \nof DH18, TA3, and RA3 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 2). RA3 was significantly reduced 275 \nrelative to expected values (Figure 2). Diversity, richness, and percent EPT were all negatively 276 \nrelated to alteration of TA3, while abundance and percent Chironomidae were positively related 277 \nto alteration of RA3 (Figure 2). Cumulative percent variance explained by flow alteration was 278 \n88.6% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 3). 279 \nIn 2013, RTH macroinvertebrate assemblages were significantly related to alteration of 280 \nMA22, FH3, DH23, and TH2 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 2). MA22 and FH3 were 281 \nsignificantly reduced relative to expected values (Figure 2). Diversity was positively related to 282 \nalteration of MA22, while percent Chironomidae was negatively related to alteration of MA22 283 \n \n14 \n \n(Figure 2). Percent EPT was positively related to alteration of TH2 (Figure 2). Cumulative 284 \npercent variance explained by flow alteration was 80.2% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 3). 285 \nIn RTH macroinvertebrate assemblages, no category of alteration metric stood out as 286 \nmost important. Of the seven important flow alteration metrics, two were duration, two were 287 \ntiming, one was magnitude, one was frequency, and one was rate of change (Table 2, Figure 2). 288 \nFour of seven metrics were high flow metrics, and the remaining three were average flow 289 \nmetrics. No metrics belonging to the low flow category were important. No metrics were 290 \nimportant in RTH macroinvertebrate assemblages in both years. One metric, DH23, was also an 291 \nimportant metric in QMH macroinvertebrate assemblages, and two others, MA22 and RA3, were 292 \nalso important metrics in fish assemblages (Table 2). 293 \nQMH Macroinvertebrates 294 \n In 2012, QMH macroinvertebrate response metrics were significantly related to alteration 295 \nof MA12, MH3, MH20, ML12, and FH11 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 3). MA12 and MH3 296 \nwere significantly reduced and ML12 significantly increased relative to expected values (Figure 297 \n3). Richness, diversity, and percent EPT were all negatively related to alteration of FH11, while 298 \npercent Chironomidae was positively related to alteration of MH3 (Figure 3). Cumulative 299 \npercent variance explained by flow alteration was 93.6% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 3). 300 \nIn 2013, QMH macroinvertebrate response metrics were significantly related to alteration 301 \nof MA29, MH17, FH4, FH5, and DH23 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 3), and MA29, MH17, 302 \nand FH4 were significantly reduced relative to expected values (Figure 3). Percent EPT was 303 \nnegatively related to alteration of FH5 and DH23, while alteration of both of these metrics were 304 \n \n15 \n \npositively related to percent Chironomidae (Figure 3). Cumulative percent variance explained 305 \nby flow alteration was 69.6% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 3). 306 \n In QMH macroinvertebrate assemblages, magnitude was the most important category of 307 \nalteration metric; six of the ten important alteration metrics belonged to this category (Table 2, 308 \nFigure 3). Frequency was the second most important category, with three of the ten. One metric 309 \nbelonged to the duration category. Seven of the ten metrics were high flow metrics, with three 310 \naverage flow and one low flow. No metrics were important in QMH assemblages in both years. 311 \nOne metric, DH23, was also important in RTH macroinvetebrate assemblages, and another, 312 \nFH11, in fish assemblages (Table 2). 313 \nCrayfish 314 \n In 2012, crayfish response metrics were significantly related to alteration of MA3, 315 \nMA32, MA33, DL18 and RA2 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 4). DL18, MA32, and MA33 316 \nwere significantly reduced and RA2 significantly increased relative to expected values (Figure 317 \n4). Diversity was negatively related to alteration of DL18, while total density and percent 318 \ngeneralist crayfish were positively related to alteration of RA2 (Figure 4). Cumulative percent 319 \nvariance explained by flow alteration was 88.6% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 3). 320 \nIn 2013, crayfish response metrics were significantly related to alteration of MA3, 321 \nMA21, DH1, and TH1 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 4). DH1 was significantly reduced and 322 \nTH1 significantly increased relative to expected values (Figure 4). Total Density was positively 323 \nrelated to alteration of DH1 while diversity was negatively related to alteration of MA21 (Figure 324 \n \n16 \n \n4). Cumulative percent variance explained by flow alteration was 90.7% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 325 \n3). 326 \nIn crayfish assemblages, magnitude was the most important category of alteration metric; 327 \nfive of the nine important alteration metrics between years belonged to this category (Table 2, 328 \nFigure 4). Of the remaining four important metrics, two belonged to the duration category, one 329 \nto the timing category, and one to the rate of change category. Six of the nine metrics were 330 \naverage flow metrics, with two high flows, and one low flow. One metric, MA3, was an 331 \nimportant metric in crayfish assemblages in both years. No specific metrics important to 332 \ncrayfish assemblages were important in other taxonomic groups (Table 2). 333 \nFish 334 \n In 2012, fish response metrics were significantly related to alteration of MA22, MA36, 335 \nFH1, FH2, FH8, and DH7 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 5). MA22, MA36, FH1, and FH2 336 \nwere significantly reduced relative to expected values (Figure 5). Diversity and richness were 337 \npositively related to alteration of MA22, FH2, and MA36 and negatively related to alteration of 338 \nDH7 (Figure 4). Percent intolerant fish was negatively related to alteration of FH1 and FH8 339 \n(Figure 5). Cumulative percent variance explained by flow alteration was 72.4% for Axis 1 and 340 \n2 (Table 3). 341 \nIn 2013, fish response metrics were significantly related to alteration of MH13, MH18, 342 \nFH11, DH7, and RA3 (RDA p<0.001, Table 2, Figure 5). MH18 was significantly increased and 343 \nRA3 significantly reduced relative to expected values (Figure 5). Percent intolerant fish was 344 \npositively related to alteration of MH18, total density negatively related to alteration of RA3, and 345 \n \n17 \n \nrichness and diversity negatively related to alteration of MH18 and DH7 (Figure 5). Cumulative 346 \npercent variance explained by flow alteration was 86.7% for Axis 1 and 2 (Table 3). 347 \nIn fish assemblages, magnitude and frequency were the most important categories of flow 348 \nalteration metrics; eight of the 11 important alteration metrics between years were in these two 349 \ncategories (Table 2, Figure 5). Of the remaining three important metrics, two belonged to the 350 \nduration category and one to the rate of change category. Eight of the 11 metrics were high flow 351 \nmetrics and the remaining three were in the average flow category. No metrics belonging to the 352 \nlow flow category were important. No metrics were important in fish assemblages in both years. 353 \nMA22 and RA3 were also important metrics in RTH macroinvertebrate assemblages and FH11 354 \nin QMH macroinvertebrate assemblages (Table 2). 355 \nAquatic Community 356 \n Considering all four assemblages over both years, 32 different metrics of hydrologic 357 \nalteration were significantly related to biological response metrics (Table 2). In order of 358 \nimportance, the five categories were ranked: magnitude (14), frequency (7), duration (6), timing 359 \n(3) and rate of change (2). In terms of average, low, and high flows, metrics relating to alteration 360 \nof high flows were the most numerous (19), followed by average flows (11), with a much lower 361 \nnumber of important alteration metrics relating to low flows (2). All seven frequency metrics 362 \nand all but one of the duration metrics were related to high flows, while the majority of important 363 \nmagnitude metrics were related to average flows. The four most important specific categories 364 \nwere MA (8), FH (7), MH (5), and DH (5). Four specific alteration metrics were important in 365 \nmultiple assemblages: MA22, FH11, DH23, and RA3 (Table 2). 366 \n \n18 \n \nDiscussion 367 \n Our hypothesis that there would be temporal variation in flow alteration-ecology 368 \nrelationships was supported. The complete overlap of sites for macroinvertebrate collections 369 \nmakes it possible to draw temporal comparisons in flow alteration-ecological response 370 \nrelationships between the two years for macroinvertebrate assemblages. The contrast between 371 \nyears was most pronounced in the RTH samples, which may be because they are collected only 372 \nfrom riffles, the stream habitat most heavily affected by drought (Dekar and Magoulick, 2007; 373 \nChester and Robison, 2011). While we expected some differences between the years, it was still 374 \nsomewhat surprising to see no consistently important metrics between the two years in either 375 \nRTH or QMH collections. In some cases, there were seemingly different relationships between 376 \nresponse metrics. For example, RTH percent EPT was negatively related to alteration of TA3 377 \n(seasonal predictability of flooding) in 2012, whereas percent EPT was positively related to 378 \nalteration of TH2 (variability in Julian date of annual maximum) in 2013. However, in both 379 \ncases percent EPT was reduced with altered flow timing (discussed further below). It appears 380 \nthat alteration of flow timing is important for percent EPT, but specific relationships vary 381 \ntemporally. 382 \nNon-stationarity in environmental conditions can complicate our ability to formulate 383 \npredictable flow-ecology relationships and pose challenges for the implementation of e-flows 384 \nscience (Poff et al., 2010; Rolls et al., 2012; Katz and Freeman, 2015; Poff 2017; Lynch et al. 385 \n2018). As the hydrologic cycle is further altered by global climate change and the severity, 386 \nduration and frequency of droughts increases (Masson-Delmotte et al., 2013, Farjad et al., 2015) 387 \nit is crucial to incorporate strategies that realistically account for these phenomena when 388 \n \n19 \n \nimplementing e-flows science into management decisions (Poff 2017). One such strategy is to 389 \nfocus on resilience, the maintenance of processes and relationships that are robust and able to 390 \nmaintain integrity despite anticipated changes in environmental conditions (Poff 2017; Mazor et 391 \nal., 2018). 392 \nOur hypotheses that flow alteration-ecology relationships would be stronger during the 393 \ndrought year and that fish assemblages would show the strongest flow-alteration relationships, 394 \nhad much less support, and showed year- and taxa-dependent caveats. Relationship strength, as 395 \nindicated by cumulative percent variance explained in Axes 1 and 2 of the RDA’s, was greater 396 \nduring the drought year for benthic macroinvertebrate assemblages, but weaker for fish and 397 \ncrayfish, with the pattern reversed during the flood year. Fish assemblage relationships were 398 \nstronger than macroinvertebrates and lower than crayfish during the flood year, but weaker than 399 \nall other groups during the drought year. Overall, relationships were slightly stronger during the 400 \ndrought year than the flood year, and relationships were actually weaker in fish than in the 401 \nmacroinvertebrate assemblages. 402 \nWith respect to which categories of alteration metrics were most important, the 403 \nprominence of magnitude and frequency is of particular interest given that regional e-flows 404 \nstudies have suggested that magnitude of flow is an important influence on aquatic communities 405 \n(Monk et al., 2006; Armstrong et al., 2011; Kendy et al., 2012), while others have found that 406 \nfrequency of floods may be one of the most important determinants of community structure in 407 \nstreams (Dodds et al., 2004; Matthews et al., 2013; Matthews et al., 2014). Anthropogenic 408 \nalteration of streamflow magnitudes is a widespread phenomenon; in an assessment of 2,888 409 \nstreamflow monitoring sites throughout the conterminous U.S., Carlisle et al. (2010) found that 410 \n \n20 \n \nstreamflow magnitude was altered at 86% of assessed streams, and that diminished magnitudes 411 \nwere better predictors of biological integrity in both fish and macroinvertebrate assemblages than 412 \nother physical and chemical covariates. Reduction in high flow frequencies has also been linked 413 \nto a decrease in the ecological integrity of river systems (Ward and Stanford, 1995). The general 414 \ntrend in our study area in both magnitude and frequency metrics was towards reduction relative 415 \nto expected values. 416 \n Compared to alteration of high and average flows, alteration of low flows appeared to be 417 \na considerably less important influence on biota in Groundwater Flashy streams in the Ozark 418 \nHighlands; only two of 32 important metrics across years and assemblages were low flow 419 \nrelated. Although both floods and droughts act as major hydrologic disturbances in stream 420 \necosystems and can exert significant influence on biota (Lake, 2000), the alteration of low-flow 421 \nhydrology has been relatively less studied than that of high flows (Rolls et al., 2012). In the 422 \npresent study, we focused on Groundwater Flashy streams, one of the most common flow 423 \nregimes in the Ozark Highlands (Leasure et al., 2016), but it is likely that in other flow regimes, 424 \nalteration of low flows may be more important. Different natural flow regimes within the same 425 \nregion may be more or less susceptible to particular forms of flow alteration, which is the reason 426 \nthat flow regime classification is a crucial step in the assessment of hydrologic alteration (Poff et 427 \nal., 2010). The seven distinct flow regimes in the Ozark Highlands can be divided into three 428 \nbroad categories – groundwater, runoff, and intermittent streams (Leasure et al., 2016). Runoff 429 \nand intermittent flow regimes are categorized by more frequent low flow spells and lower base 430 \nflows than groundwater streams; it may be that low flow metrics play a greater role in the life 431 \nhistory of biota in these streams and therefore alteration of those metrics would have greater 432 \nimpact. Poff (1992) suggested that perennial runoff and intermittent streams may be more 433 \n \n21 \n \nstrongly affected by alteration of low flows and groundwater streams more affected by alteration 434 \nof high flows; the latter at least appears to be reflected in the present study. 435 \n RTH macroinvertebrate assemblages differed in key ways from the others in this study. 436 \nThis was the only group in which magnitude was not the most important category of flow 437 \nalteration metric. It was also the only assemblage in which no flow alteration category was 438 \nclearly more prominent than the others. Predictability of flooding is thought to be critically 439 \nimportant to macroinvertebrate assemblages. Fritz and Dodds (2005) found that streams with 440 \nlow flow predictability had consistently lower macroinvertebrate taxa richness than those with 441 \ngreater predictability. Alteration of the variability in high flow timing (TH2) was also related to 442 \nboth percent EPT taxa and abundance in 2013. Predictable timing of floods may be very 443 \nimportant in aquatic macroinvertebrates that rely on life-history adaptations to avoid 444 \ndisturbances rather than escaping on a per-event basis, particularly taxa that require gill 445 \nrespiration as juveniles but have an aerial adult stage, e.g. EPT taxa (Lytle, 2008). In the present 446 \nstudy, predictability of flooding (TA3) was altered towards higher predictability and was 447 \nnegatively related to all response variables in 2012. Likewise, alteration towards increased 448 \nvariability in high flow timing (TH2) was positively related to increased percent EPT taxa and 449 \nabundance in 2013, but it is important to note that TH2 alteration ranges from strongly negative 450 \nto slightly positive so percent EPT and abundance increased with less altered (i.e., more normal) 451 \nTH2. Therefore, in both cases percent EPT was reduced with altered flow timing. It appears that 452 \nalteration of flow timing, regardless of direction, may negatively influence RTH 453 \nmacroinvertebrate assemblages in these systems. 454 \n \n22 \n \nUnlike RTH assemblages, QMH assemblages showed a pattern consistent with fish and 455 \ncrayfish assemblages with respect to the prominence of magnitude alteration metrics. 456 \nInterestingly, QMH assemblages show more of an affinity with fish than crayfish assemblages in 457 \nterms of the importance of high flow frequency (FH). Relationships between response variables 458 \nin the two years were more consistent in QMH than RTH samples. It is possible that the 459 \ninclusion of pool and run habitats, which act as refuges for macroinvertebrates during summer 460 \ndrying (Chester and Robson, 2011), may have somewhat ameliorated the effects of drought in 461 \n2012 in QMH compared to RTH samples. Temporal variation in relationships was also apparent 462 \nin QMH assemblages, however, as no individual flow alteration metrics were significant in both 463 \nyears. General trends among QMH macroinvertebrate assemblages in the region include 464 \nreduction of important metrics relating to magnitude and variability in average and high flows, as 465 \nwell as frequency and duration of high flows. These may have a variety of effects on QMH 466 \nmacroinvertebrate assemblages in the region; in a few cases, some trends may actually offset 467 \neach other. For example, decreasing magnitude of average flows (MA12) may lead to a decrease 468 \nin richness which could be somewhat ameliorated by the trend toward decreasing flood 469 \nfrequency (FH11) (Fig. 3). Examining synergies and indirect effects of flow alteration on 470 \necosystem structure and function could be a fruitful avenue for future research. 471 \nIt should be noted that care should be taken in interpreting the flow alteration-ecology 472 \nrelationships because flow can be negatively or positively altered or unaltered. Therefore, 473 \nnegative alteration can be positively correlated with a response variable. For example, in Fig. 2 474 \nthe alteration of FH3 is positively related to Taxa Richness and negatively related to 475 \n%Chironomidae, but FH3 is negatively altered and high FH3 values are near normal (i.e., 476 \n \n23 \n \nFH3=0). Therefore, Taxa Richness is greater and %Chironomidae is reduced when FH3 is near 477 \nnormal. 478 \n Magnitude alteration was the most important category in both crayfish and fish 479 \nassemblages. The two strongly differ in one important way, however – the lack of any important 480 \nfrequency alteration metrics in crayfish assemblages. The ability of crayfish species in the 481 \nregion to more fully utilize the hyporheic zone during dry periods (DiStefano et al., 2009; Larson 482 \net al., 2009) may make them less dependent on frequent high flow events than fish assemblages; 483 \nthis may lessen the impact that alteration of flood frequency has on them. MA3, variability in 484 \ndaily flows, was a consistently important metric in crayfish assemblages in the region, as it was 485 \nselected in both 2012 and 2013 despite a lack of overlap between sites. The relationship between 486 \nalteration of flow variability and density was similar to that observed in fish assemblages in this 487 \nstudy, i.e. the relationship between fish density and MA36 in 2012, and is also supported by 488 \nprevious studies of flow variability and fish density (Craven et al., 2010). 489 \n In fish assemblages, the association between richness and diversity and alteration of 490 \nvariability in both average flow magnitude (MA22 and MA36) and high flow frequency, (FH2) 491 \nis supported by studies relating hydrologic variation to North American stream fishes (Ward, 492 \n1998; Niu et al., 2012, but see McGarvey, 2014). The trend towards reduction of these metrics 493 \nin our study area could be associated with an overall decline in richness and diversity of stream 494 \nfishes in the region. While previous studies have suggested that aquatic biodiversity is often 495 \nlower in modified or disturbed streams than in those with relatively intact natural flow regimes 496 \n(Ward and Stanford, 1995; Gehrke et al., 1999), it has been an ongoing challenge for stream 497 \necologists to unravel the direct effects of flow alteration from multiple associated stressors that 498 \n \n24 \n \noften accompany development in watersheds, e.g. land-use factors or declining water quality 499 \n(Bunn and Arthington, 2002). Our study provides evidence that alteration of specific flow 500 \nmetrics can influence richness and diversity in stream biota. 501 \n Overall, our results show the importance of magnitude and high flow alteration to stream 502 \nassemblages in these systems. However, patterns related to alteration of specific flow metrics 503 \nbetween years or between taxa are less obvious. It is possible that redundancy between tested 504 \nflow metrics used to explain similar ecological processes may be responsible for lack of patterns 505 \nwith specific flow metrics. Future research that builds on established flow alteration-biological 506 \nresponse relationships in a way that specifically elucidates functional links would be worthwhile. 507 \n Another caveat to consider is that, while relationships appeared to vary between years, 508 \nour ability to detect these relationships may have varied as well based on the very different 509 \nsampling conditions between drought and flood years. Detection probability of freshwater fish 510 \ncan vary strongly between samples taken at different flow magnitudes, and this may influence 511 \ninferences based on fish community-flow relationships (Pregler et al., 2015, Gwinn et al., 2016). 512 \nSimilar factors may affect detection probability in benthic macroinvertebrate assemblages 513 \n(Meador et al., 2011; Wisniewski et al., 2013). Furthermore, while hydrology plays a major role 514 \nin structuring aquatic assemblages, it is heavily interrelated to many other factors, including 515 \ngeomorphology, land-use, and water quality; the ecological effects of hydrologic alteration are 516 \nbest examined within the context of this suite of factors (Poff et al., 2006; McManamay and 517 \nFrimpong, 2015, Lynch, et. al 2018). Finally, while we examined flow alteration-ecology 518 \nrelationships in a predominant flow regime (Groundwater Flashy streams) in the Ozark 519 \nHighlands, these relationships may strongly differ in other flow regimes even within the same 520 \n \n25 \n \necoregion (Poff, 1992; Poff et al., 2010; Leasure et al., 2016). Future studies of flow alteration-521 \necology relationships focused on other flow regimes would help to form a more complete picture 522 \nof the impact of hydrologic alteration on stream communities. 523 \nConclusions 524 \nFlow alteration appears to be an important influence on community structure in 525 \nGroundwater Flashy streams in the Ozark Highlands. The most important categories of 526 \nalteration influencing stream biota were MA, FH, MH, and DH. The fact that three of these 527 \ncategories were high flow-related suggests the overall importance of high flows as a determinant 528 \nof community structure and composition in these systems. Of the 32 important metrics across 529 \nyears and assemblages, 19 were significantly altered relative to expected values. General 530 \npatterns, such as the importance of magnitude and high flow alteration, were apparent across 531 \nassemblages and may be useful to managers and stakeholders attempting to conserve freshwater 532 \necosystems in the region. However, key differences between taxonomic groups, as well as 533 \ntemporal variation in relationships, suggest that a complex suite of flow metrics should be 534 \nconsidered for effective conservation of stream communities related to flow alteration. 535 \nEnvironmental flows concepts are increasingly finding traction in regions across the world 536 \n(Belmar et al., 2011; Buchanan et al., 2013; Rolls and Arthington, 2014; O'Brien, et al., 2017; 537 \nZhang et al., 2017), but could be enhanced by a better understanding of complexity with respect 538 \nto interactions between temporal variation, disturbance, and taxa-dependent response differences. 539 \nAcknowledgements 540 \n \n26 \n \n We thank the Arkansas Game and Fish Commission and State Wildlife Grants for 541 \nfunding. We thank our project partners: Dr. John Jackson, Jim Petersen, Jeff Quinn, and Ethan 542 \nInlander. We thank Brian Haggard and the staff of the Arkansas Water Resources Water Quality 543 \nLab and Eric Cummings and the University of Arkansas Ecological Engineering Group. We 544 \nthank Brad Austin and Scott Longing for assistance with laboratory methods and Chris 545 \nMiddaugh for GIS assistance. Finally we thank the many people who have assisted us with data 546 \ncollection in the field and laboratory: Joseph Baecher, Alexa Ballinger, Brooke Beckwith, Tom 547 \nBoersig, Lindsey Bruckerhoff, Kelsey Deal, Alan Edmundson, Matt Gideon, Alexandra Hooks, 548 \nAlexa Kusmik, Philip Malone, Brianna Olsen, Kayla Sayre, Jared Schluterman, Keith Waters, 549 \nKaitlyn Werner, Shannon Wiley, and Tyler Williams. This study was done under the auspices of 550 \nUniversity of Arkansas IACUC protocol #11018. The authors have no conflicts of interest 551 \nrelated to this research. Any use of trade, firm, or product names is for descriptive purposes only 552 \nand does not imply endorsement by the U.S. Government. 553 \n 554 \n \n27 \n \nLiterature Cited 555 \nAcuna V., Munoz, I., Giorgi, A., Omella, M., Sabater, F., & Sabater, S. (2005) Drought and post 556 \ndrought recovery cycles in an intermittent Mediterranean stream: structural and 557 \nfunctional aspects. Journal of the North American Benthological Society 24: 919-933. 558 \n 559 \nArmstrong, D.S., Richards, T.A., & Levin, S.B. (2011) Factors influencing riverine fish 560 \nassemblages in Massachusetts. US Geological Survey Scientific-Investigations Report 561 \n2011-5193, 58p http://pubsusgsgov/sir/2011/5193/ 562 \n 563 \nArthington, A.H., Bunn, S.E., Poff, N.L., & Naiman, R.J. (2006) The challenge of providing 564 \nenvironmental flow rules to sustain river ecosystems. Ecological Applications 16: 1311-565 \n1318. 566 \n 567 \nBarbour, M.T., Gerritsen, J., Snyder, B.D., & Stribling, J.B. (1999) Rapid Bioassessment 568 \nProtocols for Use in Streams and Wadeable Rivers: Periphyton, Benthic 569 \nMacroinvertebrates and Fish, Second Edition EPA 841-B-99-002. US Environmental 570 \nProtection Agency; Office of Water; Washington, DC 571 \n 572 \nBeche, L.A., Connors, P.G., Resh, V.H., & Merenlender, A.M. (2009) Resilience of fishes and 573 \ninvertebrates to prolonged drought in two California streams. Ecography 32: 778-788. 574 \nBelmar, O., Velasco, J., & Martinez-Chapel, F. (2011) Hydrological classification of natural 575 \nflow regimes to support environmental flow assessments in intensively regulated 576 \nMediterranean rivers, Segura River Basin (Spain). Environmental Management 47: 992-577 \n1004. 578 \nBeniston, M., Stephenson, D.B., Christensen, O.B., Ferro, C.A.T., Frei, C., Goyette, S., 579 \nHalsnaes, K., Holt, T., Jylha, K., Koffi, B., Palutikof, J., Scholl, R., Semmier, T., & 580 \nWoth, K. (2007) Future extreme events in European climate: an exploration of regional 581 \nclimate model projections. Climate Change 81: 71-95. 582 \nBuchanan, C., Moltz, H.L. N., Haywood, H.C., Planner, J.B., & Griggs, A.N. (2013) A test of 583 \nthe ecological limits of hydrologic alteration (ELOHA) method for determining 584 \nenvironmental flows in the Potomac River basin, USA. Freshwater Biology 58: 2632-585 \n2647. 586 \nBunn, S.E. & Arthington, A.H. (2002) Basic principles and ecological consequences of altered 587 \nflow regimes for aquatic biodiversity. Environmental Management 30: 492-507. 588 \nCarlisle, D.M., Wolock, D.M., & Meador, M.R. (2010) Alteration of streamflow magnitudes and 589 \npotential ecological consequences: a multiregional assessment. Frontiers in Ecology and 590 \nthe Environment 9: 264-270. 591 \n 592 \n \n28 \n \nChester, E.T., Matthews, T.G., Howson, T.J., Johnston, K., Mackie, J.K., Strachan, S.R., & 593 \nRobson, B.J. (2014) Constraints upon the response of fisha dn crayfish to environmental 594 \nflow releases in a regulated headwater stream network. PLoS One 2014; 9(3): e91925. 595 \ndoi:10.1371/journal.pone.0091925 596 \n 597 \nChester, E.T., Robson, B.J. (2011) Drought refuges, spatial scale and reocolonization by 598 \ninvertebrates in non-perennial streams. Freshwater Biology 56: 2094-2104. 599 \nChristensen, M.R. Graham, M.D., Vinebrooke, R.D., Findlay, D.L., Paterson, M.J., & Turner, 600 \nM.A. (