oRxiv preprint \n \n \n14 \n \nEcological Indicators, 61, pp.159-169. 491 \nMartin, G.K., Adamowicz, S.J. and Cottenie, K., 2016. Taxonomic resolution based on DNA barcoding affects 492 \nenvironmental signal in metacommunity structure. Freshwater Science, 35(2), pp.701-711. 493 \nMatthaei, C.D., Peacock, K.A. and Townsend, C.R., 1999. Patchy surface stone movement during disturbance 494 \nin a New Zealand stream and its potential significance for the fauna. Limnology and Oceanography, 44(4), 495 \npp.1091-1102. 496 \nMenezes, S., Baird, D.J. and Soares, A.M., 2010. Beyond taxonomy: a review of macroinvertebrate trait ‐497 \nbased community descrip tors as tools for freshwater biomonitoring. Journal of Applied Ecology, 47(4), 498 \npp.711-719. 499 \nMiyake, Y . and Akiyama, T., 2012. Impacts of water storage dams on substrate characteristics and stream 500 \ninvertebrate assemblages. Journal of Hydro-Environment Research, 6(2), pp.137-144. 501 \nNakagawa H., and Y . Takemon. 2015. Length -mass relationships of macro -invertebrates in a freshwater 502 \nstream in Japan. Aquat Insects. 36:53-61. 503 \nPorazinska D. L., W. Sung, R. M. Giblin -Davis, and W. K. Thomas. 2010. Reproducibility of read numbers in 504 \nhigh-throughput sequencing analysis of nematode community composition and structure. Molecular 505 \nEcology Resources. 10:666-676. 506 \nRatnasingham S., and P . D. N. Hebert. 2007. BOLD: The Barcode of Life Data System: Barcoding. Molecular 507 \nEcology Notes. 7:355-364. 508 \nRosen M. J., B. J. Callahan, D. S. Fisher, and S. P. Holmes. 2012. Denoising PCR -amplified metagenome 509 \ndata. BMC Bioinformatics. 13: 83. 510 \nSerrana, J.M., Yaegashi, S., Kondoh, S., Li, B., Robinson, C.T. and Watanabe, K., 2018. Ecological influence 511 \nof sediment bypass tunnels on macroinvertebrates in dam -fragmented rivers by DNA metabarcoding. 512 \nScientific reports, 8(1), p.10185. 513 \nShearer, K. A., Hayes, J. W., Jowett, I. G., and Olsen, D. A. (2015). Habitat suitability curves for benthic 514 \nmacroinvertebrates from a small New Zealand river. New Zealand journal of marine and freshwater 515 \nresearch, 49(2), 178-191. 516 \nShendure J., and H. Ji. 2008. Next-generation DNA sequencing. Nature Biotechnology. 26: 1135-1145. 517 \nSoininen, E.M., Gauthier, G., Bilodeau, F., Berteaux, D., Gielly, L., Taberlet, P ., Gussarova, G., Bellemain, E., 518 \nHassel, K., Stenøien, H.K. and Epp, L., 2015. Highly overlapping winter diet in two sympatric lemming 519 \nspecies revealed by DNA metabarcoding. Plos One, 10(1), p.e0115335. 520 \nSweeney, B.W., Battle, J.M., Jackson, J.K. and Dapkey, T., 2011. Can DNA barcodes of stream 521 \nmacroinvertebrates improve descriptions of communi ty structure and water quality? Journal of the North 522 \nAmerican Benthological Society, 30(1), pp.195-216. 523 \nTang C. Q., F. Leasi, U. Obertegger, A. Kieneke, T. G. Barraclough, and D. Fontaneto. 2012. The widely used 524 \nsmall subunit 18S rDNA molecule greatly underestimates true diversity in biodiversity surveys of the 525 \nmeiofauna. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. 109:16208-16212. 526 \nThompson, B.W., Riddle, M.J. and Stark, J.S., 2003. Cost-efficient methods for marine pollution monitoring at 527 \nCasey Station, East Antarctica: the choice of sieve mesh-size and taxonomic resolution. Marine Pollution 528 \nBulletin, 46(2), pp.232-243. 529 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \nnot certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprint (which wasthis version posted October 5, 2018. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/436162doi: bioRxiv preprint \n \n \n15 \n \nThudi M., Y . Li, S. A. Jackson, G. D. May, and R. K. Varshney. 2012. Current state -of-art of sequencing 530 \ntechnologies for plant genomics research. Briefings in Functional Genomics. 11:3-11. 531 \nVoelkerding K. V., S. A. Dames, and J. D. Durtschi. 2009. Next-generation sequencing: from basic research 532 \nto diagnostics. Clinical Chemistry. 55:641-658. 533 \nWatanabe K., M. T. Monaghan, Y . Takemon, and T. Omura. 2008. Biodilution of heavy metals in a stream 534 \nmacroinvertebrate food web: evidence from stable isotope analysis. Science of the Total Environment. 535 \n394:57-67. 536 \nYu D. W., Y . Ji, B. C. Emerson, X. Wang, C. Ye, C. Yang, and Z. Ding. 2012. Biodiversity soup: metabarcoding 537 \nof arthropods for rapid biodiversity a ssessment and biomonitoring. Methods in Ecology and Evolution. 538 \n3:613-623. 539 \nZhou X., Y . Li, S. Liu, Q. Yang, X. Su, L. Zhou, M. Tang, R. Fu, J. Li, and Q. Huang. 2013 . Ultra-deep 540 \nsequencing enables high -fidelity recovery of biodiversity for bulk arthropod samples without PCR 541 \namplification. GigaScience. 2:4. 542 \nZimmermann, J., Glöckner, G., Jahn, R., Enke, N. and Gemeinholzer, B., 2015. Metabarcoding vs. 543 \nmorphological identification to assess diatom diversity in environmental studies. Molecular Ecology 544 \nResources, 15(3), pp.526-542. 545 \nAuthor Contributions 546 \nJ. Serrana analyzed and interpreted the data, and wrote the manuscript. Y . Miyake conducted field survey, 547 \nsample collection, and mor phological identification. M. Gamboa conducted molecular laboratory work. K. 548 \nWatanabe designed the whole study, and interpreted the data. All authors did critical revision and approved 549 \nthe final version of the manuscript. 550 \nCompeting Interests 551 \nThe authors declare no competing interests or other interests that might be perceived to influence the results 552 \nand/or discussion reported in this paper. 553 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \nnot certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprint (which wasthis version posted October 5, 2018. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/436162doi: bioRxiv preprint \n \n \n16 \n \nFigures 554 \n 555 \nFigure 1. Map of the ten study sites along the Shigenobu River in Shikoku Island, Japan. The altitude of each site is provided in Table 1. 556 \n 557 \nFigure 2. Correlation between the log sample abundance (morphologically -identified taxa/morpho-taxa) and log 454-read abundance 558 \n(metabarcoding-identified taxa) of all sites showing analysis including (left) and excluding (right) false positive and false negative detection. 559 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \nnot certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprint (which wasthis version posted October 5, 2018. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/436162doi: bioRxiv preprint \n \n \n17 \n \n 560 \nFigure 3. Principal coordinate analysis (PCoA) ordinations of beta diversity estimates ( coords scaled by percent explained ): Binary-561 \nJaccard (Procrustes: p-value = 0.038) (top) and Bray-Curtis dissimilarity (Procrustes: p-value < 0.001) (bottom). 562 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \nnot certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprint (which wasthis version posted October 5, 2018. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/436162doi: bioRxiv preprint \n \n \n18 \n \n 563 \n 564 \nFigure 4. Redundancy analysis (RDA) ordination plot of the morphologically -identified taxa (left) and metabarcoding-identified macroinvertebrate species (right) constrained by environmental variables. 565 \nBoth global models were found to be statistically significant following a permutation ANOVA test (p = 0.035, p < 0.001 respectively). “Chl-a” stands for chlorophyll a, “CPOM” for the coarse particulate 566 \norganic matter, “DO” for dissolved oxygen, “EC” for conductivity, “TN” for total nitrogen and “TP” for total phosphorus. 567 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \nnot certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprint (which wasthis version posted October 5, 2018. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/436162doi: bioRxiv preprint \n \n \n19 \n \nTables 568 \nTable 1. Summary of altitude, DNA metabarcoding data, and species richness across the ten study sites. 569 \nStudy \nsite \nSample \nSize \nRead Processing Metabarcoding \nMorpho-\ntaxa \nMeta-taxa \n(Match) \nFalse \nPositive \nFalse \nNegative \nCorrelation Analysis \nRaw \nReads \nReads Mapped \nto OTUs OTUs Arthropod Readsa Meta-species Meta-taxa Pr > Fb R² R \nSite 01 603 18,867 10,593 14 4,338 13 3 5 2 1 3 0.03 0.73 0.85 \nSite 02 228 31,300 15,371 15 11,704 14 6 7 3 3 4 0.03 0.48 0.69 \nSite 03 208 8,593 4,941 24 2,520 20 8 9 5 3 4 0.10c 0.24 0.49 \nSite 04 551 15,033 4,797 14 3,987 12 9 12 5 4 7 0.49c 0.03 0.18 \nSite 05 1,347 12,598 5,792 28 3,534 25 9 14 6 3 8 0.08c 0.19 0.44 \nSite 06 1,051 31,291 14,848 30 11,837 24 10 16 6 4 10 0.01 0.34 0.58 \nSite 07 539 17,510 7,304 24 4,957 20 12 20 8 4 12 0.21c 0.06 0.25 \nSite 08 2,740 10,626 4,944 26 2,802 21 12 25 8 4 17 0.01 0.25 0.5 \nSite 09 843 8,784 5,106 28 1,462 22 9 22 6 3 16 0 0.39 0.62 \nSite 10 166 10,906 6,206 15 500 13 6 27 5 1 22 0.03 0.17 0.41 \nTotal 8,276 165,508 79,902 53 47,641 44 20 45 11 9 34 0.001 0.18 0.43 \na arthropod sequence match at >97% identity and e-value >10-5 identified to the species level in GenBank. \nb including false positive and false negative detection. \nc no significant correlation with p-value > 0.05. \n570 \n.CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International licenseavailable under a \nnot certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made \nThe copyright holder for this preprint (which wasthis version posted October