  "content": "Emergy-based indicators for evaluating ecosystem health: A case study of three benthic ecosystem networks influenced by coastal upwelling in northern Chile (SE Pacific coast) - PMC\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main content\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n An official website of the United States government\n \n Here's how you know \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Here's how you know \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Official websites use .gov \n \n A\n .gov website belongs to an official\n government organization in the United States.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Secure .gov websites use HTTPS \n \n A lock (\n \n \n Lock \n \n Locked padlock icon\n \n \n \n ) or https:// means you've safely\n connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive\n information only on official, secure websites.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Log in \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Dashboard\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Publications\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Account settings\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Log out\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search…\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search NCBI\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Primary site navigation\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Logged in as:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Dashboard\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Publications\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Account settings\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Log in\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search PMC Full-Text Archive \n \n \n \n \n Search in PMC \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Journal List\n \n \n \n \n \n \n User Guide\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n PERMALINK \n \n \n \n \n Copy \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n As a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with,\n the contents by NLM or the National Institutes of Health. \n Learn more:\n PMC Disclaimer \n |\n \n PMC Copyright Notice\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Ecol Indic . Author manuscript; available in PMC: 2019 Dec 1. \n \n Published in final edited form as: Ecol Indic. 2018 Dec;95:379–393. doi: 10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.07.055 \n \n \n Search in PMC \n Search in PubMed \n View in NLM Catalog \n Add to search \n \n Emergy-based indicators for evaluating ecosystem health: A case study of three benthic ecosystem networks influenced by coastal upwelling in northern Chile (SE Pacific coast) \n Fernando Berrios \n Fernando Berrios \n \n a Programa de Doctorado en Ciencias Aplicadas, mención sistemas marinos costeros, Universidad de Antofagasta, PO Box 170, Antofagasta Chile \n \n b Centro de Investigaciones Costeras Universidad de Atacama (CIC-UDA), Avenida Copayapu, Copiapo, Chile \n Find articles by Fernando Berrios \n \n \n a, b, * , Daniel E Campbell \n Daniel E Campbell \n \n c USEPA, ORD, NHEERL, AED, Narragansett, RI, USA \n Find articles by Daniel E Campbell \n \n \n c , Marco Ortiz \n Marco Ortiz \n \n d Instituto de Antofagasta (IA), Instituto de Ciencias Naturales AvH, Facultad de Ciencias del Mar y Recursos Biológicos. Universidad de Antofagasta \n Find articles by Marco Ortiz \n \n \n d \n \n \n Author information \n Copyright and License information \n \n \n \n \n a Programa de Doctorado en Ciencias Aplicadas, mención sistemas marinos costeros, Universidad de Antofagasta, PO Box 170, Antofagasta Chile \n \n b Centro de Investigaciones Costeras Universidad de Atacama (CIC-UDA), Avenida Copayapu, Copiapo, Chile \n \n c USEPA, ORD, NHEERL, AED, Narragansett, RI, USA \n \n d Instituto de Antofagasta (IA), Instituto de Ciencias Naturales AvH, Facultad de Ciencias del Mar y Recursos Biológicos. Universidad de Antofagasta \n \n * Author correspondence: fberriosrobles@gmail.com \n \n \n PMC Copyright notice \n \n PMCID: PMC6171125  NIHMSID: NIHMS1504166  PMID: 30294245 \n \n The publisher's version of this article is available at Ecol Indic \n \n Abstract \n It has been hypothesized that ecosystem health describes the state in which all processes operating within an ecosystem are functioning at a level of optimum efficiency to maximize system empower. In this study, systems analysis of networks and information flows is used within this definition of ecosystem health to assess the condition of the benthic ecosystems in three coastal bays in northern Chile. These highly productive ecosystems are characterized by the inflow of cold, nutrient-rich waters of low oxygen concentration derived from coastal upwelling of deep waters and the interruption of upwelling flow during El Niño events when warmer waters with higher oxygen and lower nutrient concentrations enter these coastal systems. Also, these ecosystems support important artisanal benthic fisheries and are affected by industrial activities in the coastal zone. Energy Systems Theory (EST) and Emergy Analysis 1 (EA) were applied to quantify the health of these benthic ecosystems and evaluate differences in their structure, organization and functional capacities, which are related to their emergy signatures. The marked dominance of these benthic ecosystems by nitrate from upwelling resulted in unbalanced emergy signatures, suggesting less development and system diversity compared to other coastal ecosystems with more balanced emergy signatures. Macro-descriptors and network properties, such as emergy-based ascendency and the quality-adjusted Shannon diversity index 2 were highest for Mejillones Bay, followed by Antofagasta and then Tongoy Bay. The Average Mutual Information (AMI) index adjusted for energy quality 3 and the emergy-based A/C (ascendency to capacity) ratio 4 , were higher for Tongoy Bay, suggesting functional differences in health among the three ecosystems. Thus, the emergy-based macro descriptors and other indicators used in our analysis indicate that the benthic networks examined have different structural and functional characteristics that lead to different characterizations of their states of health. As a result of this complexity, management policies should be implemented within a systemic context for analysis that considers all the factors determining the relative health of each ecosystem. \n Keywords: Ecosystem health, Emergy, Transformity, Upwelling, Benthic ecosystems, Coastal zone, Network analysis 1. Introduction \n The health of the coastal ecosystems of northern Chile is important, because as a consequence of waters rich in nutrients derived from coastal upwelling, they are some of the most productive ecosystems on Earth ( Strubs et al., 1998 ). In addition, this high nutrient, high productivity state alternates with a lower nutrient, lower productivity state that dominates in years when the coastal upwelling circulation is curtailed by the El Niño Southern Oscillation (ENSO). A further complicating factor in assessing the health of these systems is that the frequency and intensity of upwelling regimes alternate on a multidecadal time scale driven by changes in global wind patterns ( Oviatt, et al., 2015 ). In recent years, health assessments of the benthic systems in Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays have been made using trophic network models, which integrate the magnitudes of production and consumption of ecosystem compartments, the flow of energy through the network and the information distributed among model compartments ( Ortiz and Wolff, 2002 ; Ortiz et al., 2015 ). \n Odum (1996) pointed out that the different flows of available energy, expressed in terms of their caloric value, are not equivalent in terms of their ability to perform work. Therefore, we realized that to obtain a complete understanding of the condition of these benthic ecosystems that is grounded in thermodynamics, it was necessary to complement the conclusions gained from our ascendency evaluation using the results of an emergy evaluation of the same systems. \n Ecological studies have been pursued in at least two ways: (1) by using a reductionist approach, where each variable is studied separately based on experimental designs, population models, and models of simple interactions (e.g., predation, competition, parasitism, etc.), and (2) by using multi-species network analysis, which attempts to capture the interactions of relevant species, and from which emerge global properties that are impossible to observe and quantify in isolation ( Jørgensen, 1992 ; Odum, 1994 ). In this approach, thermodynamic principles (i.e., the first and second laws of thermodynamics and the maximum [em] power principle, which has been proposed as the fourth law of thermodynamics, [ Lotka, 1922 a , b; Odum, 1996 ])) are considered to be key principles in explaining the growth and development of ecosystems. Growth has been described as an increase in size (e.g., of the energy flow) and development as an increase in organization of the system (i.e., complexity), which is independent of size ( Ulanowicz, 1986 , 1997 ; Jørgensen et al., 2000 ). In thermodynamic terms, the increase in these two properties means that ecosystems gain order and move away from the state of thermodynamic equilibrium, and these thermodynamic properties of natural systems can be used to assess the ecological condition (e.g., the health) of ecosystems ( Jørgensen et al., 2005 ). The ecological health of ecosystems could be defined as the ability to maintain or restore optimal system function, i.e., the optimum efficiency for maximum power ( Odum, 1996 ; Campbell, 2000 ), when confronted with a disturbance, which can be used as an indicator of the ecological sustainability of the system ( Costanza and Mageau, 1999 ; Campbell, 2000 ; Lu et al., 2015 ). \n Lotka (1922a , b and 1925 ) introduced the maximum power principle (MPP) to explain the development of self-organization in systems far from thermodynamic equilibrium. The MPP indicates that the systems that will prevail are those that capture and use (available) energy to develop configurations that maximize the inflow of useful energy (power). However, Odum (1994 , 1996) showed that if energy flows alone are considered, in some cases, systems with lower levels of organization can have a greater flow of energy than those with greater organizational complexity. In this case, the MPP implies that the less organized systems will prevail in evolutionary competition over those with higher levels of organization, which is not consistent with observations and models of self-organization ( Odum, 1996 ; Campbell, 2000 ; Campbell et al., 2009 ; Li et al., 2013 ). The determination of emergy ( Odum, 1996 ) requires tracing the solar energy invested in the production of goods and services within ecosystems or economies. Therefore, Odum (1996) re-defined the MPP as the Maximum Empower Principle (MEmP) to evaluate the relative contribution of quality–adjusted power (i.e., emergy flow) to the general welfare of the system taking into account the different hierarchical levels in ecosystems. In this sense, Campbell (2000) defined ecological integrity as the emergent ecosystem structure that maximizes network empower within the context of the emergy signature received by the ecosystem, and ecosystem health as the state in which all of the processes operating within and among the components of the system are functioning at the optimum efficiency for maximum empower. Although other frameworks for evaluating ecosystem health might be chosen, Campbell (2000) provides an approach grounded in Energy Systems Theory and its metrics, such as emergy indices, which can be used as a basis for evaluating the “health” or functional integrity of any system within the context of the system’s energy and emergy signatures, including the spatial and temporal variability of the suite of forcing functions that comprise the signature. \n Odum (1988 , 1994 and 1996) criticized the use of energy values alone to assess the relative importance of each component of the ecosystem with respect to its function, because the flow of available energy alone does not represent the true contribution of the process or component to the functionality or “health” of the ecosystem as a whole. Odum (1996) pointed out that the different flows of available energy, expressed in terms of their caloric values, are not equivalent in terms of their ability to perform work. Therefore, as mentioned above, we realized that to obtain a complete thermodynamic understanding of the condition of these benthic ecosystems, it was necessary to complement the conclusions gained from our earlier systemic evaluations using the results of an emergy evaluation. Emergy evaluation is a methodological procedure to determine the relative efficacy of flows and storages of material, energy, and information within a system based on the normalization of all types of available energy (i.e., energy with the potential to do work) to equivalent units as solar joules. A solar emjoule, the unit of emergy, is a solar equivalent joule that has been used-up in the past to produce the various products and services in a system. This method integrates additional information into the assessment, such as the contribution of inputs of renewable and nonrenewable energy (i.e., from the forcing functions) that until now have not been used to assess the health of coastal-benthic marine ecosystem networks. \n The intention of the present study was to obtain a thermodynamic understanding of the conditions supporting these three benthic coastal ecosystems and to provide additional information on and to further develop indicators of ecosystem health by building on the framework for defining measuring and interpreting ecosystem health and ecological integrity proposed in Campbell (2000) . Specifically, we wanted to 1) evaluate the ecological health of the three benthic ecosystems examined in this study using emergybased indicators that capture the effects of environmental variations, such as the El Niño Southern Oscillation (ENSO) and other factors; 2) determine the influence of these environmental factors on the growth and development of the benthic ecosystems; 3) use emergy diversity indicators to evaluate and assess the health of these benthic networks and 4) use emergy-based ascendency ( Christensen, 1994 ) and its related indices to assess the network properties of the benthic ecological systems of Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays. Furthermore, we wanted to compare macroscopic indices of the structure, organization and functional capacity of these three ecosystem networks, which are representative in their various aspects of both natural and human influences on the coastal embayment ecosystems of northern Chile. The results of our study will also be compared and contrasted with methods that use more traditional approaches based on energy and mass and others that use multiple criteria to evaluate ecosystem health. 2. Methods \n 2.1. Emergy signature evaluation \n Forcing functions are sources of energy, matter and information from the larger system that characterize and drive ecological systems and that enter a system by flowing across its boundaries ( Campbell, 2000 ). The major environmental forcing functions that constitute the energy signature of the three bays are solar radiation, wind, tide, waves and upwelled nitrate, which were estimated for each coastal system ( Table 1 and Figure 3 ). The emergy signature was estimated by multiplying each available energy source by the appropriate transformity. The transformities calculated were corrected to the new baseline, which is 12E+24 seJ y −1 ( Brown et al., 2016 ; Campbell, 2016 ). It is important to clarify that Brown et al. (2016) explain that the geobiosphere baseline can only be determined by establishing equivalences between the different exergy inputs to the geobiosphere and thus it is expressed in solar equivalent joules (seJ), whereas emergy is determined by a track-sum method starting with the baseline and is expressed in emjoules (sej) as defined in section 3.2 below. The emergy base required to support primary production and consumption of species or functional groups of the ecological networks in each bay was estimated per square meter (i.e., empower density) using a procedure developed by Campbell (2004) to obtain estimates for the transformities of energy flows within the benthic ecosystems. \n Table 1. \n Energy sources supporting the development of the Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays, the energy and emergy signature of the bays. Empower base and Empower density of the benthic production systems. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Mejillones Bay \n Energy (J/y) \n Transformity (sej/J) \n Emergy (sej/y) \n Fraction of emergy inflow \n Fraction of energy inflow \n \n \n \n (1) Solar radiation 1 \n \n 1.18E+17 \n 1.00E+00 \n 1.18E+17 \n 0.1% \n 98.4% \n \n \n (2) Wind 2 \n \n 1.79E+15 \n 1.23E+03 \n 2.20E+18 \n 2.6% \n 1.5% \n \n \n (3) Wave 3 \n \n 9.07E+12 \n 3.08E+04 \n 2.79E+17 \n 0.3% \n 0.0% \n \n \n (4) Tide 4 \n \n 1.05E+14 \n 3.09E+04 \n 3.25E+18 \n 3.8% \n 0.1% \n \n \n (5) Nitrogen Upwelled 5 \n \n 3.50E+12 \n 2.28E+07 \n 7.98E+19 \n 93.2% \n 0.0% \n \n \n \n Area (m 2 ) \n 1.95E+07 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Empower base of benthic productive systems \n 8.31E+19 \n (sej/y) \n \n \n \n \n Empower density base of benthic productive systems \n 4.26E+12 \n (sej/m 2 /y) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Antofagasta Bay \n Energy (J/y) \n Transformity (sej/J) \n Emergy (sej/y) \n Fraction of emergy inflow \n Fraction of energy inflow \n \n \n \n (1) Solar radiation 1 \n \n 3.21E+17 \n 1.00E+00 \n 3.21E+17 \n 0.16% \n 98.3% \n \n \n (2) Wind 2 \n \n 4.86E+15 \n 1.23E+03 \n 5.97E+18 \n 3.01% \n 1.5% \n \n \n (3) Wave 3 \n \n 5.26E+14 \n 3.08E+04 \n 1.62E+19 \n 8.16% \n 0.2% \n \n \n (4) Tide 4 \n \n 2.86E+14 \n 3.09E+04 \n 8.83E+18 \n 4.45% \n 0.1% \n \n \n (5) Nitrogen Upwelled 5 \n \n 7.33E+12 \n 2.28E+07 \n 1.67E+20 \n 84.21% \n 0.0% \n \n \n \n Area (m 2 ) \n 5.30E+07 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Empower base of benthic productive systems \n 1.76E+20 \n (sej/y) \n \n \n \n \n Empower density base of benthic productive systems \n 3.32E+12 \n (sej/m 2 /y) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Tongoy Bay \n Energy (J/y) \n Transformity (sej/J) \n Emergy (sej/y) \n Fraction of emergy inflow \n Fraction of energy inflow \n \n \n \n (1) Solar radiation 1 \n \n 1.48E+17 \n 1.00E+00 \n 1.48E+17 \n 0.24% \n 98.3% \n \n \n (2) Wind 2 \n \n 2.41E+15 \n 1.23E+03 \n 2.96E+18 \n 4.73% \n 1.6% \n \n \n (3) Wave 3 \n \n 1.36E+14 \n 3.08E+04 \n 4.20E+18 \n 6.71% \n 0.1% \n \n \n (4) Tide 4 \n \n 8.09E+13 \n 3.09E+04 \n 2.50E+18 \n 3.99% \n 0.1% \n \n \n (5) Nitrogen Upwelled 5 \n \n 2.31E+12 \n 2.28E+07 \n 5.28E+19 \n 84.33% \n 0.0% \n \n \n \n Area (m 2 ) \n 2.73E+07 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Empower base of benthic productive systems \n 5.54E+19 \n (sej/y) \n \n \n \n \n Empower density base of benthic productive systems \n 2.03E+12 \n (sej/m 2 /y) \n \n \n \n Data sources for transformities \n \n \n Open in a new tab \n \n \n 1 By definition. \n \n \n 2 Transformity of wind energy dissipated in the boundary layer. New calculation Campbell and Erban (2017) . \n \n \n 3 New transformity for waves calculated relative to the new baseline, Campbell (2016) . \n \n \n 4 New transformity for the tide from the geobiosphere baseline paper. Brown et al. (2016) . \n \n \n 5 Transformity calculated relative to the new baseline, Brown et al. (2016) . \n \n Figure 3. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Energy sources supporting the development of the three benthic ecosystems arranged on the abscissa in order of increasing transformity. (a), (b) and (c) The energy signatures of the Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays, respectively, from Berrios et al. (2017) . (d), (e) and (f) the emergy signatures of Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays, respectively. 2.2. Emergy evaluation of benthic ecological networks \n The energy flows of the ecological variables were obtained from the input parameters and flow matrices of mass balanced trophic models built by Ortiz and Wolff (2002) and Ortiz et al. (2015) that represent the interspecific relationships (prey–predator) in the benthic systems in a regime of coastal upwelling, from which the transformities were estimated using the procedure developed by Li et al. (2010) . \n The mass flows of the ecological variables were converted to energy using factors to convert from wet weight to dry weight (x 0.2) and from dry weight to energy (x 5 kcal/g dry weight and x 4183 J/kcal). In this way, the interaction (consumption) matrix was expressed in energy terms (joules m −2 year −1 ) from which the transformities were estimated using the fundamental equation that defines emergy as the product of a flow of available energy (J E ) and the quality of that flow (transformity, tr) where [Emergy sej y −1 = Available Energy J y −1 x Transformity sej J −1 ]. The method of Li et al. (2010) allowed us to structure the equations to determine each energy flow of the ecosystem following the rules of emergy algebra, before estimating the transformities. This step, called preconditioning, is designed to prevent mistakes related to double counting in determining the value of the emergy base required for the production of each flow (see Appendix A , for the case of the Mejillones model). The first step in determining the transformities is to structure the equations from the energy systems diagram for each system ( Figure 2a , b , c ) using the energy flows present and considering the rules of emergy algebra ( Odum, 1996 ; Brown and Herendeen, 1996 ). The transformity of an energy flow may vary according to its designation as a co-product or as a split within the ecosystem network. A co-product can be identified when the two outputs are not substitutable and in this case the entire emergy input to the production process is required for each output. On the other hand, a split occurs when the outputs of a process are substitutable and in this case the emergy input to the production of the products can be divided in proportion to the available energy on each arm of an output pathway ( Odum, 1996 ). The implication is that these two products are functionally similar and therefore substitutable. However, sometimes in complex systems, there is not enough biological-ecological information to make an accurate determination that a particular flow is a split or a co-product ( Campbell, 2004 ). For example, where benthic macro fauna and filter feeders ingest different living and non-living biological materials, assimilate organic matter into biomass and produce feces (detritus) and pseudo feces, which can also be used as food, distinguishing co-products and splits within a complex network of food and waste might be difficult. If products have different properties in the way that they are used in the ecosystem, they would be classified as co-products, but if the products can be used in the same manner, e.g., as food, then classification as a split is perhaps more appropriate. In this regard, we simplified the problem by assuming that there are no co-product modules in the benthic ecosystem network, based on the procedure of Brown et al. (2006) and Zarbá and Brown (2015) . This simplification allowed us to more easily estimate the emergy indicators within the ecosystem networks. \n Figure 2a. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Energy Systems Language Model of Mejillones Bay showing the emergy base (underlined numbers) for the benthic ecosystem and the available energy flows along each pathway in J m −2 y −1 and the emergy in sej m −2 y −1 required for that pathway (bold). Figure 2b. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Energy Systems Language Model of Antofagasta Bay showing the emergy base (underlined numbers) for the benthic ecosystem and the available energy flow along each pathway in J m −2 y −1 and the emergy in sej m −2 y −1 required for that pathway (bold). Figure 2c. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Energy Systems Language Model of Tongoy Bay showing the emergy base (underlined numbers) for the benthic ecosystem and the available energy flow along each pathway in J m −2 y −1 and the emergy in sej m −2 y −1 required for that pathway (bold). The time boundary for making estimates of the flows of energy is one year. The hydrodynamic model of Moraga-Opazo et al. (2011) was used to estimate the volume of upwelled water entering Tongoy Bay per year, and the models proposed by Escribano and Hidalgo (2001) and Marin et al. (2003) were used to estimate the volume of upwelled water entering Antofagasta and Mejillones Bays, respectively. 2.3. Network analysis: Definition of eight macro-descriptors of ecosystem network properties \n Eight macroscopic indexes or descriptors were calculated to evaluate and compare the health of each benthic ecosystem. These indices are described below. \n (1). Average transformity (sej/J). \n The traditional ecological efficiency measure is calculated as the output energy of a process expressed as a percent of the input energy in ecological systems ( Lindeman, 1942 ). The usual value of the Lindeman efficiency for transfers between trophic levels is about 10%. Transformity (tr) is the reciprocal of the Lindeman efficiency. The transformity is relevant, because it is a universal measure of the position of ecological variables within their system network and of systems within the hierarchy of natural processes of the biosphere. The transformity is the ratio of the emergy input to the available energy output of a process, and it is a measure of the efficiency of production processes, when the same output is produced. In this case, higher values of transformity imply a greater emergy input or a lower available energy output, and therefore the process will be less efficient compared with other production processes that are making the same amount and quality of a product using less emergy. We interpreted transformities through comparison to average values for the indicator that had been established in past studies ( Ulgiati and Brown, 2002 ). (2). Quality-adjusted diversity. \n The energy quality-adjusted Shannon diversity index of the system ( Brown et al., 2006 ), was used to evaluate the ecological organization (diversity) of the benthic ecosystems. This index replaces the probability of observing component i in a system of n elements with the magnitude of the Emergy Importance Value (EIV), which is defined as follows (Eq.1):\n \n \n E I V i = N P i * t r i ∑ j N P j * t r j \n (1) \n \n which is the proportion of total system emergy flow (sej yr −1 ) allocated to each component. This formulation allowed the calculation of the ecosystem Shannon diversity index as follows ( Eq. 2 ):\n \n \n ∑ i = 1 n E I V i * l o g 2 ( E I V i ) \n (2) \n \n where NP i is the net production and tr i is the transformity of component i in a system of n components. The relative diversity (RD %) relates the Shannon diversity index of the system to the theoretical maximum diversity for the number of species present. It was calculated with the purpose of comparing the relative states of development of the different systems compared to the maximum possible for the species richness observed. (3). Total emergy system throughput (sej m −2 y −1 ). \n Total emergy system throughput (TEST) is the sum of the magnitudes of all emergy flows on all network pathways occurring in the system, and it describes the vigor or size of a system. This descriptor represents a measure of a system’s total metabolism, including consumption, flow into detritus, respiration and exports to the fishery. (4). The average mutual information (emergy adjusted flowbits). \n The average mutual information (AMI) index ( Ulanowicz, 1986 ) adjusted for energy quality was estimated to assess the complexity of the system in terms of its flows according to Ortiz et al. (2015) , in which it was established that a greater value of AMI implies less complexity. AMI measures the number and diversity of weighted interactions amongst the system components as follows:\n \n \n AMI = ∑ i , j T i j / T * log ( T i j * T / T i * T j ) \n (3) \n \n where the T ij is the emergy flow out of group i and going into group j , T is the sum of the all emergy fluxes in the system, T i is the emergy flow i and T j is the emergy flow j . (5). Emergy adjusted ascendency (emergy adjusted flowbits). \n The emergy-based relative ascendency (EBA) is an index that integrates both the size (TEST) and organization of a system (AMI). Ascendency has been related to system stability ( Christensen, 1995 ) and maturity ( Ulanowicz and Abarca-Arenas, 1997 ). (6). Emergy-based development capacity (emergy adjusted flow bits). \n The emergy-based Development Capacity (C) quantifies the upper limits to Ascendency, it is calculated as:\n \n \n C = ∑ i , j T i j * log T i j T \n (4) \n (7). Emergy-based overhead (emergy adjusted flow bits). \n The emergy overhead (Ov) is the difference between Ascendency and the Development Capacity, and indicates the multiplicity of information pathways such as: system input, export, dissipation and internal exchange (redundancy). This may closely relate to the capacity to withstand perturbations ( Christensen, 1995 ). (8). Emergy-based A/C in percent. \n The emergy-based A/C (ascendency to capacity) ratio describes the network efficiency. \n The A/C ratio has been suggested as a maturity index ( Costanza and Mageau, 1999 ). Additionally, it can also be used as an indicator of ecosystem development ( Kaufman and Borrett, 2010 ; González et al., 2016 ) and as a measure of a system’s ability to resist disturbances ( Ulanowicz, 1986 , 1997). \n The unit for the (EBA), (C) and (Ov) measures is ‘flowbits’, which is given by the product of a flow (e.g., g·m −2 y −1 ) and the information in bits on the pathways given in the formulae for the relevant macrodescriptors. The ‘bit’ is an information unit defined as the amount of uncertainty associated with a single binary decision ( Christensen et al., 2005 ). 2.4. Index for assessing health condition: The ratio between the Expected and Observed Emergy Throughput (EOET) \n In addition to the 8 macro-descriptors of the ecosystem network defined above, we used a 9 th index as a more direct measure of ecosystem health. The ratio between the Expected and Observed Emergy Throughput (EOET) from Brown et al. (2006) was used to evaluate the condition of the benthic ecosystems, since healthier ecosystems are expected to have greater evenness of flows across all components. This definition is applied within the context of a given set of forcing functions or emergy signature for a system as described in Campbell (2000) . The EOET is a fractional diversity index (observed diversity/ maximum diversity) as described by Eq. 3 :\n \n \n EOET i = ( TET / N ) / ( NP i * t i ) \n (5) \n \n where TET is total emergy throughput of the whole system and N is the number of biotic compartments. The resulting values were natural log transformed to linearize the response, where values larger than 0 indicate a component less important than the expected value of 1 and vice versa ( Brown et al., 2006 ). 3. Results \n 3.1. Emergy signature \n The calculations and data sources for the energy signature of the coastal zones of the three bays are documented in Appendix A of Berrios et al. (2017) . The energy signature shows that solar and wind energy comprised the highest percentage (> 99%) of the energy inflows ( Table 1 and Figure 3a, b, c ), while in the emergy signature, the upwelled NO 3 -N contributed > 84% of the inflow in all three coastal systems ( Table 1 and Figure 3d, e, f ). The emergy bases for the Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bay ecosystems are comprised of the emergy input in the sun, tide and upwelled nitrogen. Wave energy was not included, because the systems examined are deeper than the surf zone and wind emergy supports the upwelling circulation pattern and thus would double count the nitrogen inflow, if included. The renewable empower density was higher in Mejillones Bay (4.26E+12sej m- 2 y −1 ) than in Antofagasta Bay (3.32E+12sej m −2 y −1 ), which was higher than Tongoy Bay (2.03E+12sej m −2 y −1 ) ( Table 1 ). 3.2. Emergy evaluation of benthic ecological networks \n The network transformities are presented in Figure 5 and in Table 2 . Phytoplankton had the lowest transformities (ranging between 6.35E+04 sej J −1 and 1.36E+05 sej J −1 ) of all primary producers and was more efficient than the brown, red and green macroalgae in all three bays. Macroalgal transformities ranged from 2.32E+05 sej J −1 to 1.96E+06 sej J −1 ( Table 2 ). In the second trophic level, Zooplankton are the most efficient users of resources in all three bays (3.65E+05, Mejillones; 1.64E+05, Antofagasta; 2.02E+05 sej J −1 , Tongoy Bay). The scallop Argopecten purpuratus (4.67E+05, Mejillones; 1.84E+05, Antofagasta; 3.27E+05 sej J −1 ,Tongoy Bay) is nearly as efficient as Zooplankton and it is also the second most efficient organism in using resources at the second trophic level in all three bays. Sea Urchin Species (SUS) is the least efficient user of resources at the second trophic level in Mejillones and Antofagasta Bays, 1.10E+6 and 1.79E6 sej J −1 , respectively, but it did not appear in Tongoy Bay where the gastropod, Tegula sp. (2.02 E+5 sej J −1 ) takes its place. Between trophic levels 2 and 3, Other Sea Star Species (OSS) are more efficient in Mejillones and Antofagasta Bays, 4.17E+06 sej J −1 and 4.02 E+06 sej J −1 , respectively, but do not appear in the list of organisms for Tongoy Bay where the tunicate Pyura chilensis is the most efficient organism, 3.37 E+05 sej J -1 . The least efficient organisms at this level are the crabs Cancer spp. in Mejillones Bay, 1.62E+06 sej J −1 , and in Antofagasta Bay, 1.23E+06 sej J −1 and Paraxanthus barbiger in Tongoy Bay, 1.40E+06 sej J -1 . At trophic level 3 and above the cephalopod, Octopus vulgaris, is least efficient, 2.15E+06 sej J −1 , and the sea star, Luidia magallanica, 2.06E+06 sej J −1 , is the most efficient user of resources in Antofagasta Bay, whereas the L. magallanica , 2.27E+06sej J −1 , is the only trophic level 3 organism appearing in Mejillones Bay. Tongoy Bay has the greatest number of trophic level 3 organisms and within this bay the sea star, Megenaster gelatinosus, 1.08E+06 sej J −1 , (level 3.12) is clearly more efficient at exploiting resources than is the snail Xanthochorus cassidiformis , 1.34E+06 sej J −1 , (level 3.12) making it the most efficient user of resources at trophic level 3 in Tongoy Bay. \n Figure 5. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Computed transformity values for Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy bays in rank order by Trophic Level. The names of the functional groups given by number in Table 2 . Table 2. \n Transformities of ecosystem flows in Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays based on estimates of the energy of net primary production and the annual emergy inflow to each bay. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Species/functional groups \n Trophic level \n Energy (J/m 2 y) \n Emergy (sej/m 2 y) \n Transformity (sej/J) \n \n \n \n (A) Mejillones Bay \n \n \n \n \n \n \n (1) Phytoplankton \n 1 \n 3.14E+07 \n 4.26E+12 \n 1.36E+05 \n \n \n (2) Lessonia trabeculata \n 1 \n 3.12E+06 \n 1.11E+12 \n 3.56E+05 \n \n \n (3) Chlorophyta \n 1 \n 8.79E+05 \n 1.73E+12 \n 1.96E+06 \n \n \n (4) Rhodophyta \n 1 \n 1.00E+07 \n 3.56E+12 \n 3.56E+05 \n \n \n (5) Phaeophyta \n 1 \n 1.38E+06 \n 3.24E+11 \n 2.34E+05 \n \n \n (6) Detritus \n 1 \n 9.57E+06 \n 1.10E+13 \n 1.15E+06 \n \n \n (7) Zooplankton \n 2 \n 6.03E+06 \n 2.20E+12 \n 3.65E+05 \n \n \n (8) Argopecten purpuratus \n 2 \n 5.46E+05 \n 2.55E+11 \n 4.67E+05 \n \n \n (9) Aulacomya Ater \n 2 \n 8.54E+05 \n 6.84E+11 \n 8.01E+05 \n \n \n (10) Tagelus dombeii \n 2 \n 2.80E+06 \n 1.89E+12 \n 6.74E+05 \n \n \n (11) Choromytilus chorus \n 2 \n 1.27E+06 \n 7.32E+11 \n 5.78E+05 \n \n \n (12) Gari solida \n 2 \n 1.06E+06 \n 8.05E+11 \n 7.59E+05 \n \n \n (13) Tegula spp. \n 2 \n 4.63E+06 \n 5.11E+12 \n 1.10E+06 \n \n \n (14) Small Epifauna Herbivore (SEH) \n 2 \n 1.08E+06 \n 8.53E+11 \n 7.88E+05 \n \n \n (15) Sea urchin species (SUS) \n 2 \n 1.11E+04 \n 1.22E+10 \n 1.10E+06 \n \n \n (16) Other Filter feeders (OFF) \n 2.12 \n 1.29E+05 \n 1.51E+11 \n 1.17E+06 \n \n \n (17) Other Sea Star Species (OSS) \n 2.45 \n 5.33E+05 \n 2.22E+11 \n 4.17E+05 \n \n \n (18) Thais chocolata \n 2.48 \n 6.49E+02 \n 6.87E+08 \n 1.06E+06 \n \n \n (19) Large Epifauna (LE) \n 2.73 \n 3.13E+05 \n 3.96E+11 \n 1.26E+06 \n \n \n (20) Small Epifauna Carnivore (SEC) \n 2.88 \n 7.10E+05 \n 1.05E+12 \n 1.48E+06 \n \n \n (21) Cancer spp. \n 2.94 \n 2.40E+05 \n 3.89E+11 \n 1.62E+06 \n \n \n (22) Luidia magallanica \n 3.11 \n 4.70E+03 \n 1.07E+10 \n 2.27E+06 \n \n \n (B) Antofagasta Bay \n \n \n \n \n \n \n (1) Phytoplankton \n 1 \n 5.23E+07 \n 3.32E+12 \n 6.35E+04 \n \n \n (2) Lessonia trabeculata \n 1 \n 1.95E+06 \n 1.42E+12 \n 7.30E+05 \n \n \n (3) Chlorophyta \n 1 \n 5.23E+05 \n 6.33E+11 \n 1.21E+06 \n \n \n (4) Rhodophyta \n 1 \n 9.21E+05 \n 8.31E+11 \n 9.02E+05 \n \n \n (5) Phaeophyta \n 1 \n 9.26E+05 \n 7.68E+11 \n 8.29E+05 \n \n \n (6) Detritus \n 1 \n 1.35E+07 \n 6.91E+12 \n 5.13E+05 \n \n \n (7) Zooplankton \