.27E+05 \n \n \n (9) Mulinia sp. \n 2 \n 3.19E+05 \n 1.33E+11 \n 4.18E+05 \n \n \n (10) Calyptraea trochiformis \n 2 \n 4.33E+05 \n 2.06E+11 \n 4.75E+05 \n \n \n (11) Tegula sp. \n 2 \n 6.65E+05 \n 5.81E+11 \n 8.74E+05 \n \n \n (12) Taliepus sp. \n 2.18 \n 9.25E+03 \n 9.77E+09 \n 1.06E+06 \n \n \n (13)Infauna \n 2.21 \n 1.84E+06 \n 1.36E+12 \n 7.35E+05 \n \n \n (14) Pyura chilensis \n 2.4 \n 4.52E+05 \n 1.53E+11 \n 3.37E+05 \n \n \n (15) Small Epifauna (SE) \n 2.8 \n 4.67E+05 \n 4.25E+11 \n 9.09E+05 \n \n \n (16) Paraxanthus barbiger \n 2.89 \n 6.58E+04 \n 9.20E+10 \n 1.40E+06 \n \n \n (17) Luidia magallanica \n 3.09 \n 7.07E+03 \n 1.08E+10 \n 1.53E+06 \n \n \n (18) Xanthochorus cassidiformis \n 3.12 \n 2.36E+04 \n 3.16E+10 \n 1.34E+06 \n \n \n (19) Megenaster gelatinosus \n 3.12 \n 1.99E+05 \n 2.14E+11 \n 1.08E+06 \n \n \n (20) Heliaster helianthus \n 3.18 \n 4.88E+03 \n 6.21E+09 \n 1.27E+06 \n \n \n (21) Cancer porteri \n 3.3 \n 2.05E+04 \n 4.20E+10 \n 2.05E+06 \n \n \n (22) Cancer polyodon \n 3.35 \n 1.26E+05 \n 2.57E+11 \n 2.05E+06 \n \n \n (23) Cancer coronatus \n 3.36 \n 3.87E+04 \n 6.14E+10 \n 1.59E+06 \n \n \n (24) Large Epifauna (LE) \n 3.47 \n 7.25E+04 \n 1.41E+11 \n 1.95E+06 \n \n \n \n Open in a new tab 3.3. Network analysis: emergy system diversity index and emergy-based relative ascendency \n Figure 2 and Table 3(a) present the outcomes of the emergy analysis and benthic ecosystem network analyses of the three bays. With regard to the system structure and network properties, the magnitudes of the average transformity (9.28E+05 sej/J), average mutual information (2.048 bits) adjusted for energy quality, and A/C (63.08) were highest in the benthic ecosystem of Tongoy Bay. In the case of the quality-adjusted Shannon diversity (76 %), total emergy system throughput (3.55E+13 sej m −2 y −1 ), emergy-based relative ascendency (4.69E+13 flowbits), emergy capacity (1.16E+14 flowbits) and emergy overhead (6.92E+13 flowbits), the Mejillones benthic ecosystem had higher magnitudes. The Antofagasta Bay system had the same value of quality-adjusted Shannon diversity (75 %) as that of Tongoy Bay, but the latter had the lowest values of total emergy throughput,1.23E+13 sej m −2 y −1 ; emergy-based relative ascendency, 2.52E+13 flowbits; emergy capacity, 3.99E+14 flowbits and emergy overhead, 1.47E+13 flowbits ( Table 3a ). \n Table 3. \n (a) Macroscopic properties (indices) quantifying system development and organization based on the emergy system diversity index and emergy-based ascendency and percentage change (Δ %). (b) Macroscopic properties derived from Ascendency based on network analysis of biomass flows for Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n (a) Emergy network flow indices based on Ecopath \n Mejillones 1 \n \n Antofagasta 1 \n \n Tongoy 1 \n \n Bays \n Δ % \n Bays \n Δ % \n \n \n \n Total system throughput (sej m −2 y −1 ) \n 3.55E+13 \n 2.38E+13 \n 1.23E+13 \n M-A \n 32.89% \n M-T \n 65.39% \n \n \n Ascendency adjusted (Total) Flowbits \n 4.69E+13 \n 2.99E+13 \n 2.52E+13 \n M-A \n 36.21% \n M-T \n 46.31% \n \n \n Capacity Adjusted (Total) Flowbits \n 1.16E+14 \n 7.92E+13 \n 3.99E+13 \n M-A \n 31.83% \n M-T \n 65.65% \n \n \n Overhead (Ov) Adjusted Flowbits \n 6.92E+13 \n 4.93E+13 \n 1.47E+13 \n M-A \n 28.87% \n M-T \n 78.73% \n \n \n AMI Adjusted (bits) \n 1.321 \n 1.255 \n 2.048 \n T-M \n 55.10% \n T-A \n 63.21% \n \n \n A/C (%) \n 40.36 \n 37.8 \n 63.08 \n T-A \n 36.01% \n T-M \n 40.12% \n \n \n Average transformity (sej/J) \n 9.14E+05 \n 8.60E+05 \n 9.28E+05 \n A-M \n 5.90% \n A-T \n 7.36% \n \n \n AMI adjusted (bits) \n 1.321 \n 1.255 \n 2.048 \n A-M \n 4.97% \n A-T \n 38.73% \n \n \n N per m −2 \n \n 4.09E+12 \n 3.15E+12 \n 1.93E+12 \n M-A \n 29.88% \n M-T \n 111.59% \n \n \n N as a fraction of total emergy \n 0.96 \n 0.95 \n 0.95 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Quality adjusted Diversity (bits) \n 3.38 \n 3.46 \n 3.45 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Theoretical maximum diversity (bits) \n 4.46 \n 4.64 \n 4.58 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Relative diversity (%) \n 75.70 \n 74.54 \n 75.15 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n (b) Ecopath network flow indices \n Mejillones 2 \n \n Antofagasta 2 \n \n Tongoy 3 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Total system throughput (g ww m −2 y −1 ) \n 29,429.9 \n 37,539.8 \n 20,593.9 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Ascendency (Total) Flowbits \n 34,395.1 \n 48,574.3 \n 25,092.2 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Capacity (Total) Flowbits \n 142,897.9 \n 170,237.0 \n 88,686.8 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overhead (Ov) Flowbits \n 108,353.1 \n 121,434.8 \n 62,101.9 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n AMI (Average Mutual Information) (bits) \n 1.371 \n 1.294 \n 1.218 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n A/C (%) \n 24.1 \n 28.5 \n 28.3 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Open in a new tab \n \n \n 1 This study \n \n \n 2 Ortiz et al. (2015) \n \n \n 3 Ortiz & Wolff (2002) \n \n 3.4. Index for assessing health condition: The ratio between the Expected and Observed Emergy Throughput (EOET) \n The expected and observed emergy throughput ratios are shown in Figure 4 . The variables that exhibited the highest magnitudes of EOET were the Other Sea Star Species (OSS), the sea star L. magallanica and Sea Urchin Species (SUS) in Mejillones Bay, the cephalopod O. vulgaris , the crab Cancer spp. and Large Epifauna (LE) in Antofagasta Bay, and the sea star L. magallanica , the X. cassidiformis and the crab Cancer porteri in Tongoy Bay. All components belong to higher trophic levels. The compartments with the lowest values of EOET in Mejillones Bay were the Detritus, Phytoplankton, the herbivorous snail Tegula spp. In the case of Antofagasta Bay, the Detritus, the bivalve T. pannosa and Phytoplankton had the lowest EOET. Finally, in Tongoy Bay Detritus, Phytoplankton and Infauna had the lowest values of this index. In all cases the ecosystem components with low EOET belong to lower trophic levels. \n Figure 4. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Expected/Observed Emergy Throughput (EOET) versus Transformity for the Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bay ecosystem networks. 4. Discussion \n The high productivity of the coastal zone of northern Chile has been associated with the process of coastal upwelling, which causes deeper waters, which are normally cold, rich in nutrients and of low oxygen concentration to ascend to the surface ( Strubs et al., 1998 ). It is important to emphasize that this high productivity may also be interpreted as due to an extremely rich convergence of natural energy sources especially the nitrogen supplied form from deep water, but also tides, waves, wind and solar energy similar to some other systems analyzed in the coastal zone ( Campbell, 2000 ; Brown and Bardi, 2001 ; Campbell, 2004 ). In this context, the Energy Systems theoretical framework from past studies was used to relate the trophic models developed for Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays ( Ortiz and Wolff, 2002 and Ortiz et al., 2015 ) to their basis in the inflowing environmental energies, with an emphasis on the effects of coastal upwelling. Emergy methods were applied to gain insights into benthic ecosystem health in the near shore coastal waters of Northern Chile by evaluating the physical basis for the structure, organization and functional capacity of the observed benthic ecosystem networks. \n The emergy signatures estimated for the three coastal bays had empower densities of renewable resources (2020E+9, 3320E+9, 4260E+9 sej m −2 y −1 ) that were higher than many ecological systems cited in Brown and Bardi (2001) , such as an estuary in Florida (388E+9 sej m −2 y −1 ), the Sea of Cortez in Mexico (39E+9 sej m −2 y −1 ) and Lake Okeechobee, Florida (1416E+9 sej m −2 y −1 ); but they are similar to that obtained for Newnans Lake, Florida (4434E+9 sej m −2 y −1 ) by Brandt-Williams (2000) , and lower compared to the macrotidal estuarine system of Cobscook Bay, Maine USA (9553 E+9 sej m −2 y −1 ) studied by Campbell (2004) . All emergy flows were converted to the same baseline before comparison. This finding demonstrates the relative importance of the coastal zone of northern Chile, which is dominated by coastal upwelling for much of the time, in supporting ecosystem productivity and organization when compared to other aquatic systems dominated by different sources of emergy, e.g., fresh water, a macrotidal estuary and a coastal sea. It is clear that the convergence of a combination of renewable energy sources determined by environmental, meteorological and oceanographic factors (sun, wind, tide and ocean currents), has created one of the world’s highest productivity zones within the confines of a narrow coastal shelf ( Marín et al., 2001 ). Recent studies have described these three bays, in terms of their physico-chemical and biological properties, as important and unique coastal systems in themselves ( Valle-Levinson et al., 2000 ; Rutllant and Montecino, 2002 ; Cerda et al., 2010 ; Valdes et al., 2011 , 2014 ). In this study, we take an integrated energy systems approach to understanding the network of interactions occurring between human beings and their environmental support systems, through evaluating the environmental conditions required to support different human activities in these coastal environments. To further integrate the combined effects of anthropogenic and natural processes on the bays, studies such as those performed by Ulgiati and Brown (2002) and Vassallo et al. (2009) are needed to estimate processes such as the volume or mass of air or water that is required to dilute anthropic emissions to the atmosphere and sewage effluents to coastal waters to estimate the carrying capacity of these bays for different human activities. In addition, studies aimed at evaluating the minimum environmental emergy needed to support aquaculture, similar to the Odum (2000) study of salmon aquaculture in British Columbia are needed to gain an integrated understanding of the environmental, ecological and economic aspects of scallop ( Argopecten pupuratus ), kelp ( Lessonia trabeculata ) and other aquaculture operations in northern Chile. \n Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays receive high NO 3 -N concentrations primarily via a natural process of upwelling and the transfer of these high nutrient waters into the nearshore zone by means of tidal exchange as hypothesized in this study and in Campbell (2004) , or perhaps through internal waves or tidal bores occurring with a semidiurnal frequency ( Vargas et al., 2004 ), rather than from municipal sewage, terrigenous particle transport (Marín et al., 1993) or non-point runoff like many other coastal ecosystems in the world ( Garside and Garside, 2004 ). The emergy signature of the three bays is dominated by upwelled nitrogen; therefore, we suggest that tidal exchange plays an important role in dispersing upwelled N from the circulating currents within the bays into the nearshore coastal waters where the benthic ecosystems that we evaluated are found. This could be an important mechanism for delivering high concentrations of nutrients to the shallow subtidal and intertidal communities inside of the three bays as reported by Campbell (2004) for Cobscook Bay. This marked dominance of nutrients from upwelling as seen in the emergy signatures of the 3 bays ( Figure 3 ) could constitute conditions that would support a less diverse biological system consistent with the less diverse relationships observed in Odum et al. (1974) for many other systems with unbalanced emergy signatures dominated by wastes. Coastal systems influenced by upwelling have been described as immature ( Christensen, 1995 ; Taylor et al., 2008 ) and consequently they should be less diverse ecosystem according to Odum (1969) . In contrast, Campbell (2004) observed high diversity of intertidal and subtidal fauna in Cobscook Bay; however, these systems are dominated by a balance of tidal effects that both increase the area of intertidal and subtidal ecosystems and bring more resources in the form of nutrients and food to support the growth and development of the macroalgae and benthic fauna found there. \n Analysis of ecosystem organization using the emergy signature in the coastal areas of northern Chile is complicated by the fact that these systems are subject to a natural condition, El Niño or the Southern Oscillation (ENSO), which induces changes in the emergy signature of the benthic ecosystems establishing an alternating or pulsing temporal pattern. As a consequence of this pattern, the biological diversity of these coastal systems tends to increase during El Niño events, as the product of incremental increases in oxygen and temperature ( Tarazona et al., 1996 ; Moreno et al., 2008 ). In terms of Energy Systems Theory, this change can be viewed as a consequence of changes in the emergy signature, which moves towards a more balanced profile (i.e., one with less frequent upwelling and the ascent of a water mass with different physical and chemical properties ( González et al., 1998 ). In addition, to this natural oscillation of the prevailing emergy signature of these coastal ecosystems, there are anthropic effects related to industrial activities and artisanal fisheries exploitation, which result in the inflow of high quality (high transformity) stressors such as pollutants and intense and unsustainable fishing, both of which have effects on the benthic ecosystems of the three bays to a greater or lesser degree. These additional aspects of the emergy signature of the benthic ecosystems in the 3 bays are not directly evaluated in this paper, where we have focused on the ecological characteristics of the upwelling regime. However, we interpret our results within the context of the general knowledge about El Niño events and their effects on the coastal ecosystems, as well as anthropic activities and their effects on these coastal systems. \n Brown and Ulgiati ( 2004 ) have suggested evaluating ecological health through examining changes in the emergy signature of different biotic and abiotic elements of the ecosystem. An analysis of changes in the prevailing emergy signature (i.e., the quantity and quality of inflows) to a system may be a first predictor to identify possible changes in the health of ecosystems, if as hypothesized, the emergy signature is a holistic identifier of the expected structures and functions manifested by the organization developed under its influence ( Campbell, 2000 ; Brown and Ulgiati, 2004). \n Figures 2 and 5 show that the pathway from phytoplankton to the filter feeder groups is more efficient in capturing and processing emergy than the pathway from macroalgae to the herbivores. The average transformity of phytoplankton (all bays) had similar values to that recorded in Prince William Sound, a coastal embayment ecosystem of Alaska ( Brown et al., 1993 ), but it was two orders of magnitude lower than the primary producers (i.e., phytoplankton and benthic diatoms) found in Cobscook Bay, a macrotidal estuary ( Campbell, 2004 ). The macroalgae of the bays had similar transformity to the macroalgae in Cobscook Bay ( Campbell, 2004 ). This indicates that the emergy signatures received by the three bays are effectively transferred into phytoplankton, when compared to Cobscook Bay, and they have similar properties to Cobscook Bay in the efficiency with which macroalgae are produced. Cobscook Bay is a relatively inefficient producer of phytoplankton, because of heavy tidal mixing of the water column and consequent light limitation, but it is a very effective producer of macroalgae due to high nutrient supply and tidal energy dissipated in the intertidal and subtidal zones ( Campbell, 2004 ). The three bays influenced by upwelling have better conditions for producing phytoplankton and equally good conditions for macroalgal production as those found in a macrotidal estuary, which has higher overall emergy inflows. \n It is relevant to indicate that the transfer efficiencies estimated for the network energy flows fell between 10 and 30%, which will influence the resulting quantity of energy transferred. In our analysis, as in the Cobscook Bay analysis, the input of emergy into the system through the primary producers depended on the production area and the trophic level assignments used. Both could explain why the emergy is inversely related to the amount of available energy at each trophic level, resulting in higher transformity top predators. A pattern that is consistent with the thermodynamic basis for energy transference ( Odum, 1996 ). \n The emergy-based macro-descriptors of ecosystem network properties calculated from the emergy evaluations of the three benthic coastal ecosystems are compared in Table 3(a) . This comparison shows that the ecological system of Mejillones Bay is operating closest to its maximum potential diversity (i.e., it has the highest values of the Shannon relative diversity index (RD %), although this index is approximately the same for all three systems. The magnitudes of the estimated total emergy system throughput (TEST), emergy-based ascendency (EBA), Capacity (C) and Overhead (Ov) ratios for the benthic systems of Mejillones Bay are higher than in the other two bays. In Antofagasta and Tongoy Bay these indices were lower than the comparable values in Mejillones Bay. In contrast, Tongoy Bay was somewhat less efficient in capturing and processing available energy and the emergy it delivers to the system (i.e., it had the highest value of average system transformity [Tr]). It is operating slightly farther from its maximum potential ecological diversity (RD %, 75.15%) than Mejillones Bay (RD% 75.7%), but Antofagasta Bay is further still from the maximum (74.54%). Also, Tongoy Bay is less complex in terms of emergy flow through the network (i.e., it had the highest adjusted-AMI, 2.048, which is 55% and 63% larger than in Mejillones and Antofagasta Bays, respectively. Furthermore, Tongoy Bay is a more developed system according to the emergy A/C ratio (i.e., it is closer to its maximum potential for development). Ascendency has reached 63.1% of Capacity in Tongoy Bay compared to 36.01% for Antofagasta and 40.12% for Mejillones Bays, meaning that Tongoy Bay may be less resistant to disturbance ( sensu \n Christensen, 1995 ) compared to Mejillones and Antofagasta Bays. In contrast, Antofagasta Bay appears to be the most efficient system in producing ecosystem organization (Tr is the lowest of the 3 bays, 5.9% lower that Mejillones and 7.31% lower than in Tongoy Bay) and it is a more complex ecosystem (i.e., it has an AMI 4.97% lower than Mejillones and 38.73% lower than Tongoy Bay). In the case of the three benthic ecosystems examined, Mejillones Bay had the highest value for the empower density supporting the benthic ecosystem, which is demonstrated by its higher values of throughput (TEST), which is a measure of a system’s size or vigor. Consequently, the degree of system development and maturity (EBA) was higher, and it had better resistance against disturbances (higher Ov). However, the internal dynamic properties of Mejillones Bay, in terms of efficiency (i.e., transformity), organization (i.e., AMI) and the state of system maturity (i.e., A/C) were not optimally organized in a manner that generates the maximum empower from the emergy signature compared to the other two bays. The patterns observed in Mejillones Bay may be explained, primarily, because the throughflow dominates the ascendency measure ( Christensen, 1994 ; Patten, 1995 ; Fath et al., 2001 ). In terms of the emergy signature of the 3 systems, Mejillones received 111.59% more nitrate nitrogen per meter square compared to Tongoy Bay and 29.88% more than Antofagasta Bay. This interpretation is validated by the strong influence of the oxygen minimal zone that has been observed in this bay to a depth of 20 m ( Guiñez et al., 2010 ), thereby constraining the respiration of organisms that comprise the benthic communities there ( Escribano and Hidalgo, 2000 ). The Shannon ecosystem diversity indices in the three bays affected by upwelling were higher than similar indices in the Florida Everglades, USA, a system evaluated by Brown et al. (2006) . These outcomes are relevant, because these bays might be expected to be more diverse as a consequence of the great magnitude of inputs to the empower base of the benthic productive systems, as demonstrated in this study and in comparison to other ecosystems ( Brown and Bardi, 2001 ). \n The three bays have very distinct ecological properties and one, Antofagasta Bay, is a semiclosed bay with an open mouth to the south exposed to winds from the south and southwest with greater habitat heterogeneity as a product of the rocky bottom and kelp forests allowing a greater abundance of other algae and invertebrate species ( Ortiz, 2008 ). Also, Antofagasta Bay contains the La Rinconada marine reserve. Mejillones Bay has a geographical configuration that protects it from the direct action of the winds that are responsible for the coastal upwelling there. This allows longer periods between mixing and re-stratification of the surface layer in this bay ( Marín and Olivares, 1999 ) and it is heavily influenced by industrial activities. Tongoy Bay has the same geographical configuration as Mejillones Bay and similar exposure to the wind, but it is dominated by artisanal fisheries rather than industrial activities. To a first order approximation, we may assume that these dominate characteristics of the three bays should be reflected in their observed properties on analysis. \n The pattern of the EOET showed that in these systems dominated by upwelled nutrients, the lower trophic level organisms were more abundant than expected in all three systems and that the higher trophic level organisms were less abundant than expected with trophic level 3 organisms, generally, showing the greatest difference. This pattern is perhaps not unexpected given that these systems are nutrient enriched as shown by their emergy signatures. The expected and observed emergy ratios that measure ecosystem health show a clear trend with regard to their transformities, which could be interpreted as unhealthy as a consequence of the losses in the upper trophic levels reported by Brown et al. (2006) . Antofagasta Bay presents a better health condition compared to Mejillones and Tongoy Bays, which is consistent with the values of the mean trophic level of the catch from Antofagasta (1.595), Mejillones (1.692) ( Ortiz et al., 2015 ) and Tongoy Bay (2.14) ( Ortiz and Wolff, 2002 ). \n In evaluating the network and structure of mass flows in these ecological systems: the magnitude of throughput (TEST), ascendency (A), capacity (C), overhead (Ov) and the ascendency to capacity ratio (A/C) reported by Ortiz and Wollf (2002) and Ortiz et al. (2015) were all higher in Antofagasta Bay than in the other two embayments, with the exception of the AMI ( Table 3b ). These results are based on the same trophic models of the three bays used in this study, but the results of the biomass analysis differ from those of the network emergy evaluation. It is relevant to emphasize that our intention in this analysis is to complement the conclusions gained from our earlier systemic evaluations by using emergy methods to reveal uncharacterized properties of these systems, (i.e., emergy signatures, system empower, empower density, process and system efficiency, controllability (e.g., the efficacy of feedbacks), among other emergy indicators). In this regard, our approach is consistent with that proposed by Jørgensen (1992) , who observed that we can obtain a more comprehensive overall picture of the ecosystem by use of a pluralistic view of models, instead of looking for the “best” model. This approach is taken, because models by their nature are only approximations and the results obtained can be the product of the essential properties of the model or of the details of its simplifying assumptions ( Levins, 1969 ). \n Campbell (2004) , Brown et al. (2006) , Zarbá and Brown (2015) have shown using emergybased network analysis that increasing energy quality in an ecosystem network enhances feedbacks and maximizes the empower captured by the ecosystem. Such feedbacks of high quality energy are also responsible for the hierarchical organization of the network into trophic webs. The transformities of the system components in this study were determined within the benthic ecosystem network from the bottom-up by distributing the emergy inflows in the emergy signature up through the network to obtain the total emergy required for the available energy of each species or functional group. This approach differs from Christensen’s work in terms of how to calculate the emergy values ( Christensen, 1994 ). More specifically, this methodology allows the evaluation of empower within entire ecological network, instead of grouping by trophic level. This is important, because the maximization of network empower is hypothesized to be nature’s decision criterion ( Odum, 1996 , Campbell, 2000 , Campbell, 2001 ). \n This procedure allowed us to gain additional insights about these benthic ecosystems with the use of EST and the emergy methods to integrate the important physical variables as forcing functions creating ecosystem organization and to model their relationships with ecological variables using the Energy Systems Language ( Odum, 1994 ). \n The use of emergy evaluation in this study allowed us to use a quality-adjusted form of the standard network indices, AMI and the A/C ratio, as a complementary tool for characterizing ecosystem network organization. Comparison of the results from these two methods resulted in increased understanding of the system based on differences between the two evaluations ( Table 3a, b ). These indicators have been widely used to describe and compare a variety of ecosystems with different levels of organization, spatial sizes and geographic locations based on mass balanced trophic models ( Patricio et al., 2006 ; Vassallo et al., 2006 ; Ibarra-García et al., 2016 ; González et al., 2016 ; Ortiz et al., 2016 ). \n Management of such systems requires the integration of local ecological, economic and environmental components within a systems framework ( Berrios et al., 2017 ) to evaluate ecosystem health. In this context, the Energy Systems Theory and Emergy Analysis, which are based on ecological and general systems principles, irreversible thermodynamics and ecology ( Odum, 1988 , 1994, 1996) provide a powerful framework for evaluating, modeling and understanding systems and this approach offers an alternative decision criterion for determining ecosystem health, i.e., maximum empower generated in the ecosystem network. Furthermore, health assessments based on emergy have been given a strong theoretical basis as seen in Brown and Ulgiati (2004), Campbell (2000) and Campbell et al. (2014) and this approach has been modified and widely employed to characterize the condition of urban ecosystems and information systems, among others ( Liu et al., 2011 ; Garde et al., 2007 ; Su et al., 2010 , Campbell et al., 2014 ). \n In the current contribution, we recognize the following uncertainties and simplifications for the assessment of emergy: (1) the emergy inflow assigned to the nitrogen and kinetic energy of upwelling in Antofagasta and Mejillones Bays requires physical oceanographic studies; however, there are no evaluations of these systems using hydrodynamic models. In this case the models proposed by Escribano and Hidalgo (2001) and Marín et al. (2003) were used to estimate the volume of upwelled water entering Antofagasta and Mejillones Bays, respectively; (2) in the benthic networks, the split strategy was applied, assuming that the contributions of feces, pseudofeces and mortality to detritus would have the same properties and fate; (4) the effects of pollutants on the biological components of the network were not included, because the data to evaluate these effects were not available; and (5) the contributions from the economy (i.e. goods, labor and services from fishing) affecting the benthic networks were not considered. 5. Conclusions \n Based on our results, the emergy signature of all three coastal bays showed an unbalanced profile dominated by NO 3 -N from upwelling, with Mejillones Bay showing the largest imbalance, suggesting that these three benthic ecosystems may be less biologically diverse than other comparable marine ecosystems. The emergy evaluation and analysis of benthic ecological networks used indicators, such as transformity, the emergy system diversity index, emergy-based ascendency and an index for assessing ecosystem health (EOET), which showed that the three bays exhibit different states of ecosystem health. These findings could be related to the geographical configuration, orientation to the prevailing winds, habitat heterogeneity, community networks, fisheries and different anthropic activities taking place in each system. For example, in Tongoy Bay only artisanal fisheries and tourist activities are present, while Antofagasta Bay contains a marine reserve and Mejillones Bay is dominated by industrial activities and also contains an artisanal fishery. The outcomes are very interesting, because our understanding of these systems has increased by using different models and analysis to provide a more comprehensive overall picture of these systems to better reveal their holistic properties ( Jørgensen, 1992 ). This is somewhat different from the conclusions reported by Patten (1995) and Fath et al. (2001) regarding the complementarity of the different analyses based on maximizing power of flows in the ecosystem networks. Despite the considerable time and data required to complete an emergy analysis, Energy Systems Theory and the emergy indicators derived from it may provide a powerful tool for improving the assessment of ecosystem health by integrating the renewable environmental resource base of ecosystems with ecological variables and network structures. Figure 1. \n \n Open in a new tab \n Study areas and the location of upwelling centers of Mejillones, Antofagasta and Tongoy Bays in northern Chile. Highlights. \n \n Energy Systems Theory and Emergy Analysis were applied to quantify the health of benthic ecosystems. \n Unbalanced emergy signatures, suggest less development and system diversity compared to other coastal ecosystems with more balanced emergy signatures. \n Macro-descriptors and network properties suggest functional differences in health among the three ecosystems. \n Energy Systems Theory and the emergy indicators derived from it may provide a powerful tool for improving the assessment of ecosystem health. \n 6. Acknowledgments \n This work was conducted as part of the doctoral thesis of the first author financed by the Chilean National Commission for Scientific and Technical Development (CONICYT) and the Programa de Doctorado en Ciencias Aplicadas mención Sistemas Marinos Costeros at the University of Antofagasta, Chile. The views expressed in this are those of the authors and do not necessarily represent the views or policies of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. We thank Drs. Giancarlo Cicchetti, Peg Pelletier, and Henry Walker of the USEPA, ORD, NHEERL, AED Narragansett, RI for helpful reviews of the manuscript. Appendix A. \n Linear equation group obtained from Mejillones Bay model. Number represents the energy flow each pathway carries and x represents the transformity of the pathway. \n Equations Input = Output \n Phytoplankton: 3139588*(X1) = (12725+76+982+4874+1299+2818+3099+1566+3954)*( X6 ) \n L. trabeculata : 3118*(X2) = (348+15+1157+1540+57)*( X7 ) \n Chlorophyta: 879*(X3) = (199+1+579+101)*( X8 ) \n Rhodophyta: 1006*(X4) = (317+6+634+3020)*( X9 ) \n Phaeophyta: 1361*(X5) = (213+3+1157+9)*( X10 ) \n Detritus: (12725+76+982+4874+1299+2818+3099+1566+3954