 = (0.1+5)*( X27 ) Footnotes \n \n \n 1 Emergy is defined as the sum of the available energy (i.e., energy with the potential to do work) of one kind, e.g., solar joules, which is used-up, directly and indirectly, within an ecosystem for the production of goods and services. \n \n \n 2 The proportion of total system emergy flow allocated to each component. \n \n \n 3 The number and diversity of emergy-weighted interactions among the system components. \n \n \n 4 This index has been suggested as a maturity index. \n \n 7. References \n \n \n Berrios F, Campbell DE, Ortiz M, 2017. \nEmergy evaluation of benthic ecosystems influenced by upwelling in northern Chile: Contributions of the ecosystems to the regional economy. Ecol. Modell. 359, 146–164. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brandt-Williams. 2000. \nEvaluation of Watershed Control of two Central Florida lakes. Newnans Lake and Lake Weir. PhD dissertation\nEnvironmental Engineering Sciences, University of Florida, Gainesville. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brown MT, Woithe RD, Odum. HT, Montague. CL, Odum. EC, 1993. \nEmergy analysis perspectives of the Exxon Valdez oil spill in Prince William Sound, Alaska\nReport to the Cousteau Society, Center for Wetlands and Water Resources, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brown MT, Herendeen RA\n1996. \nEmbodied energy analysis and EMERGY analysis: a comparative view. Ecological Economics\n19, 219–235. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brown MT, Bardi E, 2001. \nHandbook of Emergy Evaluation: Folio #3 Emergy of Ecosystems. Center for Environmental Policy, Environmental Engineering Sciences, University of Florida, Gainesville. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brown MT, Ulgiati S, 2004. \nEmergy, transformity and ecosystem health In Jorgensen et SE.al. (eds) Handbook of Ecological Indicators for Assessment of Ecosystem Health. Elsevier, New York. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brown MT, Cohen MJ, Bardi E, Ingwersen W, 2006. \nSpecies diversity in the Florida Everglades, USA: A system approach to calculating biodiversity. Aquat. Sci\n68, 254–277. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Brown MT, Campbell DE, Ulgiati S, Franzese PP, 2016. \nThe Geobiosphere Emergy Baseline: A Synthesis. Ecol. Modell\n339, 89–91. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE, 2000. \nUsing energy systems theory to define, measure, and interpret ecological integrity and ecosystem health. Ecosyst. Health\n6(3), 181–204. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE\n2001. \nProposal for Including What Is Valuable to Ecosystems in Environmental Assessments. Environ. Sci. Technol\n35 (14), 2867–2873. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE, 2004. \nEvaluation and Emergy Analysis of the Cobscook Bay Ecosystem. In: Ecosystem Modeling in Cobscook Bay, Maine: A Boreal, Macrotidal Estuary. North East. Nat\n11(Special Issue 2), 355–424. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE, 2016. \nEmergy baseline for the Earth: A historical review of the science and a new calculation. Ecol. Modell\n339, 96–125. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE, Lu H, Knox G, Odum HT, 2009. \nMaximizing empower on a human-dominated planet: The role of exotic Spartina . Ecol. Eng\n35, 463–486. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE, Cai TT, Olsen TW, 2014. \nEcosystem Health: Energy Indicators, pp. 131–142. In: Cleveland C (Ed), Encyclopedia of Energy, Volume 2\nElsevier, New York. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Campbell DE, Erban L\n2017. \nA reexamination of the emergy input to a system from the wind. Emergy Synthesis 9, Brown MT and Sweeny S (eds). Center for Environmental Policy, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Cerda M, Knoppers B, Valdés J, Siffedine AF, Ortlieb L, Sabadini-Santos E, 2010. \nVariación espacial y temporal de las masas de agua, nutrientes y sedimentación de la materia orgánica e inorgánica en la bahía Mejillones del sur (23º S), Chile. Rev. Chil. Hist. Nat\n83, 409–420. [ Google Scholar ] \n \n Costanza R, Mageau., 1999. \nWhat is a healthy ecosystem?\nAquat. Ecol\n33, 105