  "content": "Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM)\n - Queen&#39;s University Belfast \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Queen's University Belfast Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Help & FAQ \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at Queen&#39;s University Belfast \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Impacts \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n Press/Media \n \n \n \n Student theses \n \n \n \n Equipment \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) \n S. M. Seyedpour , P. Kirmizakis , P. Brennan , R. Doherty , T. Ricken \n School of Natural and Built Environment \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 48 \n \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n Citations\n(Scopus) \n \n \n \n \n 547 \n Downloads\n(Pure) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Student theses \n (1) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n The simulation-optimization models of groundwater and contaminant transport can be a powerful tool in the management of groundwater resources and remediation design. In this study, using Multiquadratic Radial Basis Function (MRBF) a coupled groundwater flow and reactive transport of contaminant and oxidant was developed in the framework of the Meshfree method. The parameter analysis has determined the optimum shape parameter (0.97), and the results of the model were compared with a physical sandbox model which were in good agreement. The genetic algorithm approach was used to find the optimum design of the remediation using permanganate as an oxidant. To find the optimum design we considered two objectives and two constraints. The results revealed that the breakthrough of contaminant to the downstream area of interest and the concentration of the contaminant in this area is reduced significantly with optimisation. \n Original language English Pages (from-to) 389-399 Number of pages 11 Journal Science of the Total Environment Volume 669 Early online date 04 Mar 2019 DOIs https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 Publication status Published - 15 Jun 2019 \n \n \n \n Keywords Genetic algorithm Groundwater flow Point collocation method Radial basis function Reactive contaminant transport \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 Licence: Unspecified 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) Copyright 2019 Elsevier. \nThis manuscript is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/), which permits distribution and reproduction for non-commercial purposes, provided the author and source are cited. \n Accepted author manuscript, 3.5 MB Licence: CC BY-NC-ND \n \n \n \n Other files and links \n http://www.mendeley.com/research/optimal-remediation-design-simulation-groundwater-flow-coupled-contaminant-transport-using-genetic-a \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM)'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Groundwater Flow \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Pollution Transport \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Oxidant \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Genetic Algorithm \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Optimum Design \n Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Point Collocation Method \n Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Collocation Method \n Mathematics \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Reactive Transport \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 50% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Student theses\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Biogeophysical approaches to monitoring contaminated and restored sites Kirmizakis, P. (Author), Doherty, R. (Supervisor), Allen, C. (Supervisor) & Ofterdinger, U. (Supervisor), Jul 2020 Student thesis : Doctoral Thesis › Doctor of Philosophy \n \n File \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Seyedpour, S. M., Kirmizakis, P., Brennan, P. , Doherty, R. , & Ricken, T. (2019). Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) . Science of the Total Environment , 669 , 389-399. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 , https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 \n \n \n \n Seyedpour, S. M. ; Kirmizakis, P. ; Brennan, P. et al. / Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) . In: Science of the Total Environment . 2019 ; Vol. 669. pp. 389-399. \n \n \n \n @article{f6c80471945142bbabecceedb7b140b5, title = \"Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM)\", abstract = \"The simulation-optimization models of groundwater and contaminant transport can be a powerful tool in the management of groundwater resources and remediation design. In this study, using Multiquadratic Radial Basis Function (MRBF) a coupled groundwater flow and reactive transport of contaminant and oxidant was developed in the framework of the Meshfree method. The parameter analysis has determined the optimum shape parameter (0.97), and the results of the model were compared with a physical sandbox model which were in good agreement. The genetic algorithm approach was used to find the optimum design of the remediation using permanganate as an oxidant. To find the optimum design we considered two objectives and two constraints. The results revealed that the breakthrough of contaminant to the downstream area of interest and the concentration of the contaminant in this area is reduced significantly with optimisation.\", keywords = \"Genetic algorithm, Groundwater flow, Point collocation method, Radial basis function, Reactive contaminant transport\", author = \"Seyedpour, \\{S. M.\\} and P. Kirmizakis and P. Brennan and R. Doherty and T. Ricken\", year = \"2019\", month = jun, day = \"15\", doi = \"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409\", language = \"English\", volume = \"669\", pages = \"389--399\", journal = \"Science of the Total Environment\", issn = \"0048-9697\", publisher = \"Elsevier B.V.\", } \n \n \n \n Seyedpour, SM, Kirmizakis, P, Brennan, P , Doherty, R & Ricken, T 2019, ' Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) ', Science of the Total Environment , vol. 669, pp. 389-399. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 , https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 \n \n \n \n Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM). / Seyedpour, S. M. ; Kirmizakis, P.; Brennan, P. et al. In: Science of the Total Environment , Vol. 669, 15.06.2019, p. 389-399. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) AU - Seyedpour, S. M. AU - Kirmizakis, P. AU - Brennan, P. AU - Doherty, R. AU - Ricken, T. PY - 2019/6/15 Y1 - 2019/6/15 N2 - The simulation-optimization models of groundwater and contaminant transport can be a powerful tool in the management of groundwater resources and remediation design. In this study, using Multiquadratic Radial Basis Function (MRBF) a coupled groundwater flow and reactive transport of contaminant and oxidant was developed in the framework of the Meshfree method. The parameter analysis has determined the optimum shape parameter (0.97), and the results of the model were compared with a physical sandbox model which were in good agreement. The genetic algorithm approach was used to find the optimum design of the remediation using permanganate as an oxidant. To find the optimum design we considered two objectives and two constraints. The results revealed that the breakthrough of contaminant to the downstream area of interest and the concentration of the contaminant in this area is reduced significantly with optimisation. AB - The simulation-optimization models of groundwater and contaminant transport can be a powerful tool in the management of groundwater resources and remediation design. In this study, using Multiquadratic Radial Basis Function (MRBF) a coupled groundwater flow and reactive transport of contaminant and oxidant was developed in the framework of the Meshfree method. The parameter analysis has determined the optimum shape parameter (0.97), and the results of the model were compared with a physical sandbox model which were in good agreement. The genetic algorithm approach was used to find the optimum design of the remediation using permanganate as an oxidant. To find the optimum design we considered two objectives and two constraints. The results revealed that the breakthrough of contaminant to the downstream area of interest and the concentration of the contaminant in this area is reduced significantly with optimisation. KW - Genetic algorithm KW - Groundwater flow KW - Point collocation method KW - Radial basis function KW - Reactive contaminant transport UR - http://www.mendeley.com/research/optimal-remediation-design-simulation-groundwater-flow-coupled-contaminant-transport-using-genetic-a U2 - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 DO - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 M3 - Article SN - 0048-9697 VL - 669 SP - 389 EP - 399 JO - Science of the Total Environment JF - Science of the Total Environment ER - \n \n \n \n Seyedpour SM, Kirmizakis P, Brennan P , Doherty R , Ricken T. Optimal remediation design and simulation groundwater flow coupled to contaminant transport using genetic algorithm and radial point collocation method (RPCM) . Science of the Total Environment . 2019 Jun 15;669:389-399. Epub 2019 Mar 4. doi: 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409, 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.01.409 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new