 {
  "title": "Transformation of endocrine disrupting chemicals, pharmaceutical and personal care products during drinking water disinfection",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.12.106",
  "source": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-03-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Transformation of endocrine disrupting chemicals, pharmaceutical and personal care products during drinking water disinfection",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "83a354d583f2d6a1",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-10",
  "_ev": "ti:9c8f7b327569aca5",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Science of the Total Environment）",
  "content": "\"Transformation of endocrine disrupting chemicals, pharmaceutical and p\" by Frederic D.L. Leusch, Peta A. Neale et al.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n -->\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n Search \n Browse Collections \n My Account \n About \n Digital Commons Network™ \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n My Account \n \n \n \n \n FAQ \n \n \n \n \n About \n \n \n \n \n Contact Us \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n > \n \n Research outputs 2014 to 2021 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n > \n \n 5530 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research outputs 2014 to 2021 \n \n \n \n \n Transformation of endocrine disrupting chemicals, pharmaceutical and personal care products during drinking water disinfection \n \n \n \n Authors/Creators \n Frederic D.L. Leusch \n Peta A. Neale \n Francesco Bussetti , Edith Cowan University Follow \n Marcella Card \n Andrew Humpage \n John D. Orbell \n Harry F. Ridgway \n Matthew B. Stewart \n Jason P. van de Merwe \n Beate I. Escher \n \n \n \n Abstract \n Pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs) and endocrine disrupting compounds (EDCs) are frequently detected in drinking water sources. This raises concerns about the formation of potentially more toxic transformation products (TPs) after drinking water disinfection. This study applied a combination of computational and experimental methods to investigate the biological activity of eight EDCs and PPCPs commonly detected in source waters (acetaminophen, bisphenol A, carbamazepine, estrone, 17α-ethinylestradiol, gemfibrozil, naproxen and triclosan) before and after disinfection. Using a Stepped Forced Molecular Dynamics (SFMD) method, we detected 911 unique TPs, 36% of which have been previously reported in the scientific literature. We calculated the likelihood that TPs would cause damage to biomolecules or DNA relative to the parent compound based on lipophilicity and the occurrence of structural alerts, and applied two Quantitative Structure-Activity Relationship (QSAR) tools to predict toxicity via receptor-mediated effects. In parallel, batch experiments were performed with three disinfectants, chlorine, chlorine dioxide and chloramine. After solid-phase extraction, the resulting TP mixtures were analyzed by chemical analysis and a battery of eleven in vitro bioassays covering a variety of endpoints. The laboratory results were in good agreement with the predictions. Overall, the combination of computational and experimental chemistry and toxicity methods used in this study suggest that disinfection of the studied EDCs and PPCPs will produce a large number of TPs, which are unlikely to increase specific toxicity (e.g., endocrine activity), but may result in increased reactive and non-specific toxicity. \n \n \n \n Keywords \n Computational chemistry, Disinfection, Micropollutant, Predictive toxicology, Transformation product, High resolution mass spectrometry \n \n \n \n Document Type \n Journal Article \n \n \n \n Date of Publication \n 2019 \n \n \n \n Publication Title \n Science of the Total Environment \n \n \n \n Publisher \n Elsevier BV \n \n \n \n School \n School of Science \n \n \n \n RAS ID \n 31207 \n \n \n \n Comments \n Leusch, F. D., Neale, P. A., Busetti, F., Card, M., Humpage, A., Orbell, J. D., ... & Escher, B. I. (2019). Transformation of endocrine disrupting chemicals, pharmaceutical and personal care products during drinking water disinfection. Science of The Total Environment, 657 , 1480-1490.\n Available here . \n \n \n \n Copyright \n subscription content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Link to Full Text\n \n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n DOWNLOADS \n Since January 10, 2019 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Share \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n COinS \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Link to publisher version (DOI) \n 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.12.106 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n Make a Submission \n My Account \n \n Search \n \n \n \n Enter search terms:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Select context to search:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n in this series \n \n \n \n in this repository \n across all repositories \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Advanced Search\n \n \n Help Using Search \n \n \n \n\n\n \n \n \n Notify me via email or RSS \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n Follow \n \n \n Links \n \n Benefits of Submitting to RO \n \n \n Browse \n \n Collections \n Disciplines \n Author Names \n Exhibits \n \n \n Author Information \n \n Author Guidelines \n Thesis Submission Guidelines \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n Article Locations \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View articles on map \n View articles in Google Earth \n \n \n \n Contact Us \n \n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Elsevier - Digital Commons \n \n \n \n \n Home | \n About | \n FAQ | \n My Account | \n Accessibility Statement \n \n \n \n \n Privacy \n \n Copyright",
