  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Chemosphere）",
  "content": "The problem of drinking water access: A review of disinfection technologies with an emphasis on solar treatment methods \n \n \n \n \n root.skip-to-content\n RUJA: Repositorio Institucional de Producción Científica Comunidades Todo DSpace Estadísticas Enlaces de interés Licencia de Autoarchivo Cómo publicar en RUJA Normalización de la firma de autor SHERPA-RoMEO DULCINEA Licencias Creative Commons Web de propiedad intelectual de Rebiun English العربية বাংলা Català Čeština Deutsch Ελληνικά Español Suomi Français Gàidhlig हिंदी Magyar Italiano Қазақ Latviešu Nederlands Polski Português Português do Brasil Srpski (lat) Српски Svenska Türkçe Yкраї́нська Tiếng Việt Iniciar sesión Iniciar sesión Inicio Investigación Departamento de Ingeniería Electrónica y Automática DIEA-Artículos The problem of drinking water access: A review of disinfection technologies with an emphasis on solar treatment methods \n The problem of drinking water access: A review of disinfection technologies with an emphasis on solar treatment methods Archivos Manuscript unmarked.docx (12.42 MB) Fecha 2019-03 Autores Pichel, Natalia Vivar, Marta Fuentes, Manuel Título de la revista ISSN de la revista Título del volumen Editor Elsevier Resumen The lack of access to safe drinking water is one of the biggest challenges facing humanity in the 21st century. Despite the collective global effort that has been made, the drinking water sources of at least 2 billion people are faecally contaminated, resulting in more than half a million diarrhoeal deaths each year, with the majority occurring in developing countries. Technologies for the inactivation of pathogenic microorganisms in water are therefore of great significance for human health and well-being. However, conventional technologies to provide drinking water, although effective, present limitations that impede their global application. These treatment methods often have high energy and chemical demands, which limits their application for the prevention of waterborne diseases in the most vulnerable regions. These shortcomings have led to rapid research and development of advanced alternative technologies. One of these alternative methods is solar disinfection, which is recognised by the World Health Organization as one of the most appropriate methods for producing drinkable water in developing countries. This study reviews conventional technologies that are being applied at medium to large scales to purify water and emerging technologies currently in development. In addition, this paper describes the merits, demerits, and limitations of these technologies. Finally, the review focuses on solar disinfection, including a novel technology recently developed in this field. Descripción Palabras clave drinking water, waterborne diseases, conventional technologies, emerging technologies, SODIS, SOLWAT Citación 5. N. Pichel, M. Vivar, M. Fuentes, ‘The problem of drinking water access: A review of disinfection technologies with emphasis on solar treatment methods’, Chemosphere 218 (2019), 1014-1030. URI https://hdl.handle.net/10953/2168 Colecciones DIEA-Artículos Página completa del ítem Citas Los documentos del repositorio se encuentran en ruja Repositorio Institucional de Producción Científica Configuración de cookies Aviso Legal Política de privacidad Enviar Sugerencias Todos los documentos están protegidos por derechos de autor y bajo Licencia CC (by-nc-nd) si no se indica lo contrario.",
