 {
  "title": "Impact of catalyst load, chemical oxygen demand and nitrite on disinfection and removal of contaminants during catalytic ozonation of wastewater",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.09.394",
  "source": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-02-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Impact of catalyst load, chemical oxygen demand and nitrite on disinfection and removal of contaminants during catalytic ozonation of wastewater",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "7da3dcae11fc4254",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-10",
  "_ev": "ti:4a5aeef1cd7bf953",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Science of the Total Environment）",
  "content": "Impact of catalyst load, chemical oxygen demand and nitrite on disinfection and removal of contaminants during catalytic ozonation of wastewater \n \n \n <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.cf390c6bfbe7555f.css\"> \n \n Skip to main content\n English Français Log In Log in with myMcGill\n Log in Have you forgotten your password? Communities & Collections About Help Home eScholarship Research Publications Impact of catalyst load, chemical oxygen demand and nitrite on disinfection and removal of contaminants during catalytic ozonation of wastewater Impact of catalyst load, chemical oxygen demand and nitrite on disinfection and removal of contaminants during catalytic ozonation of wastewater Loading... Files Primary 17_Kolosov_and_Yargeau_2019.pdf (1.64 MB) Date 2019-02-15 Authors Kolosov, Petr Yargeau, Viviane Contributors Advisor Degree Faculty Engineering Department Department of Chemical Engineering Local Affiliated Centre Research Group Journal Title Journal ISSN Volume Title Publisher Elsevier Article Version https://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/version_types/c_ab4af688f83e57aa/ Rights © 2019 the authors under a cc by nc nd license from Elsevier Creative Commons license https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ Alternate title Abstract Calcium-silicate mineral Polonite® and aluminum-based catalyst (AL-1010S), previously identified as promising materials for catalytic ozonation, were used as catalysts to investigate the impact of some operating conditions (ratio ozone feed concentration to catalyst load) and wastewater characteristics (chemical oxygen demand - COD and nitrite - NO2 concentration) on the disinfection and removal of contaminants of emerging concern (CECs) during catalytic ozonation of wastewater. Tests conducted in synthetic wastewater using two different ozone gas concentration (4 and 8 g (nm3)) and 6 different catalyst loads provided ratios of 0.08, 0.11, 0.16, and 0.32. Results from the experiments indicated that the ratio of 0.11 was optimal and reached residual disinfection below 2 MPN mL−1 from the initial concentration of 5 ± 2 × 105 MPN mL−1 and removal of atrazine (ATZ) above 80% from the initial concentration of 100 ± 10 μg L−1 for an ozone dose of 41–45 mg L−1. Catalytic ozonation with the selected materials enhanced disinfection and ATZ removal from synthetic wastewater (SWW) in comparison to non-catalytic ozonation by making the treatment performance less sensitive to increased chemical oxygen demand (COD) and nitrite (NO2) in the matrix. Validation of the results in real wastewater effluents confirmed that catalytic ozonation enhanced disinfection. Catalytic ozonation using Polonite® and AL-1010S provided residual bacteria level of 0.6 ± 0.42 MPN mL−1 and 0.29 ± 0.41 MPN mL−1, while non-catalytic ozonation lead to an average residual bacteria level of 1.26 ± 0.09 MPN mL−1 for the same range of transferred ozone dose. However, under the conditions tested, a limited number of CECs were extracted at levels above the limits of quantification and further validation work required to evaluate the performance of catalytic ozonation for the removal of CECs. \n Description Subject Water Citation Kolosov, P., Yargeau, V. (2019). Impact of catalyst load, chemical oxygen demand and nitrite on disinfection and removal of contaminants during catalytic ozonation of wastewater. Science of the Total Environment, 651, 2139-2147. Retrieved from https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.09.394 Language en ORCID https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7332-276X URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.14905/80217 DOI 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.09.394 Collections Research Publications Endorsement Review Supplemented By Referenced By Creative Commons license Except where otherwise noted, this item's license is described as © 2019 the authors under a cc by nc nd license from Elsevier More item details DSpace software copyright © 2002-2026 LYRASIS Cookie settings Accessibility settings Privacy Policy Send Feedback Terms of Use Powered by",
  "content_kind": "fulltext",
  "fulltext_status": "已获取全文",
