 {
  "title": "Novel materials for catalytic ozonation of wastewater for disinfection and removal of micropollutants",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.07.022",
  "source": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2018-12-01",
  "category": "tech",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Novel materials for catalytic ozonation of wastewater for disinfection and removal of micropollutants",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "29bc2b3884ee3ac2",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-10",
  "_ev": "ti:fddb2993ad208b84",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Science of the Total Environment）",
  "content": "Novel materials for catalytic ozonation of wastewater for disinfection and removal of micropollutants \n \n \n <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.cf390c6bfbe7555f.css\"> \n \n Skip to main content\n English Français Log In Log in with myMcGill\n Log in Have you forgotten your password? Communities & Collections About Help Home eScholarship Research Publications Novel materials for catalytic ozonation of wastewater for disinfection and removal of micropollutants Novel materials for catalytic ozonation of wastewater for disinfection and removal of micropollutants Loading... Files Primary 21_Kolosov_et_al_2018.pdf (1.23 MB) Date 2018-12-10 Authors Kolosov, Petr Peyot, Marie-Line Yargeau, Viviane Contributors Advisor Degree Faculty Engineering Department Department of Chemical Engineering Local Affiliated Centre Research Group Journal Title Journal ISSN Volume Title Publisher Elsevier Article Version https://vocabularies.coar-repositories.org/version_types/c_ab4af688f83e57aa/ Rights © 2018 the authors under a cc by nc nd license from Elsevier Creative Commons license https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ Alternate title Abstract The purpose of this work was to identify novel materials that could be used for the catalytic ozonation of wastewater for improved removal of micropollutants and disinfection. The materials chosen for investigation were selected based on their commercial availability and composition characteristics that suggested that they could act as catalysts. Synthetic wastewater (SWW) was used to mimic municipal secondary effluent wastewater while obtaining constant and reproducible matrix characteristics. Polonite®, wollastonite, zeolite, TiO2-Al2O3 (8%/92%), and AL-1010S (AlO2-based) were tested for their potential impact on efficiency of disinfection, based on the removal of E. coli bacteria and removal of contaminants of emerging concern (CECs), relative to conventional ozonation. Atrazine (ATZ), ibuprofen (IBP), naproxen (NPX), and gemfibrozil (GBZ) were used as indicator compounds. Zeolite and wollastonite did not promote disinfection and CECs removal; TiO2-Al2O3 and AL-1010S provided improvement for both criteria, but to a lesser extent in SWW than in Milli-Q water; and Polonite® did not enhance the removal of CECs but led to the higher E. coli inactivation. These results suggest that Polonite®, AL-1010S, and TiO2-Al2O3 can act as catalysts and provide mechanisms to lower the ozone dose required to reach disinfection. The apparent kinetic constant of the reaction for the catalytic ozonation of ATZ, in the ultrapure water, was determined for Polonite®, TiO2-Al2O3 and AL-1010S. The reusability of the catalysts was demonstrated over four consecutive cycles of 6 h of treatment in a continuous flow ozonation system. \n Description Subject Water Citation Kolosov P, Peyot ML, Yargeau V. Novel materials for catalytic ozonation of wastewater for disinfection and removal of micropollutants. Sci Total Environ. 2018 Dec 10;644:1207-1218. doi: 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.07.022. Epub 2018 Jul 23. PMID: 30743834. Language en ORCID https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7332-276X URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.14905/78648 DOI 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.07.022 Collections Research Publications Load more Endorsement Review Supplemented By Referenced By Creative Commons license Except where otherwise noted, this item's license is described as © 2018 the authors under a cc by nc nd license from Elsevier More item details DSpace software copyright © 2002-2026 LYRASIS Cookie settings Accessibility settings Privacy Policy Terms of Use Powered by",
  "content_kind": "fulltext",
  "fulltext_status": "已获取全文",
  "fulltext_source": "Unpaywall(HTML)",
  "fulltext_chars": 3627,
  "fulltext_tried_at": "2026-10-12"
