 {
  "title": "Predictive score and probability of CTX-like toxicity in fish samples from the official control of ciguatera in the Canary Islands",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.03.445",
  "source": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-07-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Predictive score and probability of CTX-like toxicity in fish samples from the official control of ciguatera in the Canary Islands",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "e50adef4b6a67225",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-10",
  "_ev": "ti:5eaaf1e3c7fe1e31",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Science of the Total Environment）",
  "content": "This document is a postprint version of an article published in Science of The Total \nEnvironment© Elsevier after peer review. To access the final edited and published \nwork see https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2019.03.445 \n \n \n \nDocument downloaded from: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPredictive score and probability of CTX-like toxicity in fish samples from 1 \nthe Official Control of Ciguatera in the Canary Islands 2 \n 3 \nJ. Andres Sanchez -Henao 1, Natalia García -Álvarez 1*; Antonio Fernández 1, Pedro 4 \nSaavedra 2; Freddy Silva Sergent 1; Daniel Padilla 1; Begoña Acosta -Hernández 1; 5 \nManuela Martel Suárez 1; Jorge Diogène 3; Fernando Real 1. 6 \n 7 \n1 Division of Fish Health and Pathology, Institute of Animal Health and Food Safety (IUSA), University of 8 \nLas Palmas de Gran Canaria, 35416 Arucas, Las Palmas, Spain. 9 \n2 Department of Mathematics, University of Las Palmas de Gran Canaria, Canary Islands, Spain 10 \n3 Marine and Continental Waters Environmental Monitoring, IRTA, Ctra. Poble Nou, km 5.5, 43540, Sant 11 \nCarles de la, Ràpita, Spain. 12 \n 13 \n* C orresponding author: Natalia García Álvarez, Division of Fish Health and Pathology , Institute of 14 \nAnimal Health and Food Safety (IUSA), Veterinary School, University of Las Palmas de Gran Canaria 15 \n(ULPGC), 35416 Arucas, Las Palmas de Gran Canaria, Spain. Tel: (+34) 928 45 43 16; E-mail address: 16 \nnatalia.garcia@ulpgc.es 17 \n18 \nABSTRACT: 19 \nThis research identifies factors associated with the contamination by ciguatoxins 20 \n(CTXs) in a population of fish and proposes a predict ive score of the presence of CTX -21 \nlike toxicity in amberjack samples from the official control program of ciguatera in the 22 \nCanary Islands of the Directorate-General (DG) Fisheries (Canary Government). Out of 23 \nthe 970 samples of fish studied, 177 (18.2%) samples showed CTX-like toxicity. The fish 24 \nwere classified according to the species, amberjack (Seriola dumerili and S. rivoliana) (n 25 \n= 793), dusky grouper (Epinephelus marginatus) (n = 145) and wahoo (Acanthocybium 26 \nsolandri) (n = 32). The data were separated by species category and statistically 27 \nexamined, resulting in 137 (17.3%) amberjack and 39 (26.9%) grouper samples 28 \nshowing CTX -like toxicity; regarding wahoo species, only 1 toxic sample (3.1%) was 29 \nfound. According to fishi ng location the contamination rates suggested grouping the 30 \nislands in four clusters; namely: { El Hierro: HI; La Gomera: LG; La Palma: LP}, { Gran 31 \nCanaria: GC; Tenerife: TF}, {Fuerteventura: FU} and { Lanzarote: LZ}. For the amberjack 32 \nspecies, the multivariat e logistic regression showed the factors that maintained 33 \nindependent association with the outcome , which were the warm season (OR = 3.617; 34 \n95%CI = 1.249 – 10.474), the weight (per Kg, 1.102; 95%CI = 1.069 – 1.136) and the 35 \nisland of fish catching . A prediction score was obtained for the probability of 36 \ncontamination by CTX in amberjack fish samples. The area under de curve ( AUC) 37 \nobtained using the validation data was 0.747 (95% CI = 0.662 – 0.833). Regarding 38 \ngrouper species, the island of fishing was the only factor that showed significant 39 \ndifferences associated with the presence of CTX -like toxicity. We provide herein data 40 \nfor a better management and prediction of ciguatera in the Canary Islands, suggesting 41 \na review of the minimum limits of fish we ight established by the Canary Government 42 \nfor the control program. 43 \n 44 \nKeywords: ciguatoxin (CTX), predictive score, Canary Islands, amberjack, dusky 45 \ngrouper, wahoo. 46 \n 47 \n1. INTRODUCTION 48 \n 49 \nCiguatera Fish Poisoning (CFP) is one of the most relevant seafood -borne illnesses 50 \nworldwide and the most commonly reported human food poisoning related to natural 51 \nmarine toxins (Friedman et al., 2008; Suzuki et al., 2017). It consists of a debilitating 52 \nhuman neuro -intoxication caused by consumption of varieties of fish species from 53 \ntropical and subtropical waters, contaminated with bioaccumulat ed ciguatoxins (CTXs) 54 \n(Meyer et al., 2016). CFP is characterized by causing gastrointestinal, neurological, and 55 \ncardiovascular symptoms (Friedman et al., 2017). A range of 10 to 50 thousand people 56 \nsuffering from CFP annually worldwide has been estimated (EFSA, 2010). However, 57 \nepidemiological data remain unreliable, given that it has been estimated that only ∼ 58 \n10-20% of cases are properly diagnosed and reported ( Azziz-Baumgartner et al., 2012; 59 \nLaurent et al., 2005). 60 \n 61 \nCFP is found endemically in tropical and subtropical water s such as the Caribbean Sea, 62 \nthe Indian and the P acific Oceans (Lewis, 2006 ). In the 40 years th at followed the 63 \ndiscovery of CTX (Yasumoto et al., 1977), more than 400 fish species have been 64 \nimplicated in poisoning incidents (Tester et al., 2010), most of which are high -order 65 \ncarnivores (Lehane and Lewis, 2000; Lewis, 2006). In Europe, CFP and CTXs h ave been 66 \ngaining interest in recent years due to several reported cases in European countries 67 \n(e.g., France, Spain, the Netherlands, Germany, and Italy), mostly related to 68 \nconsumption of imported ciguateric fish or people who visited e ndemic areas of CFP 69 \n(Caillaud et al., 2010). However, none of the current methods of analysis to determine 70 \nCTX-group toxins in fish have been formally validated (EFSA, 2010). 71 \n 72 \nRegarding the East Atlantic Ocean, in the Canary Archipelago CFP had not been 73 \ndescribed until 2004, when 5 people became poisoned (Pérez-Arellano et al., 2005). In 74 \n2008 two more outbreaks were reported in the Canary Islands (Boada et al., 2010) and 75 \n11 people were also affected by CFP in Madeira Archipelago (Otero et al., 2010). 76 \nTherefore, f or some species collected from authorized first sale points , considered a 77 \nrisk factor in the Canary Islands, an action protocol with the objective of making the 78 \ndetection of CTX prior to sale and human consumption, has been implemented since 79 \n2011 by the DG Fish eries of the Canary Government (DG of Fisheries of the Canary 80 \nGovernment, 2018). In the last decade, several outbreaks of CFP affecting 113 people 81 \n(Canary Government, 2017b ) have been confirmed in the Canary Archipelago, 82 \nfollowing the consumptio n of subsistence and recreational harvested fish and not 83 \nrelated to controlled fish . Additionally, s ince 2015, CFP has been designated a 84 \nnotifiable disease in the Canary Islands. 85 \n 86 \nRegarding the marine biotoxins, p recursors of CTXs (Gamberitoxins) are produced by 87 \nbenthic dinoflagellates of the genus Gambierdiscus (Rodríguez et al., 2017). These 88 \nprecursors are transferred and metabolized through the food web, as Gambierdiscus 89 \ncells are ingested by herbivorous fish, which are then taken by piscivorous fish, both of 90 \nwhich are finally consumed by humans. It is believed that CTXs are bioaccumulated 91 \nthrough the trophic webs, thus, fish higher in the food web tend to contain the highest 92 \nCTX concentrations (Banner et al., 1966; Dickey and Plakas, 2010). In addition, CTXs are 93 \ntasteless, colourless and odourless, which increase s the risk of poisoning (Friedman et 94 \nl., 2008). To date, more than 29 CTX congeners have been identified and grouped 95 \naccording to geographic distribution : Indian CTXs (I -CTX), Caribbean CTXs (C -CTX) and 96 \nmost investigated, Pacific CTXs (P -CTX) according to the presence in the waters where 97 \nCFP is endemic (Hamilton et al., 2002; EFSA, 2010). 98 \n 99 \nDifferent species of Gambierdiscus have been isolated from water samples collected in 100 \nthe Canary Islands during a spatial study (Aligizaki et al., 2008; Rodríguez et al., 2017). 101 \nThese authors highlighted that socioeconomic impact of ciguatera on fisheries activity 102 \nand public health in the Canary Islands requires further efforts to implement a faster 103 \nanalytical response to detect CTXs in fish samples, and multidisciplinary research to 104 \nknow life cycle, distribution and toxicity of Gambierdiscus spp. 105 \n 106 \nThe major goal of the present research was to study several factors which may be 107 \nassociated with the probability to find CTX-like toxicity in fish obtained from first sale 108 \npoints in the Cana ry Islands in order to describe the statistical significance of these 109 \nassociations or achieve a predictive score, if possible. 110 \n 111 \n2. MATERIALS AND METHODS 112 \n 113 \n2.1. Study area 114 \n 115 \nThe Canary Archipelago is located in the North eastern Atlantic Ocean near Europe 116 \n(2000 Km SW from the Iberian Peninsula) and North Africa (100 Km W from the 117 \nMoroccan coast), FAO Major Fishing Area 34 in the subdivision 1.2 (FAO 2004). 118 \n 119 \nTwo co -occurring amberjack species present in this area were analyzed (Seriola 120 \ndumerili and S. rivoliana). These predators can reach large sizes living up to 15 years 121 \n(Murie and Parkyn, 2008 ); when the fish are mature, they migrate to coastal areas to 122 \nspawn. Amberjack species are important fish stocks in the Canary Islands being the 123 \nfourth and sixth most caught species in La Gomera (Seriola dumerili ) and El Hierro 124 \n(Seriola rivoliana ), respectively (Canary Government, 2017a ). Dusky g rouper 125 \n(Epinephelus marginatus) is a benthonic species that lives on the coast within 5 to 45 126 \nmeters depth; it is considered a solitary fish, very territorial and sedentary (Göthel, 127 \n1992) which predates other fish, crustaceans and cephalopods (Smale, 1986); although 128 \nit is a fish of relevance to human consumption in the Canary Archipelago, dusky 129 \ngrouper is not among the 10 most caught species in the islands (mainly represented by 130 \nfish shoals) (Canary Government, 2017a), possibly due to its solitary behaviour . Wahoo 131 \nis a pelagic animal and a seasonal migratory spec ies, relatively new in the Canary 132 \nIslands waters where it h as settled . Normally it feeds on pelagic prey (Espino et al., 133 \n2006). 134 \n 135 \n2.2. Fish sample collection 136 \n 137 \nThe official control protocol for CFP implemented by the Canary Government ( DG of 138 \nFisheries of the Canary Government, 2018 ) establishes a list of certain species and 139 \nweights of fish considered a risk factor. This list, based on the local experience, shows 140 \nthe limit weights for fish to be sampled at the authorized first sale points and 141 \ninvestigated in the Institute of Animal Health and Food Safety (IUSA) laboratory for CTX 142 \ndetection (Table 1). This list has been updated in 2018. 143 \n 144 \nThe f ish samples used in the present study were obtained between April 2016 and 145 \nDecember 2017 from the official monitoring of CFP ( DG Fisheries of the Canary 146 \nIslands). Through t he mentioned programme , fish samples from professional 147 \nfishermen associations were sent to the IUSA laboratory of the University of Las 148 \nPalmas de Gran Canaria (ULPGC) in order to determine the CTX pres ence in fish flesh. 149 \nThe laboratory received 1538 samples in the study period. However, this research only 150 \nincluded 970 fish samples selected from the total, based on the availability of relevant 151 \ninformation, such as fish species, weight of the specimen and the island of fishing. Fish 152 \nspecies and weights were consistent to those established by the offici al control 153 \nprotocol, particul arly amberjack (Seriola spp.) (n=793), dusky grouper ( Epinephelus 154 \nmarginatus) (n=145) and wahoo ( Acantocybium solandri ) (n=32). Thus, the samples 155 \nwere classified according to the specie s and different data such as the year of fish 156 \ncatching (2016/2017), period (cold/warm), weight and fishing island. 157 \n 158 \n2.3. Sample preparation and extraction of CTX 159 \n 160 \nToxin extraction was performed following the protocol proposed by Lewis in 2003 and 161 \ncarried out by Hossen et al. in 2015 with slight modifications according to the 162 \nlaboratory needs. Fish samples were first homogenized and then a portion of 10 g of 163 \nflesh wa s cooked at 70 oC during 10 minutes. When samples reached room 164 \ntemperature, 30 ml of acetone was added, mixed with ultraturrax and centrifuged at 165 \n3000 xg during 5 minutes at 4 oC; this last step was repeated twice and both 166 \nsupernatants were pooled. The resulting acetone was filtered through a 0.45 μm of 167 \nPTFE filter and evaporated with a rotary evaporator at 55 oC. The dried extract was re -168 \nsuspended in methanol:water (9:1) and N -Hexane for phase separation. The uppe r 169 \nphase of N-Hexane was discarded and the methanol phase was dried under N 2 current 170 \nat 40 oC for a subsequent partition with ethanol:water (1:3) and Diethyl-Ether (DE). The 171 \nDE was reserved and evaporated under N 2 current to dryness. The final residue was 172 \nresuspended in 4 ml of methanol and kept at -20 oC to be used in the cellular bioassay 173 \nanalysis (CBA). 174 \n 175 \n2.4. Neuroblastoma (neuro-2a) cell-based assay (CBA) 176 \n 177 \nNeuro-2a cells (Cell line: CCL131, from ATCC, LGC Standards S.L.U., Barcelona, Spain) 178 \nwere maintained in Roswell Park Memorial Institute (RPMI) -1640 medium 179 \nsupplemented with 5 -10% of foetal bovine serum (FBS) at 37 °C in a 5% CO 2 180 \natmosphere. The P -CTX-1 standard ( STD) (R.J. Lewis, The Queensland University, 181 \nAustralia) was used as a reference toxin for the assessment of CTX -like toxicity, as C -182 \nCTX reference material is not commercially available (Soliño et al., 2015). 183 \n 184 \nFor the cytotoxicity assay, cells were seeded i n a 96 -well flat bottom microplate (200 185 \nµl/well) following the procedure of Caillaud et al in 2012 using RPMI medium 186 \nsupplemented with 5% of FBS at a density of 70,000 cells per well. Cells were 187 \nincubated 24 h in the same mentioned conditions. Ouabain (0.1 mM) and veratridine 188 \n(0.01 mM) were added to half of the seeded wells to favour cell mortality in case of the 189 \npresence of CTX. In parallel to fish sample extracts testing by N euro-2a assay, a dose -190 \nresponse curve with P -CTX-1 STD was always performed as an internal control, for cell 191 \nresponse evaluation and limit of detection and quantification (LOD/LOQ) 192 \nestablishment. Thus, fish extracts and the STD were evaporated and re suspended in 193 \nRPMI medium supplied with 5% FBS. Then, cells were exposed to flesh extract and the 194 \nP-CTX-1 STD at decreasing concentrations in order to ensure the cells sensibility to 195 \nCTX. Every sample extract and the STD were assayed in triplicate. After a 24 h-period of 196 \nexposure to fish extracts and to P -CTX-1 standard solution, cell viability was evaluated 197 \nwith MTT [3 -(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium] and DMSO solutions. 198 \nThe corresponding absorbances were read at 570 nm by a multi -well 199 \nspectrophotometer scanner and plotted into the Microsoft Office Excel 2016 and 200 \nGraphPad Prism 7 softwares (GraphPad, San Diego, California, USA). For the 201 \ninterpretation of the dose-response curves, cell viabilities were related to the cell 202 \nviability of the control colum n (cells with and without pre -treatment with 203 \nouabain/veratridine, O/V). 204 \n 205 \nThe assessment of fish matrix effect on the N euro-2a assay was performed using 206 \ndifferent concentrations of several fish extracts to expose the cells with or without O/V 207 \n(50 - 200 mg T issue Equivalents (TE)/ml). Several muscle samples from grouper and 208 \namberjack showed interference with the assay with concentrations higher than 100 209 \nmg TE/ml ; on the contrary extracts from muscle tissue of Wahoo species displayed 210 \ninterference above 80 mg T E/ml. For that reason and to unify methodology, 80 mg 211 \nTE/ml was set as the maximum concentration for testing with the Neuro-2a assay. 212 \n 213 \nDue to the large amount of samples used in this surveillance study, the 50% inhibition 214 \nconcentration (IC50, with O/V) was only assessed with the P-CTX-1 STD in ppb units (pg 215 \nP-CTX-1/ml) in order to evaluate the cell response and limits. Samples extracts we re 216 \ntested using 1 to 4 columns (serial dilutions) of the 96 -well microplate, depending on 217 \nthe number of samples received every week; thus, semi -quantitative estimation of the 218 \ncontent in P -CTX-1 equivalents in fish extracts was not always possible to determ ine. 219 \nTherefore, in the present study, a response producing less than 20% cell mortality was 220 \nconsidered as a non -toxic effect, as other authors suggested (Caillaud et al., 2012), 221 \nbeing the concentration of P -CTX-1 STD causing 20% inhibition of cell viabilit y (IC20) 222 \nset as the LOD and LOQ according to this concentration of fish extract used for testing. 223 \nThus, a “positive sample” was considered when the corresponding extract showed an 224 \ninhibition of cell viability over this LOD value. According to t he mean valu e of IC20 225 \n(1.359 pg P-CTX-1/ml) obtained from all dose-response curves performed with the STD 226 \nin the study period and the maximum concentration of extracts set to avoid matrix 227 \neffect (80 mg TE/ml), the LOD/LOQ was 0.017 ppb. 228 \n 229 \n2.5. Statistical analysis 230 \n 231 \nThe statistical analysis was performed using the R package, version 3.3.1 (R 232 \nDevelopment Core Team, 2016) and IBM SPSS Statistics 23.0 software. 233 \n 234 \nUnivariate analysis : Categorical variables are expressed as frequencies and 235 \npercentages and continuous as medians and interquartile ranges (IQR = 25th – 75th 236 \npercentile). The percentages were compared, as appropriate, using the Chi -square (χ2) 237 \ntest or the exact Fisher test, t he means by the t-test and the medians by the Wilcoxon 238 \ntest for independent data. As usual, the statistical signiﬁcance was set at p-value < 0.05 239 \nand t he rates of contamination by the CTX were estimated by means of confidence 240 \nintervals (CI) at 95% using a bootstrap method. 241 \n 242 \nMultivariate analysis: In order to identify those factors that maintain independent 243 \nassociation with the outcome, a multivariate logistic regression analysis were 244 \nperformed. All variables of the study were entered into the analysis and a selection 245 \nbased on complete enumeration algorithm (Morgan and Tatar, 1972) and Bayes 246 \ninformation criterion (BIC) was carried out. For each one of these regressions, we 247 \nevaluate the lack of fit according the BIC criteria (Schwarz, 1978). The models were 248 \nsummarized as coefficients (SE), p-values (likelihood ratio test) BIC values (for the 249 \nresidual models) and odds-ratio, which were estimated by 95% CI. 250 \n 251 \nReceiver operating characteristics: The discriminant power of the score deduced from 252 \nthe logistic model was evaluated from a receiver -operating characteristic analysis 253 \n(ROC). The area under the ROC curve was estimated by means of the 95% CI. Statistical 254 \nsignificance was set at p < 0.05. 255 \n 256 \n3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION 257 \n 258 \nTo identify the factors associated with the contamination by CTX in the population of 259 \nfish under study, data were analyzed according to the following variables: 260 \n 261 \n3.1. Influence of fish species in the presence of CTX-like toxicity 262 \n 263 \nOut of the 970 fish samples included in this research, 793 (81.8%) belonged to both 264 \namberjack specie s, 145 (14.9%) to the grouper and 32 (3.3%) to the wahoo. Overall, 265 \n228 ( 18.2%) of all samples exhibited measurable CTX -like toxicity.C omparing species 266 \ncategories, Pearson’s C hi-squared (χ2) test revealed a significant difference in CTX 267 \nprevalence between species of the samples tested (p = 0.002). Although most of the 268 \nsamples correspond to amberjack species , the grouper displayed the highest 269 \npercentage of positive samples (26.9%), see Table 2. This observation may indicate 270 \nthat the chance for catching a positive grouper is two -fold higher than fishing a 271 \npositive amberjack in the Canary Islands waters possibly explained by the sedentary 272 \nbehaviour of grouper species (Göthel 1992; Espino et al., 2006) which may continually 273 \nfeed in areas where Gambierdiscus are more abundant, allowing a continuous 274 \naccumulation of the toxin . However, it must be highlighted that the fish analyzed in 275 \nthis study correspond to certain weights considered as risk factors for human health 276 \n(Table 1) and therefore, this limitation must be taken into account before raising any 277 \nconclusion. In addition , the high percentage of toxic grouper samples obtained may 278 \nsupport the review of the lower limit of weights previously suggest ed for this species 279 \n(see Table 1 and Figure 1). Additionally, 32 samples of wahoo species were analyzed 280 \ncorresponding to the 3.3% of all flesh fish studied and note that only 1 of them showed 281 \na CTX-like toxicity. Thus, this species was not considered in this analysis. 282 \n 283 \n3.2. Influence of the fish weight 284 \n 285 \nBioaccumulation of CTXs in amberjack has been reported to be highly dependent on 286 \nthe weight of the specimen (Bravo et a l., 2015). Thus, an analysis of the presence of 287 \nCTX in fish against this variable is essential to fully understand the accumulation of this 288 \nbiotoxins through the lifetime of these animals. Accordingly, in comparison with the 289 \nlast mentioned reference, the present study has tripled the number of samples under 290 \ninvestigation. 291 \n 292 \nDue to the biolog ical difference between species (Reid et al., 2016; Šegvić -Bubić et al., 293 \n2016), each species weight data were analyzed separately using Mann-Whitney (M -294 \nW)/Wilcoxon non-parametric tests (Table 2), demonstrating that the median value of 295 \nweight for fish which showed CTX-like toxicity was significantly higher than the median 296 \nweight of negative fish, both in amberjack (27 Kg vs. 20 Kg; p < 0.001) and grouper 297 \nspecies (22.5 Kg vs. 21.1 Kg; p = 0.013, respectively). In both species, CTX toxicity was 298 \nmore frequently observed in larger specimens. The descriptive statistics of the weight 299 \nof the CTX positive and negative samples according to fish species ( interquartile range-300 \nIQR, median and minimum-maximum range) are summarized in Table 2. The 301 \ndistribution of weight data between species and CTX results is represented in th e box 302 \nplot diagrams below (Figure 1). Regarding wahoo samples, only one positive result was 303 \nobtained, what makes the statistical analysis impossible to be performed. 304 \n 305 \nIt is remarkable to note the presence of positive fish with a weight close to the 306 \nminimum limit established by the government of the Canary Islands (see Table 2 and 307 \nFigure 1). The smallest positives amberjack and grouper species weighed 14.5 Kg and 308 \n17.4 Kg respectively, see Figure 1. This observation may justify an extension of the 309 \nminimum weight proposed for analysis in these two species, mainly in grouper fish, as 310 \nmentioned before. Additionally, a CFP outbreak occurring in 2016 also support s this 311 \nsuggestion, when two people in Tenerife Island were poisoned by the consumption of 312 \n7 Kg grouper, and the presence of CTX-like toxicity confirmed in our laboratory (Canary 313 \nGovernment, 2017b). 314 \n 315 \n3.3. Influence of the fishing island 316 \n 317 \nThe information of the island where the fish were caught is important to analy ze due 318 \nto the nature of the studied species and the possible risk of poisoning related to the 319 \nlocation. From the selected samples , significant difference in CTX result was found 320 \nbetween islands of fishing (p < 0.001). Lanzarote showed the highest contamination 321 \nrate (52.9%) which was more than two fold greater than the value obtained in samples 322 \nfrom Fuerteventura (21. 0%) and Gran Canaria (17.8%) and more than three times 323 \nhigher than the CTX positivity showed from El Hierro (15%) and Tenerife (13.5%). The 324 \nnumber of positive samples obtained from these Canary Islands far exceeded those 325 \nresulted in the islands of La Palma (5.1%) and La Gomera (2.4%) where the lowest 326 \nnumber of positive fish was observed (Figure 2). However, it must be considered that, 327 \nunder the official control program of c iguatera during the studied period, more than 328 \n300 samples received from Lanzarote were not accompanied by the necessary 329 \ninformation, and thus, wer e not included in this research, which could limit accurate 330 \nresult from this island. These results must be considered with caution. 331 \n 332 \nAn in depth analysis by species, both amberjack and grouper also showed a statistical 333 \ndifference in CTX results between islands of capture (p < 0.001). For amberjack species, 334 \nthe number of positive samples seems to decrease from the Eastern islands to the 335 \nWestern islands (see Table 3 and Figure 2), thus, these rates suggested grouping the 336 \nisland category in four cluster; namely: {HI; LG; LP}, {GC; TF}, {F U} and {LZ}. Table 3 337 \ndisplays the contamination rates corresponding to each cluster. 338 \n 339 \nOn the contrary, the profile mentioned above was not found regarding grou per 340 \nspecies, which showed positive results more likely linked to certain islands in 341 \nparticular, possibly due to its sedentary behaviour (Göthel, 1992; Espino et al., 2006). 342 \nThus, the highest number of ciguatoxic grouper was obtained in El Hierro, with a 343 \nremarkable percentage of toxic samples (10 samples, 90.9%) and Lanzarote (19 344 \nsamples, 41.3%). Although Fuerteventura provided the highest number of samples, 345 \nonly 3 were found positive to CTX (5.6%). Additionally, the low number of results from 346 \nGran Canaria and La Palma precluded any conclusion. 347 \n 348 \nDespite of these findings, other confounding variables should be taken into 349 \nconsideration before any conclusion can be drawn (see section 3.4.). 350 \n 351 \nIn addition, it is important to emphasise that results from each island presented one or 352 \nmore positive individuals weighted close to the minimum control limit except for those 353 \nfish caught in La Gomera. 354 \n 355 \nFurthermore, t he only positive wahoo obtained in this study was fished in El Hierro, 356 \nrepresenting the 4.5% of all samples analyzed from this island. 357 \n 358 \n3.4. Influence of the period of fishing 359 \n 360 \nFish samples studied in the present research were received in the period from April 361 \n2016 to December 2017 and analyzed for the presence of CTX-like toxicity. 362 \n 363 \nConsidering the results obtained by Rodr íguez et al. (2017), peaks of Gambierdiscus 364 \nspp. cells densities were observed in the Canary Islands associated to temperatures 365 \nhigher than 20 oC. For this reason, time frame was divided in “cold period” (January to 366 \nApril) and “warm period” (May to December) in accordance with the surface seawater 367 \ntemperature registered in both years ( NOAA, 2017), with a difference of 3oC between 368 \nboth period s. Samples available for this research only allowed comparison of data in 369 \nthe warm period between both years of study. Thus, considering species separately, a 370 \nsignificant decrease (p < 0.001) was observed in the percentage of positive samples of 371 \namberjack species caught within the warm period between 2016 and 2017 (31.4% and 372 \n12.1%, respectively). This finding could be explained due to the modification in the 373 \nofficial protocol of the lower weight limit in amberjack species from 15 Kg in 2016 to 374 \n14 Kg in 2017 (Table 1) and the increasing demand for analysis with the consequent 375 \nrise of samples received in the laboratory in 2017 over 2016 (573 and 220 samples, 376 \nrespectively, see Table 3). In contrast , grouper species maintained a similar rate of 377 \ntoxicity in the warm periods of both evaluated years. 378 \n 379 \nConsidering both years in conjunction, the a mberjack specie s showed a rate of CTX 380 \ntoxicity of 12.5% (95% CI = 5.6 – 20.8) and 17.8% (95% CI = 15.0 – 20.5) in the cold and 381 \nwarm period, respectively, what seems to be an increase in the number of positive 382 \nsamples from the cold to the warm season, but no statistically significant difference (p 383 \n= 0.261) was found ( Table 3). In this regard, it is important to stress that in the warm 384 \nperiod the laboratory received a considerably greater amount of samples (see table 3) 385 \nwhat could therefore partially explain the difference of CTX toxicity rates found 386 \nbetween seasons. Regarding grouper fish, the number of CTX-positive sam ples were 387 \nquite similar, being 26.3% (95% CI = 10.5 - 47.4) in the cold period and 27.0% (95% CI = 388 \n19.8 - 34.9) in the warm period. 389 \n 390 \n3.5. Risk gradient assessment: Predictive score of the presence of CTX-toxicity in a 391 \npopulation of amberjack fish. 392 \n 393 \nFor the statistical analysis of results of samples from t he amberjack species, using the 394 \ntraining data, the multivariate logistic regression showed that the factors that 395 \nmaintained independent association with the outcome (contamination by CTX) were 396 \nthe warm seas on (OR = 3.617; 95%CI = 1. 249 - 10.474), the weight (per K g, 1.102; 397 \n95%CI = 1.069 - 1.136) and the island of fishing (grouping according gradient, see Table 398 \n3). It should be noted that the season did not show statistical significance in the 399 \nunivariate anal ysis (see section 3.4) but did so in the multivariate testing. This is 400 \nattributable to the confounding effect of the weight, since in the warm season the 401 \nweight of the fish was significantly lower than in the cold period (p < 0.001) , as it is 402 \nshown in Figure 3. 403 \n 404 \nFor the a mberjack species, a predictive score of contamination by CTX was obtained. 405 \nFor this purpose, the data were randomly divided into a training data set (n = 510) and 406 \na validation data set (n = 283). The predictive score was obtained by mea ns of the 407 \nmultivariate logistic analysis using the training dataset. Its discriminant power was 408 \nevaluated by means of the ROC analysis using the validation dataset and was 409 \nsummarized as the estimated area under the ROC curve (AUC-ROC, Figure 4). 410 \n 411 \nThe next prediction score was then obtained from this logistic analysis: 412 \nScore = 1.286 × Warm + 0.097 × Weight + 1.962 × D1 + 2.555 × D2 + 4.191 × D3 413 \n 414 \nHere, the season is a binary variable (1 = Warm; 0 = Cold) and D1, D2 and D 3 are the 415 \ndummies variables associated with the island of fish catching (clusters) according to 416 \nthe design shown in table 4 . Four clusters were considered: El Hierro -La Gomera -La 417 \nPalma; Gran Canaria-Tenerife; Fuerteventura and Lanzarote. 418 \n 419 \nThe AUC obtained using the validation data was 0.747 (95% CI = 0.662 – 0.833) (Figure 420 \n4). Table 5 displayed the increasing probabilities of the contamination by the CTX 421 \naccording to a gradient from the cluster of western islands , in the cold season and fish 422 \nwith low weight to the eastern islands, in warm season and fish with high weight. 423 \nTherefore, the probability of finding a positive result in a sample from amberjack is less 424 \nthan 1% if the sample comes from a small specimen (less than 22 Kg), from the 425 \nwestern cluster islands {HI; LG; LP} and fished in the cold season. Additionally, as can 426 \nbe seen in Table 5, the change to the warm season leads to a strong increase in the 427 \nprobability of contamination, being over 50% when fish weighing more than 35 Kg are 428 \ncaught in Fuerteventura. And, in contrast, this probability reaches higher values (more 429 \nthan 90%) in fish caught in Lanzarote, weighting more than 41 Kg. 430 \n 431 \nThe decreasing gradient of presence of CTX in amberjack species observed from t he 432 \neastern to the western islands is strongly consistent with the results obta ined by 433 \nRodríguez et al. (2017). These authors found that the higher density of Gambierdiscus 434 \nspp. was found in Lanzarote and Fuerteventura islands , and they also reported that 435 \nG. exentricus, one of the most toxic Gambierdiscus found in the Canary Islands , was 436 \nmore abundant in the eastern islands compared to the western ones. This finding 437 \ncould be explained by the conjunction of Northwestern African upwelling and the Cold 438 \nCanary Current (Sang il et al., 2012) at the bottom of these islands, along with the 439 \nshallow waters of these eastern islands which provides abundance of nutrients for the 440 \ngrowth of plankton and seaweeds (Rodríguez et al., 2017). 441 \n 442 \nRegarding results obtained from g rouper species, the only factor that showed 443 \nsignificant differences associated with the presence of CTX was the island of fishing (p 444 \n< 0.001) , mentioned before in section 3.3. These results are in accordance with 445 \nDierking and Campora who analyzed samples of Cephalopholis argus from the 446 \nHawaiian Islands in 2009 and found no geographic patterns in toxicity between or 447 \nwithin islands. 448 \n 449 \nCiguateric groupers showed a different profile of distribution, as exposed above, which 450 \nmay be explained for a s edentary and very territorial behaviour of these fish (Reid et 451 \nal., 2016). Furthermore, i t must be considered the presence of an algae bloom of 452 \nG. caribaeus occurred in October 2016 in El Hierro (Soler-Onís et al., 2016) and the rate 453 \nof CTX bioaccumulation in fish tissue (Lehane and Lewis, 2000; Banner et al., 1966) , 454 \nwhat could explain that this certain island represents the highest percentage of CTX 455 \npositive groupers in the Canary Archipelago (90%) in this period of study (Table 3). 456 \n 457 \nEven so, the aim of the present study was not to calculate the real prevalence of CTX in 458 \nfish from the Canary Islands, but to propose a predictive value of finding a positive 459 \nsample according to different associated factors, such as fish species, weight, season 460 \nor fishing island. Although results showed different probabilities of contamination by 461 \nCTX-like toxicity between islands, none of them is free of ciguateric fish. Therefore, 462 \nofficial monitoring should continue throughout the archipelago to ensure the food 463 \nsafety. 464 \n 465 \n4. Conclusions 466 \n 467 \nThis study confirms the Canary Islands as an area of expansion of CFP endemicity and 468 \ncontains the first reported predictive score for the presence of CTX -like toxicity in 469 \namberjack fish samples from this area. 470 \n 471 \nThis work identifies the several factors associat ed with the probability of 472 \ncontamination by CTX-like toxicity of fish caught in the Canary Archipelago. 473 \n 474 \nA risk gradient was obtained for amberjack, considering weight of fish, season and 475 \nisland of fishing, this latter being the only factor significantly associated with grouper 476 \nspecies. The risk of contamination by CTX could not be adequately assessed for wahoo 477 \ndue to small sample size of this species. 478 \n 479 \nPresence of CTX in amberjack from some areas seems to be highly related to the 480 \nseason of the year which may be related to the abundance of the most toxic 481 \nGambierdiscus found in the Canary Islands. 482 \n 483 \nThe minimum weight limits establis hed by the official control of c iguatera in the 484 \nCanary Islands for amberjack and dusky grouper need to be reviewed to safeguard 485 \nconsumer health. 486 \n 487 \nConflict of interest statement 488 \nThe authors declare that there are no conflicts of interest. 489 \n 490 \nAcknowledgements 491 \nThe research was funded by Directorate-General for Fisheries of the Canarian 492 \nGovernment, through the official c ontrol program of ciguatera in the Canary Islands , 493 \nand supervised by the Public Health Service. We also thank the EuroCigua project 494 \n(“Risk characterization of ciguatera food poisoning in Europe” FRAMEWORK 495 \nPARTNERSHIP AGREEMENT GP/EFSA/AFSCO/2015/03) for their technical and valuable 496 \nassistance, and also the REPROCAN project for their advice regarding the sea surface 497 \ntemperature of the canary waters. The authors also acknowledge support from CERCA 498 \nProgramme/Generalitat de Catalunya. 499 \n 500 \nFIGURE CAPTIONS 501 \n 502 \nFigure 1. CTX results by the weight variable in amberjack (box plot graph on the left) 503 \nand grouper (box plot graph on the right). Line indicates the minimum limit of weight 504 \nestablished for CTX analysis by the DG Fisheries (14 Kg and 17 Kg for amberjack and 505 \ngrouper species respectively). The plot represents the interquartile range (Q3 - Q1). 506 \nSample size is shown below the corresponding group category. 507 \n 508 \nFigure 2: CTX results in all samples included in this study (left), in amberjack (middle) 509 \nand grouper (right) species by the location of capture. Percentages of positive samples 510 \nfrom the different Canary Islands are indicated in each bar graph. The Canary Islands: 511 \nLZ, Lanzarote; FU, Fuerteventura; GC, Gran Canaria; TF, Tenerife; LG, La Gomera; LP, La 512 \nPalma; HI, El Hierro. 513 \n 514 \nFigure 3. CTX results by the weight variable in cold and warm p eriods for amberjack 515 \n(box plot graph on the left) and grouper (box plot graph on the right). Line indicates 516 \nthe minimum limit of weight established for CTX analysis by the DG Fisheries (14 Kg 517 \nand 17 Kg for amberjack and grouper species respectively). 518 \n 519 \nFigure 4. Receiver operating characteristics for the score obtained from the logistic 520 \nregression. The score obtained with the data training was validated using the data 521 \nvalidation. 522 \n 523 \nReferences 524 \n 525 \nAligizaki, K., Nikolaidis, G., Fraga, S. (2008). Is Gambierdiscus expanding to new areas? 526 \nHarmful Algae News, 36, 6–7. 527 \nAzziz-Baumgartner, E., Luber, G., Conklin, L., Tosteson, T. R., Granade, H. R., Dickey, R. 528 \nW. et al. (2012). Assessing the incidence of ciguatera fish poisoning with two 529 \nsurveys conducted in Culebra, Puerto Rico, during 2005 and 2006. 530 \nEnvironmental health perspectives, 120(4), 526-9. doi: 10.1289/ehp.1104003 531 \nBanner, A. H., Helfrich, P., & Piyakarnchana, T. (1996). Retention of ciguatera toxin by 532 \nred snapper Lutjanus bohar. Copeia, 2, 297-301. doi: 10.2307/1441137 533 \nBoada, L. D., Zumbado, M., Luzardo, O. P., Almeida -Gonzalez, M., Plakas, S. M., 534 \nGranade, H. R. et al. (2010). Ciguatera fish poisoning on the West Africa Coast: 535 \nAn emerging risk in the Canary Islands (Spain). Toxicon, 56(8), 1516 -1519. doi: 536 \n10.1016/j.toxicon.2010.07.021 537 \nBravo, J., Suárez, F. C., Ramírez, A. S., & Acosta, F. (2015). Ciguatera, an emerging 538 \nhuman poisoning in Europe. J. Aquac. Mar . Biol., 3, 00053. doi: 539 \n10.15406/jamb.2015.03.00053 540 \nCaillaud, A., de la Iglesia, P., Darius, H. T., Pauillac, S., Aligizaki, K., Fraga, S. et al. (2010). 541 \nUpdate on methodologies available for ciguatoxin determination: perspectives 542 \nto confront the onset of ciguatera fish poisoning in Europe. Mar Drugs , 8(6), 543 \n1838-1907. doi: 10.3390/md8061838 544 \nCanary Government. Consejería de Agricultura, Ganadería, Pesca y Aguas (2017 a). 545 \nRanking de especies más significativas por islas. 2017. 546 \nhttp://www.gobiernodecanarias.org/agricultura/sgt/temas/estadistica/pesca/i547 \nndex.html 548 \nCanary Government. Servicio Canario de Salud ( 2017b). Summary of records from 549 \nSVEICC (Sistema de Vigilancia Epidemiológica de la Intoxicación por Ciguatera 550 \nen Canarias) during 2008-2017 period. 551 \nDG of Fisheries of the Canary Government (2018). Official con trol protocol for CTX 552 \ndetection of fish sampled at the authorized first sale points, implemented by 553 \nthe Canary Government. https://sede.gobcan.es/sede/verifica_doc; number of 554 \nelectronic document: 09rKTHXIFNLhuID_Sm4jM1RRY5HpKgpMN. 555 \nDickey, R. W., & Plakas, S. M. (2010). Ciguatera: a public health perspective. Toxicon, 556 \n56(2), 123-136. doi: 10.1016/j.toxicon.2009.09.008 557 \nDierking, J., & Campora, C. E. (2009). Ciguatera in the Introduced Fish Cephalopholis 558 \nargus (Serranidae) in Hawai‘i and Implications for Fishery Management. Pacific 559 \nScience, 63 (2), 193-204. doi: 10.2984/049.0630203 560 \nEFSA. European Food Safety Authority (2010). Scientific Opinion on marine biotoxins in 561 \nshellfish - Emerging toxins: Ciguatoxin group. EFSA Journal , 8(6), 38. doi: 562 \n10.2903/j.efsa.2010.1627 563 \nEspino, F., Boyra, A., Tuya, F., Haroun, R., (2006). Guía visual de especies marinas de 564 \ncanarias. First ed. Oceanográfica: Divulgación, Educación y Ciencia S.L. Gran 565 \nCanaria. p 97. 566 \nFAO Major Fishing Areas. ATLANTIC, EASTERN CENTRAL (Major Fishing Area 34). CWP 567 \nData Collection. In: FAO Fisheries and Aquaculture Department [online]. Rome. 568 \nUpdated 1 October 2004. 569 \nFriedman, M. A., Fernandez, M., Backer, L. C., Dickey, R. W., Bernstein, J., Schrank, K. et 570 \nal. (2017). An Updated Review of Ciguatera Fish Poisoning: Clinical, 571 \nEpidemiological, Environmental, and Public Health Management. Mar Drugs , 572 \n15(3). doi: 10.3390/md15030072 573 \nFriedman, M. A., Fleming, L. E., Fernandez, M., Bienfang, P., Sch rank, K., Dickey, R. et 574 \nal. (2008). Ciguatera fish poisoning: treatment, prevention and management. 575 \nMar Drugs, 6(3), 456-479. doi: 10.3390/md20080022 576 \nGöthel, H., (1992). Fauna marina del Mediterráneo . Ediciones Omega, S.A., Barcelona. 577 \npp 319. 578 \nHamilton, B., Hurbungs, M., Vernoux, J. P., Jones, A., & Lewis, R. J. (2002). Isolation and 579 \ncharacterisation of Indian Ocean ciguatoxin. Toxicon, 40(6), 685 -693. doi: 580 \n10.1016/S0041-0101(01)00259-8 581 \nHossen, V., Solino, L., Leroy, P., David, E., Velge, P., Dragacci, S. et al. (2015). 582 \nContribution to the risk characterization of ciguatoxins: LOAEL estimated from 583 \neight ciguatera fish poisoning events in Guadeloupe (French West Indies). 584 \nEnviron Res, 143(Pt B), 100-108. doi: 10.1016/j.envres.2015.09.014 585 \nLaurent, D., Yeeting, B. M., Labrosse, P., & Gaudechoux, J. P. (2005). Ciguatera: a field 586 \nreference guide / Ciguatera: un guide pratique. 587 \nLehane, L., & Lewis, R. J. (2000). Ciguatera: recent advances but the risk remains. Int J 588 \nFood Microbiol, 61(2-3), 91-125. doi: 10.1016/S0168-1605(00)00382-2 589 \nLewis, R. J. (2003). Detection of toxins associated with ciguatera fish poisoning Manual 590 \non Harmful Marine Microalgae. Second ed., Vol. 11. pp 267-277. 591 \nLewis, R. J. (2006). Ciguatera: Australian perspectives on a global problem. Toxicon, 592 \n48(7), 799-809. doi: 10.1016/j.toxicon.2006.07.019 593 \nMeyer, L., Capper, A., Carter, S., & Simpfendorfer, C. (2016). An investigation into 594 \nciguatoxin bioaccumulation in sharks. Toxicon, 119, 234 -243. doi: 595 \n10.1016/j.toxicon.2016.06.007 596 \nMorgan, J. A. & Tatar, J. F., (1972). Calculation of the residual sum of squares for all 597 \npossible regressions. Technometrics, 14, 317-325. 598 \nMurie, D.J. & Parkyn, D.C., (2008). Age, growth and sex maturity of Greater Amberjack 599 \n(Seriola dumerili ) in the Gulf of Mexico. SEDAR33 -RD13. SEDAR, North 600 \nCharleston, SC. 41pp. 601 \nNOAA. National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. Earth System Research 602 \nLaboratory, (2017) NOAA High Resolution SST data provided by the 603 \nNOAA/OAR/ESRL PSD, Boulder, Colorado, USA, from their Web site at 604 \nhttps://www.esrl.noaa.gov/psd/ 605 \nOtero, P., Perez, S., Alfonso, A., Vale, C., Rodriguez, P., Gouveia, N. N. et al. (2010). First 606 \ntoxin profile of ciguateric fish in Madeira Arquipelago (Europe). Anal Chem , 607 \n82(14), 6032-6039. doi: 10.1021/ac100516q 608 \nPerez-Arellano, J. L., Luzardo, O. P., Perez Brito, A., Hernandez Cabrera, M., Zumbado, 609 \nM., Carranza, C. et al. (2005). Ciguatera fish poisoning, Canary Islands . Emerg 610 \nInfect Dis, 11(12), 1981-1982. doi: 10.3201/eid1112.050393 611 \nR Core Team (2016). R: A language and environment for statistical computing. R 612 \nFoundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria. URL https://www.R -613 \nproject.org/. 614 \nReid, K., Crochelet, E., Bloomer, P., & Hoareau, T. B. (2016). Investigating the origin of 615 \nvagrant dusky groupers, Epinephelus marginatus (Lowe, 1834), in coastal 616 \nwaters of Réunion Island. Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution , 103, 98 -103. 617 \ndoi: 10.1016/j.ympev.2016.07.012 618 \nRodríguez, F., Fraga, S., Ramilo, I., Rial, P., Figueroa, R. I., Riobo, P., & Bravo, I. (2017). 619 \n\"Canary Islands (NE Atlantic) as a biodiversity 'hotspot' of Gambierdiscus: 620 \nImplications for future trends of ciguatera in the area\". Harmful Algae, 67, 131-621 \n143. doi: 10.1016/j.hal.2017.06.009 622 \nSangil, C., Sansón, M., Afonso-Carrillo, J., Herrera, R., Rodríguez, A., Martín-García, L., & 623 \nDíaz-Villa, T. (2012). Changes in subtidal assemblages in a scenario of warming: 624 \nProliferations of ephemeral benthic algae in the Canary Islands (eastern Atlantic 625 \nOcean). Marine Environmental Research , 77, 120-128. doi: 626 \n10.1016/j.marenvres.2012.03.004 627 \nSchwarz, G. E. (1978). Estimating the dimension of a model. Annals of Statistics , 6(2), 628 \n461-464. 629 \nŠegvić-Bubić, T., Marrone, F., Grubišić, L., Izquierdo -Gomez, D., Katavić, I., Arculeo, M. 630 \net al. (2016). Two seas, two lineages: How genetic diversity is structured in 631 \nAtlantic and Mediterranean greater amberjack Seriola dumerili Risso , 1810 632 \n(Perciformes, Carangidae). Fisheries Research , 179, 271 -279. doi: 633 \n10.1016/j.fishres.2016.03.018 634 \nSmale, M.J., (1986). The feeding biology of four predatory reef fishes off the south -635 \neastern Cape coast, South Africa. S. Afr. J. Zool . 21(2), 111 -130. Doi: 636 \n10.1080/02541858.1986.11447968 637 \nSoler-Onís, E., Fernández -Zabala, J., Ojeda -Rodríguez, A., & Amorim, A. (2016). Bloom 638 \nof Gambierdiscus caribaeus in the temperate -subtropical waters of El Hierro, 639 \nCanary Islands (North East Atlantic) Harmful Algae News, 55, 15-17. 640 \nSuzuki, T ., Ha, D. V., Uesugi, A., & Uchida, H. (2017). Analytical challenges to 641 \nciguatoxins. Current Opinion in Food Science , 18, 37 -42. doi: 642 \n10.1016/j.cofs.2017.10.004 643 \nTester, P. A., Feldman, R. L., Nau, A. W., Kibler, S. R., & Wayne Litaker, R. (2010). 644 \nCiguatera fish poisoning and sea surface temperatures in the Caribbean Sea and 645 \nthe West Indies. Toxicon, 56(5), 698-710. doi: 10.1016/j.toxicon.2010.02.026 646 \nYasumoto, T., Bagnis, R., Thevenin, S., & Garcon, M. (1977). A Survey of Comparative 647 \nToxicity in the Good Cha in of Ciguatera. NIPPON SUISAN GAKKAISHI , 43(8), 648 \n1015-1019. doi: 10.2331/suisan.43.1015 649 \nTable 1 . Fish species included in the present work , and weight limits established for \nCTX analysis by the Canary Government through the official control protocol. \n Species Latin name Weight* (Kg) \n Amberjack Seriola spp. 15/14** \n Wahoo Acanthocybium solandri 35 \nDusky grouper Epinephelus marginatus 17 \n \n* A particular fish is sampled if weight is equal to or greater than this value \n** The minimum weight regarding amberjack was decreased from 15 to 14 Kg in 2017 \nby DG Fisheries of the Canary Government, to better adju st the risk of CTX detection \nfor this species \nTable 2. Percentages of CTX positive and negative samples according to fish species and the \ncorresponding fish weights, expressed as the mean and median values (Kg). \nSD, standard deviation. Min, minimum weight. Max, maximum weight. \n*Minimum weight limits established for CTX analysis by the Canary Government through the official control \nprotocol. \n \nSpecies Presence of CTX-like toxicity Total Negative Positive \n \nAmberjack \n(14 kg)* \nNumber of samples 656 137 793 \n% of samples 82.7% 17.3% \nMean weight ± SD 22.77 ± 7.8 28.9 ± 10.3 \nMedian weight (min-max) 20.0 (14-54) 27.0 (14.5-54.9) \nGrouper \n(17 kg)* \nNumber of samples 106 39 141 \n% (Species samples) 73.1% 26.9% \nMean weight ± SD 21.4 ± 3.2 22.7 ± 3.3 \nMedian weight (min-max) 21.1 (17.0-34.1) 22.5 (17.4-33.0) \nWahoo \n(35 kg)* \nNumber of samples 31 1 32 \n% (Species samples) 96.9% 3.1% \nMean weight ± SD 39.5 ± 6.0 40.0 \nMedian weight (min-max) 37 (28.0-58.0) 40.0 \nTOTAL SAMPLES Number of samples 793 177 970 % (Total samples) 81.8% 18.2% \nTable 3. Rates of contamination by CTX according to the considered factors \n Species \n Amberjack (n = 793) Dusky grouper (n = 145) \nFactor N Crude rate * (95% CI) P N Crude rate * (95% CI) P \nYear < \n0.001 \n 0.555 \n2016 220 30.5 (24.5 - 36.8) 54 24.1 (13.0 - 37.0) \n2017 573 12.2 (9.6 - 15.0) 91 28.6 (19.8 - 37.4) \nPeriod 0.261 0.951 \nCold 72 12.5 (5.6 - 20.8) 19 26.3 (10.5 - 47.4) \nWarm 721 17.8 (15.0 - 20.5) 126 27.0 (19.8 - 34.9) \nFishing Island < \n0.001 \n < 0.001 \nLZ 73 60.3 (49.3 - 71.2) 46 41.3 (27.1 - 55.5) \nFU 169 26.0 (19.5 - 32.5) 54 5.6 (-0.6 - 11.7) \nGC 125 16.8 (10.4 - 23.2) 4 50 (1 - 99) \nTF 111 13.5 (7.2 - 19.8) 28 17.9 (3.7 - 32.0) \nLG 165 2.4 (0.6 - 4.8) 0 - \nLP 76 5.3 (1.3 - 10.5) 2 - \nHI 74 6.8 (2.7 - 13.5) 11 90.9 (73.9 - 107.9) \nGradient < \n0.001 \n < 0.001 \nHI; LG; LP 315 4.1 (2.2 - 6.3) 13 76.9 (53.8 - 100) \nGC; TF 236 15.3 (11.0 - 19.9) 32 21.9 (9.4 - 37.5) \nFU 169 26.0 (19.5 - 33.1) 54 5.6 (0 - 13) \nLZ 73 60.3 (47.9 - 71.2) 46 41.3 (28.3 - 54.3) \nIsland \nOrientation \n < \n0.001 \n 0.099 \nEastern 367 29.7 (25.1 - 34.6) 104 23.1 (15.4 - 31.7) \nWestern 426 6.6 (4.2 - 9.2) 41 36.6 (19.5 - 51.2) \nFishing Island: LZ, Lanzarote; FU, Fuerteventura; GC, Gran Canaria; TF, Tenerife; LG, La Gomera; LP, La \nPalma; HI, El Hierro. (*) Toxin \n \nTable 4. Design of the dummies variables associated with the island of fishing (clusters). \nCluster 𝐷1 𝐷2 𝐷3 \nEl Hierro, La Gomera, La Palma 0 0 0 \nGran Canaria, Tenerife 1 0 0 \nFuerteventura 0 1 0 \nLanzarote 0 0 1 \n1 = warm season; 0 = cold season. \n \nTable 5. Probabilities of contamination by CTX in amberjack species according to a \ngradient of risk (from the western islands in the cold season with low weight of fish to \neastern islands in warm season and specimens with high weight) . Study period (2016 -\n2017) \nGradient Islands Period Weight (Kg) Probability-CTX (95% CI)* \n1 HI, LG, LP Cold 15 0.31 (0.07 ; 1.31) \n2 HI, LG, LP Cold 17 0.37 (0.09 ; 1.55) \n3 HI, LG, LP Cold 19 0.45 (0.11 ; 1.83) \n4 HI, LG, LP Cold 21 0.55 (0.14 ; 2.17) \n5 TF, GC Cold 23 4.55 (1.48 ; 13.12) \n6 TF, GC Cold 25 5.47 (1.82 ; 15.31) \n7 TF, GC Warm 27 20.3 (13.8 ; 28.8)** \n8 TF, GC Warm 29 23.6 (16.1 ; 33.2) \n9 FU Warm 31 40.4 (29.0 ; 52.9) \n10 FU Warm 33 45.2 (32.8 ; 58.2) \n11 FU Warm 35 50.0 (36.6 ; 63.4) \n12 FU Warm 37 54.8 (40.5 ; 68.4) \n13 LZ Warm 39 88.3 (77.7 ; 94.3) \n14 LZ Warm 41 90.2 (80.3 ; 95.4) \n15 LZ Warm 43 91.8 (82.6 ; 96.3) \n16 LZ Warm 45 93.1 (84.7 ; 97.1) \n(*) The probabilities of the presence of CTX-like toxicity are expressed as percentages \n(**) The change to the warm season leads to a strong increase in the probability of contamination \nThe Canary Islands: LZ, Lanzarote; FU, Fuerteventura; GC, Gran Canaria; TF, Tenerife; LG, La Gomera; LP, \nLa Palma; HI, El Hierro.",
