  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Abstract Ecological communities are reorganizing in response to warming temperatures. For continuous ocean habitats this reorganization is characterized by large‐scale species redistribution, but for tropical discontinuous habitats such as ",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "d23673ce94e0dcd9",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-10",
  "_ev": "ti:1d0218ac533ea7b4",
  "quality_src": "A",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Global Change Biology）",
  "content": "Thermal stress induces persistently altered coral reef fish assemblages 1 \n 2 \n 3 \nJames P.W. Robinson1*, Shaun K. Wilson2,3, Simon Jennings4, Nicholas A.J. Graham1 4 \nAffiliations: 5 \n1Lancaster Environment Centre, Lancaster University, Lancaster, LA1 4YQ, UK 6 \n2Department of Biodiversity, Conservation and Attractions: Marine Science Program, 7 \nKensington, WA 6151, Australia 8 \n3Oceans Institute, University of Western Australia, Crawley, WA 6009, Australia  9 \n4International Council for the Exploration of the Sea, H. C. Andersens Boulevard 44-46, 1553 10 \nCopenhagen V, Denmark 11 \n 12 \n*Correspondence: james.robinson@lancaster.ac.uk 13 \n 14 \nEcological communities are reorganizing in response to warming temperatures, 15 \nproducing unexpected ecosystem configurations [1,2]. For continuous ocean habitats 16 \nthis reorganization is characterized by large-scale species redistribution [3], but for 17 \ntropical discontinuous habitats such as coral reefs, spatial isolation coupled with strong 18 \nhabitat dependence of fish species [4] imply that turnover and local extinctions are more 19 \nsignificant mechanisms [5,6]. In these systems, transient marine heatwaves are causing 20 \ncoral bleaching and profoundly altering habitat structure [7]. Despite severe bleaching 21 \nevents becoming more frequent [8] and projections indicating annual severe bleaching 22 \nby the 2050s at most reefs [9], long-term effects on the diversity and structure of fish 23 \nassemblages remain unclear. Using a 23-year time-series of fish and benthic surveys 24 \nspanning a thermal stress event in Seychelles, we describe and model structural changes 25 \nand recovery trajectories of fish communities after mass bleaching. Fish assemblages 26 \ntransitioned into previously unseen compositions that persisted over 15 years, which 27 \nexceeds realized and projected intervals between thermal stress events on coral reefs. 28 \nAfter bleaching, fish communities homogenized in time and space, leading to 29 \nassemblages characterized by exceptional herbivore dominance. Composition changes 30 \noccurred despite recovery of coral habitat on some reefs, and were most pronounced 31 \nafter macroalgal regime shifts on other reefs. Modification to reef habitats caused by 32 \nfrequent marine heatwaves will lead to persistent changes in fish diversity, community 33 \nstructure and function. Our results indicate that fish communities historically 34 \nassociated with coral reefs will not reestablish following severe coral bleaching, 35 \nrequiring substantial adaptation by managers and resource users. 36 \n 37 \nKeywords: beta diversity, biodiversity, biotic homogenization, bleaching, community 38 \nstructure, coral reef ecology, regime shifts, thermal stress 39 \n 40 \nRESULTS AND DISCUSSION 41 \n2,500 limit, currently at 2,344 words. 42 \nWe investigated the effects of a marine heatwave and associated severe coral bleaching on 43 \nthe compositional turnover and persistence of fish assemblages. Because reef-associated 44 \nfishes are highly dependent on complex coral-dominated habitat [4,10], bleaching-driven 45 \nhabitat collapse can reduce species richness [11] and homogenize compositions [12] within 46 \n1-3 years. Knowledge of long-term changes in fish communities following bleaching will 47 \nhelp to reveal how recurring thermal stress events may lead to permanent changes in these 48 \ncommunities. We focus on Seychelles where, in 1998, a strong El-Ninõ coincided with the 49 \nIndian Ocean Dipole to cause severe coral bleaching, leading to loss of >90% coral cover and 50 \ncollapse of habitat structure [13]. Using data collected over 1994-2017, we examined 51 \ntemporal change in richness (α diversity) and composition (β diversity) of fish assemblages 52 \nfollowing bleaching. Because reefs either underwent regime shifts to macroalgal states or 53 \nrecovered coral cover and complexity [7] and also experienced severe bleaching in 2016 [8], 54 \nour analyses examine compositional turnover on both regime-shifted and recovering reefs, 55 \nand document how fish assemblages reorganized between successive mass bleaching events. 56 \nBiodiversity losses were most severe on regime-shifted reefs, which were dominated 57 \nby macroalgae (mean cover >20% from 2005-2017) and remained at low α diversity levels 58 \nthroughout the post-bleaching recovery period (Figure 1A,H). In contrast, recovering reefs 59 \nsteadily increased coral cover to reach pre-bleaching levels by 2014 (mean cover = 27%) 60 \n(Fig. 1A,G), and α diversity increased from 46 species (± 2.47 S.E.) in 2005 to exceed pre-61 \nbleaching levels by ~eight species in 2017 (58.5 ± 3.00). Although both reef states supported 62 \nsimilar levels of richness prior to bleaching (1994 richness: recovering = 52.1 ± 1.92; regime-63 \nshifted = 55.3 ± 3.50) and followed similar temporal trajectories to stabilize richness levels 64 \nby 2011, regime-shifted reefs did not recover pre-bleaching richness, reaching a maximum of 65 \n46 species (± 2.80) in 2008 (Figure 1A). Most strikingly, temporal trends in β diversity 66 \nindicated that fish communities settled into compositions which had not existed before 67 \nbleaching. Across all reefs, community compositions were dissimilar to their 1994 baseline 68 \n(0.37 < β1994 < 0.77) and did not recover towards pre-bleaching compositions over 2005-2014 69 \n(year mean = -0.01, 95% CI = -0.02, 0.00) (Figure 1C,D). Collectively, these α and β 70 \ndiversity patterns indicate that coral reef fish communities did not return to pre-bleaching 71 \ndiversity levels over a 16-year recovery window (1998-2014). Persistence of post-bleaching 72 \ncompositions has been observed for cryptobenthic fish assemblages [14], and we confirm that 73 \nsuch long-term bleaching impacts extend to species spanning multiple trophic levels and 74 \nproviding important ecosystem services (e.g. fisheries). 75 \nRegeneration of coral-dominated habitat was somewhat effective in mitigating 76 \nbleaching impacts on fish communities, with reefs that recovered (i.e. structurally complex 77 \nhabitat, dominated by branching corals with negligible macroalgal cover) having fish 78 \ncompositions most similar to those recorded pre-bleaching (Figure 1B,D). However, coral 79 \ncommunity composition is also a strong structuring influence on fish communities after 80 \nbleaching [12,14], and the contrasting effects of branching and massive corals on fish 81 \ncompositions here imply that compositional shifts in coral growth forms are similarly 82 \nimportant. For example, stress-tolerant corals such as massive Porites were associated with 83 \nlower species richness (Figure 1B) and higher compositional dissimilarity through time 84 \n(β1994) (Figure 1D), possibly because these sites failed to recover pre-bleaching compositions 85 \nof dominant branching coral growth forms [15] which provide shelter for abundant and 86 \ndiverse small-bodied fishes [16,17]. Because several coral growth forms contribute to 87 \nseascape-level complexity [18,19], and bleaching impacts were minimized but not reversed 88 \nby fast growth of branching corals, our findings confirm that fish communities should not be 89 \nexpected to fully recover when coral communities reassemble [20,21]. 90 \nRegime-shifted reefs were most dissimilar to their coral-dominated pre-bleaching 91 \nbaselines (mean β1994 = 0.57 ± 0.01 on regime-shifted reefs; 0.50 ± 0.01 on recovering reefs), 92 \nand β1994 was maximized on reefs with high macroalgal cover and low structural complexity 93 \n(Figure 1C,D). Declines in coral cover and flattening of habitat structure likely prevented 94 \ncoral-associated species from relocating or recruiting to macroalgal reefs, despite these fish 95 \nbeing present on nearby recovering reefs. These patterns build upon previous observations 96 \nfrom Seychelles which have demonstrated that macroalgal overgrowth has resulted in 97 \nbottom-heavy fish biomass pyramids and reduced functional diversity [7,21]. 98 \nFish communities also homogenized in time and space. βseq, a measure of 99 \ncompositional similarity between sequential survey years, declined from 2005-2017 (year 100 \nmean = -0.05, 95% CI = -0.066, -0.026) at similar rates for recovering and regime-shifted 101 \nreefs (regime state * year mean = -0.01, 95% CI = -0.042, 0.023) (Fig. 1E,F). For recovering 102 \nreefs, declines in βseq were mirrored by temporal changes in spatial dissimilarity, whereby 103 \nβspatial declined from 0.61 to 0.50 to fall below baseline βspatial levels by 2017 (Figure S1A). In 104 \ncontrast, compositional dissimilarity of regime-shifted reefs remained greater than baseline 105 \nlevels throughout 2005-2014 (mean βspatial = 0.57) (Figure S1B). Such high spatial 106 \nheterogeneity suggests that habitat associations underlie compositional differences on 107 \nregime-shifted reefs, as macroalgal habitat quality can vary substantially among seasons and 108 \nyears [22,23]. 109 \nWe examined how functional groups and species contributed to compositional 110 \ndifferences following bleaching by tracking temporal patterns in functional group richness 111 \n(Figure 2). Corallivore and invertivore group richness recovered non-linearly and, by 2014 112 \nhad reached pre-bleaching levels on recovering reefs but not on regime-shifted reefs (Figure 113 \n2B,D). Herbivore richness increased linearly from 2005-2014 on both reef states though, 114 \nagain, recovering sites consistently supported more species than regime-shifted reefs and 115 \nexceeded pre-bleaching levels by 2008, whereas regime-shifted reefs maintained pre-116 \nbleaching levels of herbivore richness from 2008-2017 (Figure 2C). Changes in richness of 117 \nplanktivore, mixed-diet feeding, and piscivore groups were also strongly differentiated by 118 \nreef regime, with planktivore, mixed-diet feeder, and piscivore groups consistently less 119 \nspeciose than 1994 baselines on regime-shifted reefs, but less impacted on recovering reefs 120 \nwhere pre-bleaching richness levels were matched or exceeded throughout 2005-2017 (Fig. 121 \n2A,E,F). Regime shifts, therefore, led to a greater loss of functional redundancy (i.e. fewer 122 \nspecies with similar functional roles). This implies that on reefs close to human settlements, 123 \nsuch as Seychelles, the vulnerability of fish functional groups to fishing [24,25] will be 124 \nexacerbated by bleaching, with transitions to macroalgal states likely to produce communities 125 \nthat are functionally depauperate relative to historic coral-dominated states [26]. In contrast, 126 \nthe functional capacity of fish communities was restored on reefs that resisted regime shifts, 127 \nwith richness levels of all six dietary groups returning to pre-bleaching baselines on 128 \nrecovering reefs. For piscivores, which returned to pre-bleaching richness most slowly (by 129 \n2014) (Fig. 2F), it is likely that recovery was lagged because these are long-lived species that 130 \ndepend on reef-associated fishes for food [27] and require temporally stable reef habitats 131 \n[28]. 132 \nBy comparing species-level biomass estimates of 1994 (pre-bleaching) and 2014 133 \n(post-bleaching) fish assemblages, we found that compositional dissimilarity between pre- 134 \nand post-bleaching communities was characterized by biomass changes within herbivore, 135 \ninvertivore and mixed-diet functional feeding groups, and reduced biomass of planktivore, 136 \ncorallivore and piscivore species (Figures 3, 4). For positive and negative biomass changes 137 \ncombined, herbivores explained 23.0% and 27.1% of observed βBC at recovering and regime-138 \nshifted reefs, respectively (Figure 3A), and exhibited biomass changes of ±0-163 kg ha-1 139 \n(Figure 4). For herbivore species, the mean biomass difference between 1994 to 2014 was 140 \npositive at both recovering (mean = 7.14, 95% CIs = 0.60, 13.84) and regime-shifted reefs 141 \n(mean = 5.01, 95% CIs = -1.375, 13.248) (Figure 3B), indicating that declines in commonly 142 \nobserved species were outweighed by gains in newly abundant species (Figure 4). Although 143 \nour infrequent visual surveys cannot be used to describe population dynamics, the recovery 144 \nperiod spanned several generations of most species (Table S1) and thus these patterns are 145 \nlikely due to positive population feedbacks in dominant species that benefited from bleaching 146 \n(e.g. herbivores) and slow or failed recovery of species dependent on return of habitat 147 \nstructure (e.g. planktivores, corallivores). For example, increases in herbivore biomass are 148 \noften associated with coral declines [4, 29, 30] and, here, species responses depended on 149 \nhabitat type, where browsing species associated with macroalgal reefs and scraping species 150 \nassociated with recovering reefs [31]. 151 \nThe relative infrequency of our fish surveys, focus on adult fish and use of a fixed 152 \nspecies list mean that we cannot disentangle the relative contributions of recruitment, growth 153 \nand relocation among habitat types and depths, nor account for potential emergence of new 154 \nspecies. However, it is likely that short-term dispersal among reef habitats [12,32], increased 155 \npopulation growth due to greater food availability [33], and high juvenile survivorship [34] 156 \nall contributed to the long-term persistence of these populations. Reef fish populations are 157 \necologically connected over relatively small scales, as larval dispersal distances are typically 158 \n<5 – 15 km and many species are territorial as adults (damselfishes, butterflyfishes) or inhabit 159 \nsmall home ranges (<3 km linear distance) [35]. Additionally, Seychelles reefs are isolated by 160 \ndeep water dispersal barriers for reef fishes and by limited connectivity to distant continental 161 \nreefs (>1,000 km) (Kool & Graham unpublished data), meaning that species larval 162 \nconnectivity or relocation from neighbouring reef systems are unlikely to be responsible for 163 \nthe majority of compositional changes we observe. 164 \nOther functional groups made lower overall contributions to βBC, and also tended to 165 \nhave the greatest degree of species declines. For example, invertivores and mixed-diet 166 \nfeeders had moderately high dissimilarity contributions (9.4 - 13.9%) that were mostly due to 167 \nspecies declines, particularly on regime-shifted reefs (Figures 3A, 4B). Although 168 \ndissimilarity contributions from planktivore and piscivore groups were relatively minor (1.62 169 \n– 4.99%), almost every species declined in biomass and group-level posterior means were 170 \nnegative for both recovering and regime-shifted reefs (Figures 3B, 4). In contrast, corallivore 171 \nbiomass changes were more closely linked to regime state, with biomass increases explaining 172 \nobserved βBC at recovering reefs (relative contribution from species with biomass increases = 173 \n1.23% and from biomass decreases = 0.13%) but biomass declines explaining observed βBC 174 \nat regime-shifted reefs (0.23%, 1.43%). Positive responses of highly coral-associated species, 175 \nwhich could also be driven by recruitment as well as growth and relocation, reduced 176 \ncompositional differences relative to regime-shifted reefs. Such strong effects may be 177 \nbecause corallivore species are highly dependent upon live branching coral for food and 178 \nshelter [36]. 179 \nIn each analysis, we considered how fishing protection influenced recovery and 180 \nchange in fish assemblages after bleaching. By enhancing grazing functions [37] and 181 \nminimizing stressors on coral populations [38], ecosystem protection of coral reefs is 182 \nexpected to accelerate recovery towards pre-bleaching conditions. Such effects, however, 183 \ndepend upon the severity of bleaching, disturbance history, and local anthropogenic stressors. 184 \nFor example, large-scale and well-enforced protection of the Great Barrier Reef has 185 \nshortened recovery times to minor bleaching events by up to ~2.4 years [20] whereas at 186 \nIndian Ocean reefs, where 1998 bleaching was severe and protected areas are small and less 187 \neffectively enforced, both fished and no-take areas experienced declines in fish richness and 188 \nabundance after bleaching [39]. In Seychelles, no-take areas were a poor predictor of 189 \nbleaching responses of benthic communities [7] and, here, did not improve recovery of fish 190 \ncommunity composition after bleaching (Figure 1D,F). Protected reefs did support higher 191 \nspecies richness (Figure 1B) but experienced greater biomass declines (Figure 3C). Although 192 \nrichness patterns were potentially confounded by high abundances that inflated diversity 193 \nestimates in no-take areas, large biomass declines highlight how reef protection, which 194 \npromoted pre-bleaching reef fish biomass [27], did not insure fish communities against 195 \nhabitat collapse. 196 \nIrrespective of benthic recovery trajectory or ecosystem protection, post-bleaching 197 \nassemblages were characterized by a speciose and high biomass herbivore group and low 198 \nrepresentation of planktivore, invertivore, and piscivore species. Such communities may be 199 \nconsidered hybrid states, which contain aspects of pre- and post-bleaching configurations but 200 \nwith altered ecosystem functioning [6]. Concepts of such ‘no-analog’ states have typically 201 \nbeen developed to describe highly-degraded terrestrial ecosystems [6,40], but are equally 202 \nrelevant for coral reefs where species may have unexpected, differential responses to climate 203 \nimpacts [5]. For example, one striking effect of the 1998 bleaching event was to raise 204 \nherbivore productivity and diversity on both regime-shifted and recovering reefs. Herbivore 205 \npopulations are expected to increase with algal productivity following rapid declines in coral 206 \ncover [41] but, in pristine systems, return to baseline levels once benthic habitat has returned 207 \nto a coral-dominated state [42]. However, on fished Seychelles reefs herbivores remained 208 \ndominant >10 years after bleaching, with both richness and biomass exceeding pre-bleaching 209 \nlevels by 2014. Such sustained herbivore productivity may occur due to long-term 210 \navailability of diverse algal resources, but also in response to dampening of natural predation 211 \nlevels when upper trophic levels are overexploited and their recovery from bleaching is slow. 212 \nFurthermore, high herbivore survivorship during the first few years after bleaching may have 213 \nhelped to sustain large herbivore populations over decadal time scales, particularly for long-214 \nlived acanthurid and scarid species [27]. Thus, high herbivore productivity may be 215 \ncharacteristic of bleached reefs that are adjacent to human populations, which may help 216 \nbuffer fisheries from declining predatory fish populations by continuing to provide catches of 217 \nlow trophic level species [31]. 218 \nAs low-latitude ecosystems that operate near their thermal limits [43], coral reefs are 219 \nmore likely to reorganize and suffer local extinctions than receive temperature driven non-220 \nnative species. Thus, the diversity patterns documented here suggest that climate-driven 221 \ncompositional changes on coral reefs will be particularly unique, and driven indirectly by 222 \nchanges in physical habitat structure rather than directly by the effects of changes in 223 \ntemperature on species distributions, for example in sub-tropical coastal [44] and temperate 224 \nshelf [45] ecosystems. The implications for future coral reef ecosystems are stark, given that 225 \ncoral reef fish communities did not return to pre-bleaching diversity levels over a 16-year 226 \nrecovery window (1998-2014) which was bounded by two climate-driven mass coral 227 \nbleaching events (1998, 2016). With over 60% of reefs projected to experience annual 228 \nbleaching-level thermal stress by ~2050 [9] and bleaching recovery windows only ~six years 229 \nby 2016 [8], we conclude that realized and projected increases in the frequency of thermal 230 \nstress events on coral reefs will cause persistent changes in fish diversity and community 231 \nstructure. These changes will alter the functions and ecosystem services historically provided 232 \nby reef fishes, such as grazing rates [26] and the species accessible to fisheries [31]. This 233 \nposes a challenge for management and reef dependent communities to adapt to these alters 234 \nreef fish configurations. 235 \n 236 \n 237 \nEXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES 238 \n 239 \nStudy sites 240 \nSix surveys of 21 Seychelles reef sites were conducted from 1994-2017 (one in each of the 241 \nyears 1994, 2005, 2008, 2011, 2014, 2017). Surveys were spatially stratified to encompass 242 \ncarbonate, patch, and granitic habitat types (n = 7 per habitat) in both fished (12) and 243 \nprotected (9) areas. All 21 sites were surveyed in each year, except 2017, when 18 sites were 244 \nsurveyed. 245 \n 246 \nFish and benthic survey data 247 \nFish and benthic community surveys were conducted at each site in each year using point 248 \ncounts of 7 m radius. Within each point count area, one diver (NAJG or SJ) estimated 249 \nindividual body lengths for any diurnally active reef-associated fish on a list of 129 species 250 \nand estimated to be ≥8 cm total length. Individual sizes (total length to nearest cm) were 251 \nconverted to biomass using published length ~ weight relationships [46]. Each species was 252 \nassigned to one of six functional feeding groups (planktivore, corallivore, herbivore, 253 \ninvertivore, mixed-diet feeder, piscivore) based on published dietary information [46] and 254 \nassigned an estimated average age to first maturity based on life history information [47] 255 \n(Table S1). Following fish surveys, one diver (SW or SJ) performed visual assessments of 256 \nbenthic habitat composition within the point count area. Benthic composition was recorded as 257 \nthe percent cover (%) of macroalgae, sand, rubble and rock substrate, and three 258 \nmorphological types of hard coral (branching, massive, encrusting). Structural complexity 259 \nwas assessed on a 6-point scale ranging from flattened sites with no vertical relief (0) up to 260 \nhighly complex sites characterized by overhangs and caves (5) [48]. These visual cover and 261 \ncomplexity estimates correlate closely with line transect methods and commonly-used 262 \nrugosity metrics [49]. For each survey year, benthic habitat observations were averaged 263 \nacross replicates to give site-level estimates of the percent cover of macroalgae, branching, 264 \nmassive, and encrusting corals, and structural complexity. 265 \n 266 \n Fish and benthic point count surveys were repeated for 8 (2011, 2014, 2017) or 16 267 \n(1994, 2005, 2008) replicates per site. Because species richness estimates depend on the area 268 \nsampled, we ensured that temporal comparisons were valid by only analyzing data from the 269 \nfirst 8 replicates in 1994, 2005 and 2008. By conducting surveys from a fixed species list, at 270 \nsimilar depths, and repeating locations in space and time with equal effort (8 survey 271 \nreplicates per site), we minimize issues of sample incompleteness that might bias diversity 272 \nestimates through a failure to detect rare species [50]. Although we did not census all resident 273 \nfish species, our species list incorporates taxa that are commonly observed on Seychelles 274 \nreefs [51]. 275 \n 276 \nTemporal trends in diversity and community composition 277 \nBleaching-induced shifts in community composition were assessed by examining temporal 278 \nchange in 1) α diversity (i.e. species richness), 2) β diversity (i.e. compositional 279 \ndissimilarity), and 3) species biomass. First, we measured α diversity by tallying the total 280 \nnumber of species observed at each site in each year, for the full community and each 281 \nfunctional group. Temporal richness patterns among functional groups were assessed relative 282 \nto pre-bleaching assemblages where, by estimating the difference between observed richness 283 \nand 1994 richness for each site in 2005-2017, we examined how different functional groups 284 \nresponded to bleaching and subsequent habitat changes, and how those groups contributed to 285 \noverall richness patterns. Although sampling effort was standardized across the time series, 286 \nrichness estimates may have been sensitive to spatial and temporal variation in abundances 287 \n[52]. 288 \n Shifts in community composition were described with β diversity metrics. We 289 \ngenerated species × site matrices with mean biomass (kg ha-1) estimates, that were averaged 290 \nacross replicates at each site in each year (n = 6 community matrices, each with dimensions 291 \n129 species x 21 sites). From these matrices, we calculated β diversity using the Bray-Curtis 292 \nindex (βBC) [53] for pairwise comparisons between sites across survey years (temporal) or 293 \namong sites in each survey year (spatial). βBC is bounded by 0 (perfect similarity) and 1 294 \n(perfect dissimilarity), and quantifies biomass (𝑥𝑥) differences of each species i at site k 295 \nbetween years a and b, relative to overall biomass: 296 \n𝛽𝛽𝐵𝐵𝐵𝐵 =\n∑ (𝑥𝑥𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖−𝑥𝑥𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖)𝑖𝑖\n∑ (𝑥𝑥𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖+𝑥𝑥𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖)𝑖𝑖\n (1) 297 \n We used βBC to quantify temporal shifts in community composition in three ways. 298 \nFirst, we quantified compositional turnover relative to pre-bleaching compositions by 299 \nexpressing βBC as the site-level difference in species biomass between each survey year and 300 \n1994, thus giving estimates for each of the 21 sites (k) in each post-bleaching survey year 301 \n(e.g. βk,2014 = pairwise comparison between k2014 and k1994). This approach, hereafter β1994, 302 \nenabled us to measure the dissimilarity between pre and post-bleaching communities, and to 303 \nassess the degree of recovery to pre-bleaching community compositions. Second, we 304 \nquantified turnover between survey years by expressing βBC as the site-level difference in 305 \nspecies biomass between successive survey years, thus giving estimates at 21 sites for five 306 \ntemporal comparisons (e.g. βk,2014 = pairwise comparison between k2014 and k2011). This 307 \napproach, hereafter βseq, was used to assess changes in composition between survey years. 308 \nThird, we quantified temporal trends in spatial β diversity by estimating βBC for pairwise site 309 \ncombinations among either recovering or regime-shifted reefs, separately for each survey 310 \nyear (e.g. β1,2_2014 = pairwise comparison between recovering sites 1 and 2 in 2014). Site-level 311 \ndissimilarity values were the mean βBC across all pairwise site comparisons in each year. This 312 \napproach, hereafter βspatial, enabled us to assess changes in spatial similarity of community 313 \ncompositions through time, separately for recovering and shifted reefs. Null and alternative β 314 \nmetrics indicated that temporal β diversity patterns were robust to potential sampling biases 315 \nand richness gradients (Supplementary Methods). 316 \n To complement our assessment of temporal β diversity trends, we sought to identify 317 \nwhich functional groups and species accounted for compositional differences between pre- 318 \nand post-bleaching assemblages. We defined pre- and post-bleaching assemblages as those 319 \nsurveyed in 1994 and 2014, respectively, thereby incorporating potential reorganization of 320 \nfish communities following the 1998 bleaching event while excluding any additional impacts 321 \nof the 2016 bleaching event. For βBC values generated by pairwise comparison of sites in 322 \n1994 and 2014, we measured each species’ contribution to compositional dissimilarity with a 323 \nSIMilarity PERcentage analysis (SIMPER) [54]. The percentage contribution to βBC was the 324 \nabsolute difference in biomass between 1994 and 2014 for each species i at each site k, xik,1994 325 \n– xik,2014. Compositional shifts were reported by summing SIMPER percentages across 326 \nfunctional groups, grouping species by the direction of biomass change (increased vs. 327 \ndecreased), for either recovering or regime-shifted reefs. Finally, we characterized species-328 \nlevel biomass changes by calculating the difference in biomass of each species before 329 \nbleaching (1994) and after recovery (2014) in each reef regime. 330 \n 331 \nStatistical modelling 332 \nWe utilized Bayesian hierarchical models to compare temporal changes in fish community 333 \ncomposition between recovering and regime-shifted reefs. For diversity metrics, models were 334 \nfitted to predict variation in overall species richness (Model 1, M1), functional group richness 335 \n(M2 fitted separately to 6 functional groups), β1994 (M3), and βseq (M4) (Tables S2, S3). After 336 \nidentifying appropriate temporal structures, we hypothesized that site-specific benthic 337 \nrecovery rates and fishery access would predict temporal change in overall fish community 338 \ndiversity. Diversity models (α, β metrics) were fitted with management status (no-take or 339 \nfished), branching coral, encrusting coral, massive coral and macroalgae (% cover), and 340 \nstructural complexity as explanatory covariates. For species-level biomass differences 341 \nbetween 1994 and 2014, we modelled the same fixed effects used in diversity models, and as 342 \nwell as mean observed species size (cm; fixed term) and functional feeding group (random 343 \nterms for recovering and regime-shifted reefs) (M5). Model fitting procedures are described 344 \nin full in the Supplementary Methods. Temporal trends in βspatial, which were pairwise site 345 \ncomparisons and thus not independent samples, were visualized but not modelled. 346 \nAll analyses were conducted in R [55]. β metrics were estimated using beta.temp function in 347 \nbetapart [56], SIMPER analyses were run in vegan [57], and Bayesian hierarchical models 348 \nwere implemented in Stan using rethinking [58] following [59]. We archive our R code and 349 \nmodel outputs at an open source repository (github.com/jpwrobinson/beta-bleaching). 350 \n 351 \nAcknowledgements 352 \nThis research was supported by the Royal Society (CH160077, UF140691). We thank 353 \nSeychelles Fishing Authority, Nature Seychelles, and Global Vision International for field 354 \nassistance, and Cameron Freshwater for statistical advice. 355 \n 356 \nAuthor contributions 357 \nNAJG conceived the study. NAJG, SW and SJ conducted ecological surveys. JPWR 358 \nperformed all statistical analyses and led the manuscript writing, with substantial input from 359 \nSW, SJ and NAJG. 360 \nFigure captions 361 \nFigure 1. Temporal change in fish assemblage diversity from 2005-2017. (A) Species 362 \nrichness with (B) posterior parameter estimates, (C) β1994 with (D) posterior parameter 363 \nestimates, and (E) βseq with (F) posterior parameter estimates. Temporal trend lines (a,c,e) 364 \nwere generated from mean posterior predictions through time, conditioned on mean post-365 \nbleaching benthic cover and complexity for recovering (blue solid line) and regime-shifted 366 \n(red dashed line) sites, with 95% highest posterior density intervals. Observed site-level 367 \nvalues are displayed as points jittered for each survey year, and baseline 1994 species 368 \nrichness included as boxplots in (a). Posterior parameter estimates indicate mean (point), 369 \n95% (thick line) and 50% (thin line) credible intervals generated from a posterior distribution 370 \nof 1,000 samples for each explanatory covariate. (G-H) Temporal change in cover of hard 371 \ncoral (blue) and macroalgae (red) on recovering (G) and regime-shifted (H) reefs, with points 372 \nindicating site-level mean percent cover (± 2 standard errors). See also Figure S1 which 373 \nshows βspatial temporal trends, Figure S2 which shows incidence-based and null model-based 374 \nβ estimates, and Tables S2 and S3 which show Bayesian model structures and priors. 375 \n 376 \n 377 \nFigure 2. Change in species richness in each year relative to 1994 for functional feeding 378 \ngroups. Panels show (A) planktivores, (B) corallivores, (C) herbivores, (D) invertivores, (E) 379 \nmixed-diet feeders, and (F) piscivores. Lines are mean posterior predictions over time 380 \ngenerated from top-ranked linear or non-linear temporal models for recovering (solid) and 381 \nregime-shifted reefs (dashed), shaded with 95% highest posterior density intervals and 382 \noverlaid with mean observed richness change (± 2 standard errors). See also Table S1 which 383 \nshows species in functional feeding groups and average age to maturity, and Tables S2-S4 384 \nwhich show Bayesian model structures, priors, and model selection on temporal structures. 385 \n 386 \n 387 \nFigure 3. Biomass composition of fish assemblages on recovering and regime-shifted 388 \nreefs. (A) SIMPER analysis of species contributions to βBC dissimilarity between 1994 and 389 \n2014 for recovering (blue) and regime-shifted (red) sites. Bars are species-level contributions 390 \nsummed by functional feeding group for species that increased (positive y values, solid color) 391 \nor decreased (negative y, shaded color) in biomass from 1994 to 2014, where overall βBC was 392 \n0.53 at recovering sites and 0.60 at regime-shifted sites. White lines indicate net change in 393 \nbiomass. (B-C) Bayesian model predictions of species-level biomass change from 1994 to 394 \n2014 showing (B) the predicted change in biomass by functional group and (C) effect of 395 \nfixed explanatory covariates on biomass change. Posterior parameter estimates indicate mean 396 \n(point), 95% (thick line) and 50% (thin line) credible intervals, of 1,000 samples of the 397 \nposterior distribution for each functional feeding group on recovering (red) and regime-398 \nshifted reefs (blue), and each fixed explanatory covariate (black). See also Tables S2 and S3 399 \nfor Bayesian model structures and priors. 400 \n 401 \n Figure 4. Change in species biomass between pre- and post-bleaching fish assemblages 402 \non recovering and regime-shifted reefs. Bars are the mean biomass difference (kg ha-1) 403 \nfrom 1994 to 2014 for each species, on a log10 scale and colored by functional feeding group. 404 \nBars directed right indicate that biomass increased from 1994 to 2014 and bars directed left 405 \nindicate that biomass decreased from 1994 to 2014. (A) Recovering reef species are ordered 406 \nby biomass change, descending from the largest increase (bars directed right) to the largest 407 \ndecrease (bars directed left). (B) Regime-shifted reef species, ordered to correspond with 408 \nspecies order on recovering reefs. Missing bars indicate species that were not observed in a 409 \ngiven year and reef state. Across all reefs, three species were not observed in either 1994 or 410 \n2014 (Diagramma pictum, Epinephelus tukula, Lethrinus rubrioperculatus). See also Figures 411 \nS3 and S4 for species names and relative biomass changes on each reef habitat type. 412 \n 413 \nREFERENCES 414 \n1. Scheffer, M., Carpenter, S., Foley, J.A., Folke, C. & Walker, B. (2001). Catastrophic shifts 415 \nin ecosystems. Nature, 413, 591–596. 416 \n 417 \n2. Dornelas, M., Gotelli, N.J., McGill, B., Shimadzu, H., Moyes, F., Sievers, C., et al. (2014). 418 \nAssemblage time series reveal biodiversity change but not systematic loss. Science, 344, 419 \n296–299. 420 \n 421 \n3. Pecl, G.T., Araújo, M.B., Bell, J.D., Blanchard, J., Bonebrake, T.C., Chen, I.-C., et al. 422 \n(2017). Biodiversity redistribution under climate change: Impacts on ecosystems and human 423 \nwell-being. Science, 355, eaai9214. 424 \n 425 \n4. Wilson, S.K., Graham, N.A.J., Pratchett, M.S., Jones, G.P. & Polunin, N.V.C. (2006). 426 \nMultiple disturbances and the global degradation of coral reefs: are reef fishes at risk or 427 \nresilient? Glob. Chang. Biol., 12, 2220–2234. 428 \n 429 \n5. Graham, N.A.J., Cinner, J.E., Norström, A.V., and Nyström, M. (2014). Coral reefs as 430 \nnovel ecosystems: embracing new futures. Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability 431 \n7, 9–14. 432 \n 433 \n6. Hobbs, R.J., Higgs, E. & Harris, J.A. (2009). Novel ecosystems: implications for 434 \nconservation and restoration. Trends Ecol. Evol., 24, 599–605. 435 \n 436 \n7. Graham, N.A.J., Jennings, S., MacNeil, M.A., Mouillot, D. & Wilson, S.K. (2015). 437 \nPredicting climate-driven regime shifts versus rebound potential in coral reefs. Nature, 518, 438 \n94–97. 439 \n 440 \n8. Hughes, T.P., Anderson, K.D., Connolly, S.R., Heron, S.F., Kerry, J.T., Lough, J.M., et al. 441 \n(2018). Spatial and temporal patterns of mass bleaching of corals in the Anthropocene. 442 \nScience, 359, 80–83. 443 \n 444 \n9. van Hooidonk, R., Maynard, J.A. & Planes, S. (2013). Temporary refugia for coral reefs in 445 \na warming world. Nat. Clim. Chang., 3, 508. 446 \n 447 \n10. Messmer V, Jones GP, Munday PL, Holbrook SJ, Schmitt RJ, Brooks AJ (2011) Habitat 448 \nbiodiversity as a determinant of fish community structure on coral reefs. Ecology, 92, 2285–449 \n2298. 450 \n 451 \n11. Halford, A.R. & Caley, M.J. (2009). Towards an understanding of resilience in isolated 452 \ncoral reefs. Glob. Chang. Biol., 15, 3031–3045. 453 \n 454 \n12. Richardson, L.E., Graham, N.A.J., Pratchett, M.S., Eurich, J.G. & Hoey, A.S. (2018). 455 \nMass coral bleaching causes biotic homogenization of reef fish assemblages. Glob. Chang. 456 \nBiol., 99, 1134. 457 \n 458 \n13. Graham, N.A.J., Wilson, S.K., Jennings, S., Polunin, N.V.C., Bijoux, J.P. & Robinson, J. 459 \n(2006). Dynamic fragility of oceanic coral reef ecosystems. Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci.