an-scale 556 \nintegrity of coral reef ecosystems. PLoS One, 3, e3039. 557 \n 558 \n40. Williams, J.W. & Jackson, S.T. (2007). Novel climates, no-analog communities, and 559 \necological surprises. Front. Ecol. Environ., 5, 475–482. 560 \n 561 \n41. Rogers, A., Blanchard, J.L. & Mumby, P.J. (2017). Fisheries productivity under 562 \nprogressive coral reef degradation. J. Appl. Ecol., 55, 1041–1049. 563 \n 564 \n42. Gilmour, J.P., Smith, L.D., Heyward, A.J., Baird, A.H. & Pratchett, M.S. (2013). 565 \nRecovery of an isolated coral reef system following severe disturbance. Science, 340, 69–71. 566 \n 567 \n43. Rummer, J.L., Couturier, C.S., Stecyk, J.A.W., Gardiner, N.M., Kinch, J.P., Nilsson, 568 \nG.E., et al. (2014). Life on the edge: thermal optima for aerobic scope of equatorial reef 569 \nfishes are close to current day temperatures. Glob. Chang. Biol., 20, 1055–1066. 570 \n 571 \n44. Wernberg, T., Bennett, S., Babcock, R.C., de Bettignies, T., Cure, K., Depczynski, M., et 572 \nal. (2016). Climate-driven regime shift of a temperate marine ecosystem. Science, 353, 169–573 \n172. 574 \n 575 \n45. Fossheim, M., Primicerio, R., Johannesen, E., Ingvaldsen, R.B., Aschan, M.M. & 576 \nDolgov, A.V. (2015). Recent warming leads to a rapid borealization of fish communities in 577 \nthe Arctic. Nat. Clim. Chang., 5, 673–677. 578 \n 579 \n46. Froese, R. & Pauly, D. (2018). FishBase (www.fishbase.org). 580 \n 581 \n47. Thorson, J.T., Munch, S.B., Cope, J.M. & Gao, J. (2017). Predicting life history 582 \nparameters for all fishes worldwide. Ecol. Appl., 27, 2262–2276. 583 \n 584 \n48. Polunin, N.V.C. & Roberts, C.M. (1993). Greater biomass and value of target coral-reef 585 \nfishes in two small Caribbean marine reserves. Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser., 100, 167–167. 586 \n 587 \n49. Wilson, S.K., Graham, N.A.J. & Polunin, N.V.C. (2007). Appraisal of visual assessments 588 \nof habitat complexity and benthic composition on coral reefs. Mar. Biol., 151, 1069–1076. 589 \n 590 \n50. Ch